window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("scoping", {"topics":[{"key":"scoping/customer-discovery","sources":["Rob Fitzpatrick, 2013 — The Mom Test","Steve Blank, 2005 — The Four Steps to the Epiphany"],"why":"- **Requests arrive solution-shaped.** An executive saw a demo and wants \"an agent for procurement\" or\n  \"GPT over our documents\". That one sentence compresses a pain someone feels, a guess about what would\n  relieve it, and a political mandate.\n- **Building the literal request is the classic failure.** It is fast and feels responsive, and it is the\n  most common way an AI project ends up as a polished demo nobody uses.\n- **The naive shortcuts fail predictably.**\n  - *Taking the request at face value* inherits the requester's guess, made without knowing what the\n    technology is good at.\n  - *Asking \"would you use a tool that does X?\"* produces polite yeses, because hypothetical questions\n    about the future cost nothing to answer.\n  - *Prototyping first and \"seeing what they think\"* anchors everyone on your first design and skips the\n    questions that decide success: how often the task happens, what an error costs, who must approve a\n    change, and whether the data is even reachable.\n- **AI raises the stakes because it is probabilistic.** A conventional feature works or it does not; an AI\n  feature works on some fraction of cases. Whether 85% is a triumph or a liability depends on facts only\n  discovery surfaces: the cost of a wrong answer, whether a human reviews the output, and how accurate the\n  current manual process really is.","how":"### 1. Map the people before the problem\nEvery enterprise problem has several roles, and they are rarely the same person:\n\n| Role | What they control or care about |\n|---|---|\n| **Economic buyer** | Controls the budget and decides whether this continues past a pilot |\n| **Champion** | Wants it to succeed and will spend political capital; often the original requester |\n| **End users** | Do the work today; their adoption decides whether value is realised |\n| **Data and system owners** | Can grant or block access to the sources you need |\n| **Risk functions** | Security, legal, compliance, works councils; veto power late if ignored early |\n\n- **Map these first because each sees a different problem.** The VP sees headcount cost, the user sees a\n  tedious lookup, compliance sees audit exposure.\n- **A solution that satisfies only one of them stalls.**\n\n### 2. Interview about past behaviour, not future intentions\n- **Ask about specific recent events.** The core technique, popularised by *The Mom Test*: \"Walk me\n  through the last claim you processed. What did you open first? Where did you get stuck?\"\n- **Past behaviour is evidence; opinions about hypothetical products are not.**\n- **Useful follow-ups:** \"How often does that happen?\", \"What did you do about it?\", \"What have you tried\n  before?\", \"What happens if you get it wrong?\".\n- **No past attempts means low pain.** If someone has never tried to solve the problem, it is probably not\n  painful enough to fund.\n\n### 3. Quantify the pain\nTurn anecdotes into a rough annual cost:\n\n```\ncost ≈ volume × (time per item × loaded hourly cost  +  error rate × cost per error)  +  cost of delay\n```\n\n| Term | Who gives it to you |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | The system admin or a report |\n| Time per item | Users |\n| Error rate and cost per error | The team lead or QA |\n| Cost of delay | The buyer |\n\n- **You need this because it bounds the value of any solution** and feeds the ROI model; a problem worth\n  200 hours a year does not justify a six-month integration.\n- **Note which term dominates.** When errors cost more than time, the design must optimise for accuracy and\n  review, not speed.\n\n### 4. Separate the problem from the proposed solution\n- **Restate each request as the outcome it serves.** \"A chatbot over policy documents\" might really be\n  \"adjusters need the applicable coverage clause, with a citation, inside the claims screen\".\n- **Ask \"why\" two or three times.** That usually moves from the solution to the job.\n- **Keep the requester's idea as one hypothesis, not the specification.**\n\n### 5. Collect artifacts, not descriptions\n- **Ask for real samples:** 30 to 50 actual tickets, documents, emails or records, including the ugly ones.\n- **A description of the data is always cleaner than the data.**\n- **Samples do double duty:** they become the seed of the golden dataset and reveal access problems\n  immediately.\n\n### 6. Find the decision process and success evidence\n- **Ask:** \"If this worked, how would you know? Who decides whether it goes to production, and what would\n  they need to see?\"\n- **This produces the success criteria and baseline before you build**, because criteria defined after a\n  demo are negotiated to fit what was built.\n\n### 7. Synthesise and play it back\n- **Write a one-page problem statement:** who, what job, current cost, constraints, success evidence, open\n  risks, and what you deliberately are not solving.\n- **Read it back to the champion and users.** Disagreement here is cheap; disagreement after a build is\n  expensive.\n\n### Interview hygiene and when to stop\n- **Interview users without their manager in the room**, because people describe the official process in\n  front of the person who owns it.\n- **Have a second person take notes** so the interviewer can follow up instead of writing.\n- **Talk far less than the interviewee and never pitch mid-interview.** The moment you describe your idea,\n  answers start rating it rather than describing work.\n- **Look for disconfirming evidence.** Ask \"when is this *not* a problem?\" and \"who handles this without\n  trouble?\".\n- **Stop at saturation, not before full coverage.** Stop interviewing a role when two or three consecutive\n  interviews add no new pain, workaround or exception, and not before you have covered every role on the\n  map.\n\n### Commitment is the real signal\n- **Enthusiasm is free.**\n- **Committed customers give things up:** time with real users, sample data, a named sponsor and an access\n  request in flight. Those who only say \"this is great\" are not yet committed.","example":"### Cost-of-pain calculator\nThe formula from step 3 as code, so each interviewee's number goes into one place and the dominant term\nis obvious. All inputs are illustrative.\n\n```python\ndef annual_pain(volume_per_year, minutes_per_item, hourly_cost,\n                error_rate, cost_per_error, cost_of_delay=0.0):\n    time_cost = volume_per_year * (minutes_per_item / 60) * hourly_cost\n    error_cost = volume_per_year * error_rate * cost_per_error\n    total = time_cost + error_cost + cost_of_delay\n    return {\"time\": round(time_cost), \"errors\": round(error_cost),\n            \"delay\": round(cost_of_delay), \"total\": round(total),\n            \"dominant\": max((\"time\", time_cost), (\"errors\", error_cost),\n                            (\"delay\", cost_of_delay), key=lambda t: t[1])[0]}\n\n# Lookup step only: 120 claims a day, ~250 working days, 15 minutes each\nprint(annual_pain(volume_per_year=120 * 250, minutes_per_item=15, hourly_cost=60,\n                  error_rate=0.02, cost_per_error=1500))\n# {'time': 450000, 'errors': 900000, 'delay': 0, 'total': 1350000, 'dominant': 'errors'}\n# Errors dominate, so the design must optimise for accuracy and review, not speed.\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: \"ChatGPT over our policy wordings\"\nAll figures illustrative. An insurer's VP of Claims asks for \"a ChatGPT-style assistant over our policy\nwordings\". The FDE runs six 45-minute interviews: the VP, a claims team lead, three adjusters and the\nclaims-system admin.\n\n| Finding | Source | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| A typical claim takes about 40 minutes of hands-on work | Adjusters walking through their last three claims | Repeated across all three |\n| Roughly 15 of those minutes go to finding which clauses apply | Adjusters | Wordings live in a store organised by product version; adjusters match the policy's issue date to the right version |\n| About 120 claims a day, so lookup is 120 × 15 = 1,800 minutes, or 30 person-hours, daily | Team lead | Volume report |\n| Wrong-version lookups are a known cause of payment leakage | Team lead | Supervisors spot-check 10% of claims |\n| Nobody asked for a chat interface | Adjusters | They live in the claims screen and do not want another tab |\n| Policy issue date and product code are available | Claims-system admin | Exposed through the claims system's API |\n\n- **Errors matter more than time** here, which shifts the design toward accuracy and citations.\n- **The played-back problem statement:** \"Given an open claim, surface the applicable clauses from the\n  correct wording version, with citations, inside the claims screen. Success: on 50 historical claims\n  labelled by a senior adjuster, the correct clause appears in the top three for at least the share a\n  senior adjuster agrees is acceptable, and median lookup time drops.\"\n- **The reframe:** a retrieval-and-citation problem with a hard metadata filter (version by date), not a\n  chatbot. It now has a baseline, a metric and an integration point.","patterns":["**Standard interview guide per role** — when running more than a handful of interviews, keep one guide per role on the stakeholder map: past-event prompts, the four follow-ups, and the error-cost question. Store notes in one shared template so findings are comparable. Monitor how many guides end with a concrete frequency and a concrete workaround.","**Saturation tracker** — after each interview, log new pains, workarounds and exceptions per role in a simple table. Stop a role after two or three consecutive interviews add nothing new. Monitor coverage: every role on the map has at least one row.","**Commitment checklist as a gate** — before build starts, require sample data received, user time booked, a named sponsor and an access request filed. Track days since each was requested; escalate to the sponsor when one stalls rather than building on synthetic data.","**Signed one-page problem statement** — version the statement in the shared engagement folder, read it back to the champion and users, and get the buyer's written sign-off for multi-quarter work. Monitor open risks and \"not solving\" items for drift when scope changes.","**Samples become the eval seed** — check the 30 to 50 collected records into the evaluation set with labels from a senior user. Watch the share of \"ugly\" records (scans, inconsistent formats), because a prototype tested only on clean files hides the real failure rate."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Solution-shaped request\"] --> M[\"Map stakeholders\"]\n    M --> I[\"Interview on past events\"]\n    I --> Q[\"Quantify cost of pain\"]\n    I --> S[\"Collect 30 to 50 samples\"]\n    Q --> P[\"Restate as outcome\"]\n    S --> P\n    P --> D[\"Find decision process\"]\n    D --> W[\"One-page problem statement\"]\n    W --> B{\"Playback agreed?\"}\n    B -->|\"no\"| I\n    B -->|\"yes\"| C{\"Real commitment?\"}\n    C -->|\"no\"| E[\"Escalate sponsor and access\"]\n    C -->|\"yes\"| G[\"Ready to scope build\"]","caption":"Discovery runs from the stakeholder map to a played-back problem statement; a failed playback or missing\ncommitment loops back instead of moving to build."},"interview":[{"q":"Why do you interview about past behaviour instead of asking users what they want?","a":"- **Past events are evidence.** \"Walk me through the last claim\" reveals real steps, frequency and\n  workarounds that the person actually did.\n- **Hypotheticals are cheap to answer.** \"Would you use X?\" produces polite yeses that predict nothing, so\n  pilots built on them see near-zero usage.\n- **Feature wish-lists describe solutions, not problems**, and anchor the conversation on one design."},{"q":"A VP gives you a clear spec for a chatbot and says users have already agreed. What do you do?","a":"- **Treat the spec as one hypothesis.** Restate it as the outcome it serves and ask \"why\" two or three\n  times to reach the job.\n- **Interview end users without the VP present**, because people describe the official process in front of\n  its owner.\n- **Play back a one-page statement to the VP first**, so the reframe strengthens the champion instead of\n  bypassing them."},{"q":"When would you cut discovery short?","a":"- **When the problem is obviously large or small**, an order-of-magnitude cost estimate is enough to decide\n  and further precision buys nothing.\n- **When the sponsor needs momentum**, run a narrow prototype in parallel, but keep the interviews; skipping\n  them trades a few days for the risk of building the wrong thing.\n- **Never skip the decision process question**, because a pilot nobody has criteria for stalls at the end."},{"q":"How would you size the problem for an ROI case, and which number usually surprises people?","a":"- **Use the cost formula:** volume × (time per item × loaded hourly cost + error rate × cost per error) +\n  cost of delay, with each term from a different person.\n- **Error cost often dominates.** In the insurer case, 30 person-hours a day of lookup mattered less than\n  payment leakage from wrong-version clauses.\n- **That changes the design**, toward accuracy, citations and review rather than raw speed."},{"q":"What signals tell you a customer is actually committed?","a":"- **They give up something scarce:** time with real users, 30 to 50 real samples, a named sponsor and an\n  access request already filed.\n- **Enthusiasm alone is not commitment.** Three weeks of positive meetings without data is a risk to\n  escalate, not a green light."}],"tradeoffs":["**Depth vs momentum.** Deeper discovery lowers the risk of building the wrong thing, but delays visible progress. When the sponsor needs momentum, run a narrow prototype in parallel rather than skipping interviews.","**Users vs politics.** Interviewing end users surfaces the real job, but can bypass the champion politically. When the organisation is hierarchical, get the champion to introduce you and share findings with them first.","**Precision vs time.** Quantifying pain precisely makes the ROI case defensible, but costs time. When the problem is obviously large or small, an order-of-magnitude estimate is enough to decide.","**Alignment vs discomfort.** Playing back a written problem statement forces alignment, but exposes disagreements early. That discomfort is the point, unless stakeholders are already aligned, when a short confirmation suffices."],"pitfalls":["**Asking hypothetical questions like \"would you use this?\".** Symptom: unanimous enthusiasm in interviews followed by near-zero usage in the pilot. Fix: ask about the last specific occurrence.","**Interviewing only the requester and their managers.** Symptom: a solution that fits the org chart but not the screen users actually work in, and users route around it. Fix: interview end users directly.","**Accepting a description of the data instead of samples.** Symptom: a prototype that works on clean demo files and collapses on the first real batch of scanned or inconsistent records. Fix: get real samples.","**Skipping the decision process.** Symptom: a successful pilot that stalls because security, legal or the budget holder were never asked what they needed to see. Fix: ask who decides, early.","**Asking leading questions or pitching mid-interview.** Symptom: notes full of quotes that agree with the original idea and almost no concrete descriptions of past events, frequencies or workarounds.","**Letting the first solution idea become the spec.** Symptom: every later conversation is about features of the chatbot rather than whether claims get processed faster or more accurately."],"when":"- **Use at the start of every engagement**, and again whenever scope changes materially, such as a new\n  department or a new data source.\n- **Keep it proportional.** A two-week pilot might need five interviews and one sample dataset; a\n  multi-quarter programme needs a stakeholder map and a written problem statement signed off by the buyer.\n- **Measure:** can you state the job, the current cost, the cost of an error, the success evidence and the\n  decision-maker in a page? If you cannot, you are not ready to build.\n- **Interviews:** for FDE and architect roles, expect a scenario where a customer asks for a specific AI\n  solution; the strong answer reframes it into the underlying problem before proposing anything.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Joins user interviews to see real inputs and edge cases, and turns collected samples into the first evaluation set before writing pipeline code.","architect":"Owns the problem statement and constraints that shape the design, and refuses to commit to an architecture until error cost and integration point are known.","fde":"Runs the interviews on site, maps stakeholders and politics, and converts a solution-shaped executive request into a measurable problem the customer agrees to."},"check":{"q":"A customer's COO asks you to \"build an agent that automates our procurement approvals\" and gives you\ntwo weeks for discovery. What do you do first, and what finding would make you push back on building an\nagent at all?","a":"First map the stakeholders: the COO as buyer, the procurement lead as likely champion, approvers and\nrequesters as users, the ERP owner for data, and finance controls and audit for risk. Then interview\napprovers about their last several approvals: what they checked, where they waited, how often they\nrejected and why. Quantify volume, time per approval and the cost of a wrong approval. I would push back\non an agent if the interviews show most delay is waiting in an approver's queue rather than judgement\nwork, or if approvals are mostly deterministic policy checks (thresholds, approved vendors), because then\nrouting rules or a policy engine fix it more cheaply and auditably, and the AI role shrinks to\nsummarising the exceptions."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which interview question produces the most reliable evidence about a user's pain?","options":["\"Would you use a tool that summarised these documents automatically?\"","\"Walk me through the last time you had to find this information. What did you do?\"","\"On a scale of 1 to 10, how painful is document search for you?\"","\"What features would your ideal AI assistant have?\""],"answer":1,"explanation":"Questions about specific past events reveal actual behaviour, frequency and workarounds. Hypothetical and rating questions invite polite, cheap answers, and feature wish-lists describe solutions, not problems."},{"q":"Why does discovery for an AI system need to establish the cost of a wrong output, more than for a conventional feature?","options":["Because AI systems are more expensive to host than conventional software","Because regulators require error costs for every software project","Because AI output is correct only some of the time, so whether a given accuracy is acceptable depends on error cost and review","Because error cost determines which model provider to choose"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A probabilistic system succeeds on a fraction of cases. The same accuracy can be excellent or unacceptable depending on what a mistake costs and whether a human checks, so discovery must surface both."},{"q":"A customer is enthusiastic in every meeting but has not provided sample data or user access after three weeks. What is the best reading?","options":["The project is low-risk because stakeholder sentiment is strongly positive","The team should build with synthetic data so as not to lose momentum","Commitment is not yet real; escalate the access request and the sponsor's role before building","The data is probably clean, so it can be integrated later without risk"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Enthusiasm costs nothing; giving time, data and access does. Missing samples also hide the data problems that most often sink AI projects, so building on synthetic data defers the real risk."}]},{"key":"scoping/ai-use-case-discovery","sources":[],"why":"- **Ideas outrun evaluation.** Once an organisation decides to \"do AI\", every team has a list, vendors\n  pitch their own, and the loudest executive's idea tends to win by default.\n- **Without a discipline, three failure patterns repeat:**\n  - *AI for a deterministic problem.* A task that is really \"apply these five rules\" or \"join these two\n    tables\" gets an LLM, which is slower, costlier and less reliable than twenty lines of code or a report.\n  - *High value, impossible first step.* The most valuable idea (say, autonomous underwriting decisions)\n    has the highest error cost, the strictest regulation and the least labelled data, so it spends a year\n    in review and never ships.\n  - *Feasible but worthless.* A team builds a neat summariser for a document nobody reads.\n- **Ranking by value or enthusiasm fails because value is one of four independent axes.** A good *first*\n  candidate is valuable, genuinely needs pattern-matching over unstructured input, has data and an\n  integration point reachable now, and has occasional errors bounded by review or reversibility.\n- **Discovery checks all four before committing engineers.**","how":"### 1. Build the candidate inventory from work, not wishes\n- **Collect from real work:** walk workflows, read ticket queues, ask teams where time goes (see customer\n  discovery and workflow analysis).\n- **Write one line per candidate:** *\"[role] spends [time] doing [task] on [input] to produce [output]\"*.\n- **The format forces an input and an output**, because a use case without both cannot be built or\n  evaluated.\n\n### 2. Gate on AI fit\n| Signs it needs a model | Signs it does not |\n|---|---|\n| Inputs are unstructured (free text, documents, images, speech) | The logic is a stable set of rules |\n| The input-to-output mapping is patterned but too varied for hand-written rules | The answer is a database lookup |\n| There is tolerance for imperfect output | Every output must be exactly correct with no review |\n\n- **Test for verification asymmetry.** Tasks where checking an answer is much cheaper than producing it\n  (drafting a reply a human approves, extracting fields a human confirms) are strong fits.\n- **Because** the human review turns a probabilistic component into a reliable system at low cost.\n\n### 3. Estimate value\n- **Formula:** volume × time per item × loaded cost, plus error and delay costs, and any revenue effect.\n- **Keep it order-of-magnitude here;** the ROI model refines the winners later.\n- **Note whether value needs full automation or only assistance**, because assisted value (say, half the\n  time saved) is reachable far sooner than full automation.\n\n### 3a. Ask what accuracy the value requires\nValue depends on accuracy, and differently for reviewed and unreviewed use cases. That difference is\nwhat verification asymmetry means in numbers.\n\n**Reviewed (assist).**\n- *w* = minutes to do the item by hand; *e* = minutes to check and edit a good draft; *r* = minutes wasted\n  reading a bad draft before doing it by hand anyway; *p* = draft quality.\n- Expected time is `p·e + (1−p)·(r + w)`, so the assistant breaks even at `p* = r / (w − e + r)`.\n- With w = 12, e = 3, r = 1 (illustrative), p* ≈ 10%.\n- At p = 70%, expected time is 0.7·3 + 0.3·13 = 6 minutes, half the manual time. Mediocre accuracy still\n  pays.\n\n**Unreviewed (automate).**\n- Each error lands as its full cost *C*, so the break-even error rate is `value saved per item ÷ C`.\n- Saving about $8 of handling time per item against a $200 average cost per wrong decision allows at most\n  a 4% error rate. So 96% accuracy only breaks even, before any regulatory or reputational cost.\n\n**The consequence.**\n- The same model can be a clear win as an assistant and a loss as an automaton.\n- Both calculations assume reviewers really check. As drafts improve, reviewers start approving without\n  reading (automation bias), so measure how many errors the review step actually catches.\n\n### 4. Assess feasibility\n- **Data:** can you get representative data this month?\n- **Integration point:** is there somewhere output can land (an API, a queue, a UI people already use)?\n- **Ground truth:** are there examples of correct outputs to evaluate against?\n- **Economics:** is latency or cost per item compatible with the volume?\n- **Blocked means not first.** A candidate blocked on a six-month data-access approval is not a first use\n  case regardless of value.\n- **Run a one- or two-day probe per shortlisted candidate:** a plain prompt against 20 to 50 real items,\n  scored by someone who does the work. It will not give final accuracy, but it separates \"plausibly\n  reachable\" from \"the model cannot read these documents at all\" before anyone writes a business case.\n\n### 5. Classify risk\n- **Consider:** consequence of error, who is affected (internal staff vs customers vs the public),\n  reversibility, regulatory exposure and data sensitivity.\n- **Map onto the organisation's AI risk classification.** High-risk use cases need governance work that\n  should run in parallel, not be discovered at launch.\n\n### 6. Rank and pick a portfolio\n- **Plot survivors on value vs feasibility,** with risk as a gate or a penalty.\n- **Pick a first use case that is** frequent, measurable against a known baseline, human-reviewed, and\n  reachable with current data. It buys trust and builds reusable plumbing (connectors, evaluation harness,\n  access patterns).\n- **Keep one larger strategic bet in discovery alongside it,** so the programme is not only quick wins.\n\n### 7. Choose the technique last\n- **Only after a candidate survives** do you decide prompt, RAG, fine-tuning, a workflow or an agent.\n- **Technique-first (\"we want an agent\")** is how deterministic problems end up with LLMs in them.","example":"### Screening pass in code\nA minimal screening pass. Gates remove candidates that do not need a model or cannot start now; the rest\nare scored, with high-risk ones penalised rather than banned so they stay visible as strategic bets. All\nfigures are illustrative inputs a team would estimate in workshops.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Candidate:\n    name: str\n    hours_per_year: float      # volume x time per item, from workflow interviews\n    unstructured_input: bool   # free text, documents, images, speech\n    rule_solvable: bool        # a stable rule set or lookup already answers it\n    data_reachable: bool       # representative samples obtainable this month\n    human_review: bool         # a person checks output before it takes effect\n    error_cost: int            # 1 = trivial, 3 = moderate, 5 = regulatory or customer harm\n\ndef screen(c: Candidate):\n    if c.rule_solvable or not c.unstructured_input:\n        return None, \"automate without a model\"\n    if not c.data_reachable:\n        return None, \"blocked: start the access request now\"\n    risk = c.error_cost - (2 if c.human_review else 0)   # review absorbs some error cost\n    return c.hours_per_year / max(risk, 1), \"candidate\"\n\nideas = [\n    Candidate(\"Draft replies to supplier emails\", 3000, True, False, True, True, 2),\n    Candidate(\"Flag invoices over approval limit\", 1500, False, True, True, False, 3),\n    Candidate(\"Auto-decide loan applications\", 9000, True, False, True, False, 5),\n    Candidate(\"Extract fields from scanned contracts\", 2500, True, False, False, True, 3),\n]\nfor c in ideas:\n    score, verdict = screen(c)\n    print(f\"{c.name:40} {verdict:40} {'' if score is None else round(score)}\")\n```\n\n| Candidate | Outcome | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Supplier-email drafts | Score 3,000 | Risk 0 floors at 1 |\n| Loan decisions | Score 1,800 | Three times the hours, but unreviewed and high-consequence |\n| Invoice limits | Automate without a model | It is a rule |\n| Contract extraction | Blocked on data | Tells you what access request to start in parallel |\n\n### Break-even accuracy: assist vs automate\nThe two formulas from step 3a, so a workshop can test a candidate's numbers live. Inputs are the\nillustrative figures above.\n\n```python\ndef assist_break_even(w, e, r):\n    \"\"\"Draft quality p* at which reviewing a draft costs the same as doing it by hand.\"\"\"\n    return r / (w - e + r)\n\ndef assist_expected_minutes(p, w, e, r):\n    return p * e + (1 - p) * (r + w)\n\ndef automate_max_error_rate(value_saved_per_item, cost_per_error):\n    return value_saved_per_item / cost_per_error\n\nprint(round(assist_break_even(w=12, e=3, r=1), 2))                   # 0.1\nprint(round(assist_expected_minutes(p=0.7, w=12, e=3, r=1), 1))      # 6.0 minutes vs 12 by hand\nprint(automate_max_error_rate(value_saved_per_item=8, cost_per_error=200))  # 0.04\n```\n\n- **Reading it:** an assistant pays from about 10% good drafts, while the automaton needs fewer than 4%\n  errors just to break even.\n- **Before trusting the assist number,** measure reviewer catch rate, because automation bias pushes the\n  real error rate toward the model's raw rate.","patterns":["**Two-day probe harness** — for every shortlisted candidate, keep one script that runs a plain prompt over 20 to 50 real items and writes outputs to a sheet the person who does the work scores. Reuse it across candidates so probes are comparable. Monitor the share scored usable and the failure types.","**Seeded-error review audits** — once an assist use case is live, periodically insert drafts with known errors into the review queue and record how many reviewers catch. A falling catch rate signals automation bias. Monitor catch rate per reviewer and edit distance between draft and final.","**Risk review in parallel** — file the use case with the organisation's AI risk classification process the day it is shortlisted, not at launch. Track days in review per candidate, so a high-risk strategic bet does not surprise the sponsor.","**Living candidate backlog** — keep the one-line inventory with input, output, value estimate, baseline and risk class in a shared tracker, and re-score after each deployment. Monitor which assumptions (data quality, adoption) the last deployment disproved.","**Shared plumbing first** — sequence use cases so the first one builds connectors, the evaluation harness and access patterns the next ones reuse. Track how much of each new use case's build time goes to reused versus new integration work."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Candidate: role, task, input, output\"] --> F{\"Needs a model?\"}\n    F -->|\"rule or lookup\"| A[\"Automate without a model\"]\n    F -->|\"unstructured, patterned\"| D{\"Data reachable now?\"}\n    D -->|\"no\"| X[\"Start access request\"]\n    D -->|\"yes\"| P[\"Probe 20 to 50 real items\"]\n    P --> R[\"Classify risk and review\"]\n    R --> V[\"Score value vs feasibility\"]\n    V --> S[\"Portfolio: first use case plus strategic bet\"]\n    S --> T[\"Choose technique last\"]","caption":"Each candidate passes three gates before it is scored; failures are routed to a non-model fix or a\nparallel access request rather than dropped silently."},"interview":[{"q":"What is verification asymmetry and why does it matter for picking use cases?","a":"- **Definition:** checking an output is much cheaper than producing it, as with a drafted reply a human\n  approves or extracted fields a human confirms.\n- **Why it matters:** human review then turns a probabilistic component into a reliable system at low\n  cost, so even mediocre accuracy saves time.\n- **Caveat:** it only holds while reviewers really check; measure catch rate against automation bias."},{"q":"A customer asks you to \"find agent use cases\" across their company. How do you respond?","a":"- **Reframe to problems first.** Build the inventory from real workflows as \"role, time, task, input,\n  output\" lines, without naming a technique.\n- **Gate on AI fit and data reachability**, so rule-solvable tasks and blocked candidates are routed to\n  scripts or access requests.\n- **Choose the technique last**; some survivors will be a prompt or a workflow, not an agent."},{"q":"When would you use a model for a task that rules could in principle solve?","a":"- **When the rules are messy, change often or are undocumented**, approximating them with a model can be\n  cheaper than codifying and maintaining them.\n- **Not when the logic is a stable threshold or lookup** on structured data, because a model adds cost,\n  latency and error to a problem a rule answers exactly."},{"q":"Draft quality is 70% on a task that takes 12 minutes by hand. Is an assistant worth it?","a":"- **Use the assist formula:** expected time `p·e + (1−p)·(r + w)`. With e = 3 and r = 1, that is\n  0.7·3 + 0.3·13 = 6 minutes, half the manual time.\n- **Break-even is about 10%** good drafts (`p* = r / (w − e + r)`), so 70% pays clearly.\n- **The same model unreviewed** would need errors below `value saved per item ÷ C`, for example 4% at $8\n  saved against $200 per error."},{"q":"How do you balance quick wins against strategic value in an AI portfolio?","a":"- **First use case:** frequent, measurable against a baseline, human-reviewed and reachable with current\n  data, because it earns trust and builds reusable plumbing.\n- **Alongside it:** one larger strategic bet kept in discovery, with its risk review running in parallel,\n  so the programme is not only quick wins."}],"tradeoffs":["**Trust vs headline value.** Starting with a low-risk, reviewed use case builds trust and reusable plumbing quickly, but its value may be modest. When the sponsor needs a headline result to keep funding, pair it with a strategic bet.","**Transparency vs false precision.** A formal scoring matrix makes prioritisation transparent and harder to hijack, but its weights are judgement dressed as numbers. When two candidates score close, decide on qualitative factors instead.","**Model vs codified rules.** Gating out rule-solvable tasks keeps LLMs where they add value, but some messy rule sets are cheaper to approximate with a model than to codify. That flips when rules change often or are undocumented.","**Speed vs governance learning.** Penalising high-risk use cases speeds the first delivery, but defers the governance learning the organisation will eventually need. Run the risk review early when the strategic case depends on it."],"pitfalls":["**Choosing the technique before the problem (\"find us agent use cases\").** Symptom: a shortlist where every item is an agent and several are just scheduled scripts with an LLM bolted on.","**Ranking by value alone.** Symptom: a first project stuck in legal, security or data-access review for months while stakeholders lose patience.","**Ignoring where output lands.** Symptom: a model that works in a notebook but has no system, screen or queue to deliver into, so nobody uses its results.","**Not checking for a measurable baseline.** Symptom: a finished pilot where nobody can say whether it improved anything, because the current time or error rate was never recorded.","**Assuming human review makes any accuracy acceptable.** Symptom: reviewers approve drafts unread once they are usually right, so errors reach customers at the model's raw error rate.","**Treating the candidate list as fixed.** Symptom: a stale backlog that ignores what the first deployment taught about data quality and user adoption."],"when":"- **Use when** a customer or organisation has more AI ideas than capacity, at the start of an AI\n  programme, and when choosing the next use case after a first deployment.\n- **Run it as a short workshop series:** collect candidates from real workflows, screen them with the\n  people who own the work, and leave with two or three shortlisted candidates, each with an input, output,\n  value estimate, baseline and risk class.\n- **Avoid when** the problem is already fixed by contract; go straight to decomposition.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"the customer has twenty AI ideas, which do you start with?\" and answer with the\n  four axes, not with the most exciting idea.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sanity-checks feasibility of shortlisted candidates by pulling sample data, trying a quick prompt and estimating per-item latency and cost before anyone commits.","architect":"Owns the portfolio view: which use cases share connectors, evaluation tooling and data access, and therefore which sequence builds a reusable platform.","fde":"Facilitates the use-case workshop with the customer's teams, keeps executive pet projects honest against the screening criteria, and starts access requests for blocked candidates."},"check":{"q":"A bank's innovation team brings you two candidates: AI that writes first drafts of responses to\ncustomer complaints for agents to edit, and AI that decides whether to refund disputed card transactions.\nThe second is worth more. Which do you recommend first, and why?","a":"The complaint drafts. Both involve unstructured text, but the drafts have strong verification\nasymmetry: an agent reads and edits each one, so occasional errors are caught cheaply and the value\n(time saved per complaint) is measurable against current handling time. Refund decisions act directly on\ncustomer money, are likely to be regulated and auditable, need explanation for each decision, and\nprobably lack clean labelled outcomes. They belong in the portfolio as a strategic bet with a parallel\nrisk review, perhaps starting as decision support for a human. Starting there would likely stall in\ngovernance and risk the programme's credibility."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which property most strongly suggests a task is a good first AI use case?","options":["It has the highest estimated annual value in the inventory","Checking an output is much cheaper than producing it, and a person reviews outputs before they take effect","It can be fully automated with no human involvement from day one","It uses the newest model capabilities the vendor has released"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Verification asymmetry plus human review turns a probabilistic component into a reliable system at low cost. Highest value often comes with the highest risk and access hurdles, which stall first projects."},{"q":"A candidate is \"flag purchase orders above the approval limit for the department\". What is the right screening outcome?","options":["A strong AI candidate, because it is high-volume and repetitive","A candidate for fine-tuning, because approval limits are company-specific","Solve it with a rule or a query, because the logic is a stable threshold check on structured data","Block it until the team can label thousands of historical purchase orders"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The task is a deterministic comparison on structured fields. A model would add cost, latency and error to a problem that a rule answers exactly; repetitiveness alone does not make a task need AI."},{"q":"Why choose the implementation technique (RAG, fine-tuning, agent) only after a use case survives screening?","options":["Because techniques change too fast to plan for","Because the vendor contract usually determines the technique","Because choosing the technique first biases the inventory toward problems that fit it, including ones that need no model","Because screening criteria are different for each technique"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Starting from \"we want an agent\" turns discovery into a search for agent-shaped problems and lets deterministic tasks sneak in. The task's input, output, error cost and data should decide the technique."}]},{"key":"scoping/problem-decomposition","sources":[],"why":"- **One big prompt cannot be debugged.** \"Automate invoice exception handling\" as a single prompt works on\n  the three invoices you tried and fails opaquely on the fourth. You cannot tell whether the PDF was\n  misread, the wrong field extracted, the purchase order lookup missed, or the model reasoned badly, so\n  every fix is a prompt tweak that might break something else.\n- **Errors compound.** If a task silently requires five steps that each succeed 95% of the time\n  independently, the chain succeeds about 0.95⁵ ≈ 77% of the time. You only see this, and only know which\n  step to improve, if the steps are explicit.\n- **Most of a business process is not a model problem.** Lookups, joins, threshold checks and API calls are\n  deterministic; putting them inside a prompt makes exact operations approximate.\n- **Run-time planning does not replace design-time decomposition.** Letting an agent work out the steps\n  moves decomposition from design time, where you can test it, to run time, where it varies per request.\n  Agent planning has its place (see task decomposition in the agents module), but you still need to know\n  what the agent should do, what tools it needs and how to judge it.","how":"### 1. State the goal as an observable output\n- **Write what exists when the problem is solved:** \"each invoice exception is routed to the right owner\n  with a reason code and a drafted resolution, within one business day\".\n- **An observable output can be tested;** an aspiration (\"streamline AP\") cannot.\n\n### 2. Work backwards from the output\n- **Ask what must be true immediately before the output can be produced, and repeat.**\n- **Backwards keeps every sub-problem tied to the goal**, because forward brainstorming produces steps\n  nobody needs.\n\n### 3. Split at natural seams\n| Seam | Example |\n|---|---|\n| **Data type changes** | Scanned image to text, text to structured fields |\n| **A decision is made** | Classify the exception reason |\n| **A system boundary is crossed** | Reading the ERP, sending an email |\n| **Responsibility passes between people** | AP clerk to purchasing owner |\n\n- **Seams are where you put a test and an interface**, because the artefact passed across them is concrete\n  and inspectable.\n\n### 4. Write a contract for each node\nRecord for each sub-problem: input schema, output schema, technique, owner and acceptance test. The\ntechnique choice is the heart of the exercise, in order of preference:\n\n1. **Deterministic code** for lookups, joins, arithmetic, rules and API calls.\n2. **Classical ML or a classifier** for high-volume, fixed-label decisions with labelled history.\n3. **Retrieval** where the answer exists in a corpus and must be cited.\n4. **LLM generation or extraction** for unstructured input and open-ended output.\n5. **Human** for high-consequence or low-volume judgements.\n\n- **Push work toward the top of this list wherever it suffices**, because every probabilistic node needs an\n  evaluation set and contributes compounding error.\n- **Stop splitting when a node has exactly one technique, one owner and an acceptance test you could write\n  today.** Splitting further adds interfaces without adding testability.\n- **Not finished:** a node that still needs two techniques, or whose test you cannot state.\n\n### 5. Check coverage and independence\n- **MECE test** (from consulting: mutually exclusive, collectively exhaustive): sub-problems cover the goal\n  with no gaps and little overlap.\n- **Gaps** show up as \"and then somehow\" in the walkthrough.\n- **Overlaps** show up as two components that both decide the same thing.\n\n### 6. Estimate per-node quality and find the critical node\n- **Give each probabilistic node a rough expected accuracy**, multiply along the chain, and see which node\n  dominates the loss.\n- **Add mitigations where they are cheapest:** a human review on one node, a deterministic validator after\n  an extraction (does the amount equal the sum of line items?), or a confidence threshold that routes\n  uncertain cases to a person.\n\n**Mitigations change *what* you multiply.** Split each node's outcomes three ways:\n\n| Outcome | Meaning | Rate, for error rate *q* and catch fraction *c* |\n|---|---|---|\n| Correct | Passed on, right | 1 − q |\n| **Escalated** | Caught and sent to a person | `q·c` (plus correct items the check flags by mistake) |\n| **Silent error** | Wrong and passed on | `q·(1 − c)` |\n\n- **Silent errors are what compound and reach users;** escalations cost reviewer time and lower the\n  automation rate.\n- **Report two end-to-end numbers:** the share of items fully automated and correct, and the share that\n  went wrong silently.\n- **Because** a design that trades silent errors for escalations can be a large improvement even though its\n  \"automation rate\" goes down.\n\n### 7. Prototype the riskiest node first\n- **The node with the most uncertainty**, usually the one with the messiest input, gets a spike before\n  anything else is built.\n- **Because** if it cannot reach the required quality, the whole design changes.\n\n### 8. Keep the tree as a living artefact\n- **It becomes the backbone** of the statement of work, the requirements trace and the evaluation plan.\n- **Tests follow the tree:** one test suite per node plus an end-to-end suite.","example":"### Multiplying per-node success rates\nInvoice exception handling, decomposed into five nodes with rough per-node success rates a team might\nestimate from a spike (illustrative). The script multiplies along the chain, assuming independent\nfailures, and shows which single improvement raises end-to-end success most.\n\n```python\nfrom math import prod\n\nnodes = [  # (name, technique, estimated success rate)\n    (\"parse PDF to text\",                 \"OCR + layout parser\",   0.98),\n    (\"extract vendor, amount, PO number\", \"LLM extraction\",        0.93),\n    (\"match invoice to PO in ERP\",        \"deterministic join\",    0.99),\n    (\"classify exception reason\",         \"LLM classifier\",        0.88),\n    (\"draft resolution email\",            \"LLM generation\",        0.95),\n]\n\ndef end_to_end(rates):\n    return prod(rates)\n\nbase = [r for _, _, r in nodes]\nprint(f\"end-to-end: {end_to_end(base):.1%}\")          # about 75%\n\nfor i, (name, tech, r) in enumerate(nodes):\n    improved = base.copy()\n    improved[i] = 0.99                                 # what if this node were near-perfect?\n    print(f\"{name:36} {tech:20} {r:.0%} -> e2e {end_to_end(improved):.1%}\")\n```\n\n| Change | End-to-end success |\n|---|---|\n| Baseline | About 75%, worse than any single node |\n| Classifier 88% → 99% | About 85% |\n| Extractor → 99% | Only about 80% |\n| ERP join → 99% | No change; it is already at 99% |\n\n- **Effort goes to the classifier:** a fixed reason-code list, few-shot examples, or routing low-confidence\n  cases to a human.\n- **Treat the product as a rough guide, not a forecast.** Real failures are often correlated (a bad scan\n  breaks both parsing and extraction), and a validator that catches an error before it propagates breaks\n  the simple multiplication.\n\n### Silent errors vs escalations\nThe same chain with a validator after extraction (catching an illustrative 70% of its errors) and a\nconfidence threshold on the classifier (catching 60%). Error rates are 1 minus the success rates above.\n\n```python\nfrom math import prod\n\ndef outcomes(nodes):                       # nodes: (error rate q, catch fraction c)\n    correct = prod(1 - q for q, c in nodes)\n    not_escalated = prod(1 - q * c for q, c in nodes)\n    return correct, not_escalated - correct, 1 - not_escalated\n\nbase    = [(0.02, 0.0), (0.07, 0.0), (0.01, 0.0), (0.12, 0.0), (0.05, 0.0)]\nguarded = [(0.02, 0.0), (0.07, 0.7), (0.01, 0.0), (0.12, 0.6), (0.05, 0.0)]\nfor name, n in ((\"no checks\", base), (\"validator + threshold\", guarded)):\n    ok, silent, esc = outcomes(n)\n    print(f\"{name:22} automated-correct {ok:.1%}  silent {silent:.1%}  escalated {esc:.1%}\")\n# no checks              automated-correct 75.4%  silent 24.6%  escalated 0.0%\n# validator + threshold  automated-correct 75.4%  silent 12.8%  escalated 11.7%\n```\n\n- **Silent errors roughly halve** while the automation rate falls from 100% to about 88%, which is the\n  trade the two-number report makes visible.\n- **Simplifications:** no false flags on correct items, and errors caught at one node are not counted again\n  later; real numbers come from labelled runs.","patterns":["**Typed contract per node** — define each node's input and output as a schema (for example a Pydantic model or JSON Schema) and validate at every seam, so a rephrased model output fails loudly instead of breaking a downstream regex. Monitor schema-validation failures per node.","**Deterministic validators at seams** — after extraction, check invariants in code: amount equals the sum of line items, PO number exists in the ERP, dates are in range. Route failures to a review queue. Monitor catch rate and how many flagged items were actually correct.","**Confidence-gated routing** — send classifier outputs below a tuned threshold to a person, and tune the threshold on labelled data against reviewer capacity. Monitor escalation volume against queue capacity and the silent-error rate on audited samples.","**Per-node plus end-to-end eval suites** — keep one evaluation set per probabilistic node and one end-to-end set in CI, run on every prompt or model change. Monitor both, because node metrics can all look fine while the end-to-end result is poor.","**Per-node trace logging** — log each node's input, output and outcome (correct, escalated, error) under one request ID, so error analysis can attribute a bad final result to the node where it started."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    I[\"Invoice PDF\"] --> P[\"Parse PDF to text\"]\n    P --> E[\"LLM: extract vendor, amount, PO\"]\n    E --> V{\"Validator: amount equals line items?\"}\n    V -->|\"fails\"| H[\"Human queue\"]\n    V -->|\"passes\"| J[\"Deterministic join to PO in ERP\"]\n    J --> C[\"LLM: classify exception reason\"]\n    C --> T{\"Confidence above threshold?\"}\n    T -->|\"no\"| H\n    T -->|\"yes\"| D[\"LLM: draft resolution email\"]\n    D --> O[\"Routed to owner with reason code\"]","caption":"The invoice-exception chain: deterministic and probabilistic nodes alternate, and a validator or\nthreshold at a seam turns silent errors into escalations to a person."},"interview":[{"q":"Why decompose an AI problem instead of giving one capable model the whole task?","a":"- **Debuggability:** with explicit nodes you can tell whether parsing, extraction, lookup or reasoning\n  failed, instead of tweaking one prompt and hoping.\n- **Compounding error becomes visible:** five steps at 95% each give about 77% end to end, and only explicit\n  steps tell you which one to improve.\n- **Exact work stays exact:** lookups and arithmetic move into code instead of becoming approximate inside a\n  prompt."},{"q":"Every component reports over 90% accuracy, but users say a quarter of cases are wrong. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Multiply, do not average:** several 90%+ nodes in a chain can easily land near 75% end to end.\n- **Trace a sample of bad cases** through per-node logs to find where each error started, and check for\n  correlated failures such as bad scans hurting parsing and extraction together.\n- **Add an end-to-end eval set** if none exists, and fix the node that dominates the loss first."},{"q":"When would you merge nodes or let an agent plan instead of an explicit pipeline?","a":"- **Merge** when one model call handles two steps reliably, because each extra seam adds interface code and\n  latency.\n- **Use an agent** when the space of paths is genuinely open-ended and a fixed pipeline cannot foresee the\n  cases, while keeping a design-time decomposition of goals, tools and acceptance tests."},{"q":"Adding a validator lowered your automation rate. How do you explain that it is an improvement?","a":"- **Report two numbers:** automated-and-correct share and silent-error share. A validator converts silent\n  errors (`q·(1 − c)` remain) into escalations (`q·c`).\n- **Silent errors reach users and compound;** escalations only cost reviewer time.\n- **Example:** silent errors falling from about 25% to 13% while escalations rise to 12% is usually a good\n  trade if the queue has capacity."},{"q":"How do you decide which node to prototype first?","a":"- **The riskiest one,** usually the messiest input such as scanned documents, because if it cannot reach the\n  required quality the whole design changes.\n- **Not the easy ones,** which leave a mostly finished pipeline blocked on the hard extraction for weeks."}],"tradeoffs":["**Testability vs integration cost.** Fine-grained decomposition makes each node testable and replaceable, but adds interfaces, latency and integration code. When one model call handles two steps reliably, merging them is simpler.","**Exactness vs brittleness.** Moving logic into deterministic code gives exactness and auditability, but brittle rules break on variation. It flips when the inputs are too varied to enumerate rules for.","**Predictability vs adaptability.** Explicit pipelines are predictable and debuggable, but cannot adapt to cases the designer did not foresee. When the space of paths is genuinely open-ended, an agent with tools fits better.","**Node metrics vs end-to-end truth.** Per-node evaluation finds the weak link quickly, but node metrics can all look fine while the end-to-end result is poor. Always keep an end-to-end test alongside the node tests."],"pitfalls":["**Decomposing by org chart or by existing system instead of by data flow.** Symptom: nodes whose outputs nobody consumes and handoffs that recreate today's delays.","**Putting deterministic steps inside the prompt.** Symptom: arithmetic errors, invented IDs or wrong dates in otherwise fluent output.","**Treating per-node accuracy as end-to-end accuracy.** Symptom: a team reporting 90%+ on every component while users see a large share of cases go wrong.","**Leaving node outputs unstructured.** Symptom: downstream code parsing free text with regexes and failing whenever the model rephrases.","**Reporting a single success rate that mixes escalations and silent errors.** Symptom: operations say \"too many items reach the queue\" and risk says \"too many errors reach customers\", and neither can be traded off because they were never measured separately.","**Building the easy nodes first.** Symptom: a mostly finished pipeline blocked for weeks on the one hard extraction that should have been spiked on day one."],"when":"- **Use before** estimating, before writing a statement of work and before choosing a model. It is worth\n  doing even for small builds, because the output (nodes with contracts and tests) is the evaluation plan.\n- **Avoid heavy decomposition** only for a genuinely single-step task, such as summarising one document type\n  for internal reading.\n- **Measure:** every node has an owner, a technique and a test, and you can name the node that dominates\n  end-to-end error.\n- **Interviews:** decomposing a vague goal aloud, marking which parts are deterministic, and multiplying\n  error rates is a strong signal of senior judgement.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements each node behind a typed interface with its own tests, and adds validators between nodes so failures are caught at the seam where they occur.","architect":"Owns the decomposition itself: where the seams go, which nodes are deterministic, where humans sit, and how node contracts map to services and evaluation suites.","fde":"Decomposes the customer's goal in a workshop with their subject experts, and uses the tree to agree scope, ownership and what \"done\" means for each piece."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants a system that \"answers any question about our 10,000 contracts\". How would you\ndecompose it, and which node would you prototype first?","a":"Start from observable outputs: an answer with citations to clauses, or \"not found\". Split into:\ndocument ingestion and parsing (scans, tables); metadata extraction (counterparty, dates, contract type)\ninto structured fields; question routing, since \"which contracts expire next quarter?\" is a structured\nquery over extracted fields, not retrieval; clause retrieval for open questions; answer generation\nconstrained to retrieved text; and citation checking. Deterministic parts are the date queries and\nfilters. I would spike parsing and metadata extraction on a sample of the ugliest real contracts first,\nbecause everything else depends on them and scanned legal documents are where quality is most uncertain."},"quiz":[{"q":"A pipeline has four independent probabilistic steps with success rates 0.95, 0.90, 0.97 and 0.92. What is the approximate end-to-end success rate?","options":["About 93%, the average of the four","About 90%, the weakest step","About 76%, the product of the four","About 97%, the strongest step"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With independent failures the chain succeeds only when every step succeeds, so rates multiply: 0.95 × 0.90 × 0.97 × 0.92 ≈ 0.76. Averages and the weakest step both overstate end-to-end quality."},{"q":"Which is the best seam at which to split a sub-problem?","options":["Between two teams' sprint boundaries","Where the data changes form, such as scanned image to text or text to structured fields","After every model call, regardless of what it produces","At the point where the prompt exceeds a fixed token budget"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A change of data form produces a concrete, inspectable artefact that can carry a contract and a test. Splits by team or token count do not correspond to anything you can verify."},{"q":"In the decomposition, why move a purchase-order lookup out of the LLM and into code?","options":["Because LLMs cannot read purchase-order numbers","Because code is always cheaper to maintain than prompts","Because the lookup is exact and deterministic, and doing it in a prompt makes an exact operation approximate","Because ERP systems forbid access from LLMs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A join or lookup has one correct answer that code returns every time. Inside a prompt it gains error rate, cost and latency while adding nothing, and becomes a compounding failure point."}]},{"key":"scoping/workflow-analysis","sources":["Mike Rother and John Shook, 1999 — Learning to See","Wil van der Aalst, 2016 — Process Mining: Data Science in Action"],"why":"- **AI is pitched at tasks; customers buy outcomes.** \"Summarise the ticket\" or \"extract the fields\" is a\n  task. The customer wants tickets resolved faster, claims paid correctly, fewer escalations. The workflow\n  links the two, and it is where many AI deployments quietly fail to deliver.\n- **Elapsed time is dominated by waiting, not working.** Items sit in queues, wait for an approver, or wait\n  for missing information. If AI halves research time but the ticket then waits two days for approval,\n  customer-visible cycle time barely moves.\n- **Flow efficiency is usually small.** Lean practice calls working time ÷ elapsed time *flow efficiency*;\n  in office processes it is commonly a small fraction, so most of the opportunity sits in the waits.\n- **The documented process is not the real one.** Diagrams show the happy path. Real work includes\n  exception paths, rework loops, side channels (spreadsheets, chat messages, personal email) and tacit\n  checks experienced staff never write down.\n- **Designing from the official diagram breaks.** The system fails on the cases that make up much of the\n  actual volume, or skips a check that quietly mattered. Workflow analysis sees the real flow first.","how":"### 1. Fix the scope and the unit of work\n- **Choose one workflow and its unit:** a ticket, a claim, an invoice, a contract.\n- **Define start and end:** complaint received to complaint closed.\n- **Measure everything per unit**, because averages across mixed units hide the bottleneck.\n\n### 2. Observe, do not only interview\n- **Sit with people doing the work** (\"go to the gemba\", in lean terms) and watch several real items end to\n  end.\n- **Interviews give the believed process;** observation shows the copy-paste between systems, the lookup in\n  a personal spreadsheet and the colleague they message for an answer.\n\n### 3. Map the steps with actors and systems\n- **Draw a swimlane diagram:** one lane per role or system, boxes for steps, arrows for handoffs, diamonds\n  for decisions.\n- **For each step capture:** who does it, which system they use, what input they need and what they\n  produce.\n- **Mark handoffs explicitly**, because every handoff is a queue and a chance for context loss.\n\n### 4. Measure touch time and wait time\n| Measure | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| *Touch time* | Someone is actively working on the item |\n| *Wait time* | The item sits before the step starts |\n\n- **This split is the core of a value stream map.**\n- **Mine the event log where one exists** (ticket history, case management audit trail, ERP change log).\n  Every record of case ID, activity and timestamp lets you compute waits and paths for thousands of cases\n  instead of the handful you observed. This is the basis of process mining.\n\n### 5. Enumerate variants and exceptions\n- **Count distinct paths** and how frequent each is.\n- **Track rework loops** (an item sent back), escalations and \"request more information\" steps.\n- **Design for the variants that carry the volume**, and decide explicitly which rare variants stay manual.\n\n### 6. Locate the constraint\n- **Find the step that limits throughput or dominates elapsed time.**\n- **A growing queue is the tell.** It means items arrive faster than that step completes them: its capacity\n  is below the arrival rate.\n- **Little's law explains why it hurts:** average items in progress = throughput × average time in system,\n  for a stable system. With throughput capped by the constraint, every extra item queued adds directly to\n  time in system.\n\n**Sanity check (illustrative numbers):**\n\n| Situation | Result |\n|---|---|\n| 200 tickets open, team closes 50 a day | Average time in system = 200 ÷ 50 = 4 days |\n| Halve research effort, approval caps throughput | Still 50 a day, so the 4 days stay |\n| Double approval capacity, or remove items that did not need approval | Throughput rises, up to the capacity of the next-slowest step, and the same WIP clears faster |\n\n- **Theory-of-constraints rule in one line:** an hour saved at a non-constraint is a mirage; an hour saved\n  at the constraint is an hour for the whole system.\n\n### 7. Identify AI insertion points\n- **Ask how AI could help at each step:** removing a step, shortening touch time, preventing a rework loop\n  (by catching missing information at intake), or eliminating a wait (by pre-computing what the approver\n  needs).\n- **Mark where humans must stay in the loop** and what they will review.\n- **Check where the constraint moves.** If agents finish research twice as fast, items reach approval twice\n  as fast; if approval was near capacity, it becomes the new constraint and its queue grows.\n- **Every human review is a new step with its own capacity.** 400 items a day at 2 minutes of review each\n  is over 13 reviewer-hours daily, which someone must staff.\n\n### 8. Draft the to-be workflow and the baseline\n- **Draw the proposed flow** with the AI component, its human checkpoints and the fallback path when it is\n  unsure.\n- **Record the baseline:** current cycle time, touch time, error and rework rates, because without them the\n  pilot cannot show improvement.","example":"### Mining touch and wait time from a ticket log\nA support team wants AI to \"write better research notes\". Exporting three tickets from the ticketing\nsystem's history (illustrative data) and splitting each step into touch and wait time shows where time\nactually goes.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict, Counter\n\n# (case_id, activity, start_hour, end_hour) exported from the ticketing system\nlog = [\n    (\"T1\", \"receive\", 0.0, 0.1), (\"T1\", \"triage\", 4.0, 4.2), (\"T1\", \"research\", 22.0, 22.6),\n    (\"T1\", \"approve\", 47.0, 47.1), (\"T1\", \"reply\", 49.0, 49.3),\n    (\"T2\", \"receive\", 0.0, 0.1), (\"T2\", \"triage\", 2.0, 2.1), (\"T2\", \"research\", 26.0, 26.4),\n    (\"T2\", \"approve\", 50.0, 50.1), (\"T2\", \"reply\", 51.0, 51.2),\n    (\"T3\", \"receive\", 0.0, 0.1), (\"T3\", \"triage\", 6.0, 6.3), (\"T3\", \"research\", 20.0, 20.8),\n    (\"T3\", \"request info\", 21.0, 21.1), (\"T3\", \"research\", 70.0, 70.5),\n    (\"T3\", \"approve\", 93.0, 93.1), (\"T3\", \"reply\", 95.0, 95.2),\n]\ncases = defaultdict(list)\nfor case, act, start, end in log:\n    cases[case].append((start, end, act))\n\ntouch, wait, variants = defaultdict(float), defaultdict(float), Counter()\nfor steps in cases.values():\n    steps.sort()\n    variants[\" > \".join(a for _, _, a in steps)] += 1\n    prev_end = steps[0][0]\n    for start, end, act in steps:\n        wait[act] += start - prev_end      # time the item sat in a queue before this step\n        touch[act] += end - start          # time someone actually worked on it\n        prev_end = end\n\nt, w = sum(touch.values()), sum(wait.values())\nprint(f\"touch {t:.1f}h, wait {w:.1f}h, flow efficiency {t / (t + w):.1%}\")\nfor act in touch:\n    print(f\"{act:13} touch {touch[act]:4.1f}h   wait before {wait[act]:5.1f}h\")\nprint(len(variants), \"variants; most common:\", variants.most_common(1)[0])\n```\n\n| Result across the three tickets | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Work vs waiting | 4.3 hours of work inside 191 hours of waiting |\n| Flow efficiency | About 2% |\n| Research touch time | 2.3 hours |\n| Waiting before research | 104 hours, including two days in T3 waiting for customer information |\n| Waiting for approval | 70 hours |\n\n- **Better research notes would save little.**\n- **The waits are the target:** catch missing information at intake and pre-package the approver's\n  evidence.\n\n### Worked scenario: the constraint moves\nDaily capacities for a ticket flow (illustrative). Arrivals are 60 tickets a day.\n\n| Stage | Research capacity | Approval capacity | Throughput | Queue that grows |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Before | 55 a day | 58 a day | 55 a day (research caps it) | Before research, +5 a day |\n| AI doubles research speed | 110 a day | 58 a day | 58 a day (approval caps it) | Before approval, +2 a day |\n| Plus pre-packaged approval evidence | 110 a day | 70 a day | 60 a day (all arrivals) | None; WIP stops growing |\n\n- **Doubling research capacity bought only 3 tickets a day**, because the constraint moved to approval.\n- **Little's law turns that into days:** at a steady WIP of 200, moving throughput from 55 to 60 cuts average\n  time in system from about 3.6 to about 3.3 days; clearing the backlog shrinks WIP and time further.\n- **Staffing check:** if the AI step adds a 2-minute human review on all 60 tickets, that is 2 reviewer-hours\n  a day of new capacity someone must provide.","patterns":["**Emit the same events before and after** — when the AI step goes live, have it write case ID, activity and timestamp into the same log as the existing steps, so pre- and post-launch flows are computed by one script. Monitor cycle time, waits per step and flow efficiency on the same dashboard.","**Queue-length alerting per step** — track open items in front of each step daily and alert when a queue grows for several consecutive days, because that is the signature of a step whose capacity is below the arrival rate. Watch the step downstream of any AI insertion first.","**Intake completeness check** — run an automated check at intake for the information later steps need, and request missing items immediately instead of discovering them at research. Monitor the share of items that enter a \"request info\" loop and the wait it adds.","**Variant budget** — automate the variants that carry most of the volume and route rare variants to a manual path by explicit rule. Monitor the share of items on the manual path; a rising share means the variant mix has shifted.","**Reviewer capacity as a planned step** — size human review as items a day × minutes per review before launch, staff it, and monitor review queue length and edit rate so rubber-stamping or full rework shows up early."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Scope: one workflow, one unit\"] --> O[\"Observe real items\"]\n    S --> L[(\"Event log: case, activity, time\")]\n    O --> M[\"Swimlane map with handoffs\"]\n    L --> M\n    M --> T[\"Split touch time vs wait time\"]\n    T --> V[\"Count variants and rework\"]\n    V --> C[\"Locate the constraint\"]\n    C --> A[\"Choose AI insertion point\"]\n    A --> Q{\"Constraint moved downstream?\"}\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| C\n    Q -->|\"no\"| B[\"To-be flow plus baseline\"]","caption":"The analysis loop: map the real flow from observation and logs, split touch from wait, find the\nconstraint, then check that the AI insertion does not just move the queue to the next step."},"interview":[{"q":"What is flow efficiency and why does it change where you put AI?","a":"- **Definition:** touch time ÷ elapsed time per unit of work. In office processes it is commonly a small\n  fraction; the ticket example was about 2%.\n- **Why it matters:** if waits dominate, shortening touch time barely moves cycle time, so AI belongs where\n  it removes a wait or a rework loop, such as completeness checks at intake."},{"q":"An AI pilot halved handling time but the sponsor says nothing improved. How do you investigate?","a":"- **Pull the event log** for before and after and split touch from wait per step.\n- **Look for the moved constraint:** a queue that shrank before the improved step and grew before the next\n  one means downstream capacity now caps throughput.\n- **Check the new review step** for its own queue, because added human review is a step with finite\n  capacity."},{"q":"When would you not target the constraint?","a":"- **When it sits outside your mandate**, such as another team's approval or a policy you cannot change.\n  Then pick the best step within scope and state explicitly that cycle time is capped elsewhere.\n- **When the constraint is a deliberate control**, such as a regulatory sign-off, where the better lever is\n  pre-packaging evidence rather than removing the step."},{"q":"200 tickets are open and the team closes 50 a day. What is the average time in system, and what would change it?","a":"- **Little's law:** 200 ÷ 50 = 4 days, assuming a stable system.\n- **Only the constraint changes it.** Faster research leaves 50 a day if approval caps throughput; more\n  approval capacity, or removing items that never needed approval, raises throughput up to the next-slowest\n  step."},{"q":"Why observe users if you already have a complete event log?","a":"- **Logs only record steps that touch a system.** Spreadsheets, chat questions and mental checks leave no\n  trace.\n- **Observation explains the log**, for example why one variant has a long wait, and reveals tacit checks an\n  AI design would otherwise skip."}],"tradeoffs":["**Depth vs coverage.** Direct observation reveals tacit steps and side channels, but covers only a handful of cases. When an event log exists, combine both so observation explains what the log measures.","**Statistics vs blind spots.** Process mining gives statistically meaningful paths and waits, but only sees steps that write to a system. Work done in email or chat is invisible unless captured separately.","**Rigour vs customer patience.** A detailed current-state map makes the constraint unarguable, but takes time customers may resent. When the bottleneck is obvious and agreed, map just that segment in depth.","**Outcome vs mandate.** Targeting the constraint maximises outcome improvement, but the constraint may sit in another team or a policy you cannot change. Then pick the best step within your mandate and say so explicitly."],"pitfalls":["**Analysing the documented process instead of the real one.** Symptom: an AI system that handles the happy path but fails on the exception paths that make up much of real volume.","**Measuring only touch time.** Symptom: a pilot that cuts handling time per item yet leaves customer-visible cycle time unchanged, and the sponsor sees no improvement.","**Ignoring side-channel tools.** Symptom: users keep their old spreadsheet open next to the new system, because a check or lookup it supported was never modelled.","**Inserting AI without redesigning the human step around it.** Symptom: reviewers either approve everything unread or redo the work from scratch, erasing the saving.","**Speeding up one step without checking downstream capacity.** Symptom: a queue that shrinks in front of the improved step and grows in front of the next one, with cycle time unchanged.","**Not recording a baseline before changing the flow.** Symptom: an argument after launch about whether things improved, settled by anecdote."],"when":"- **Use whenever** an AI component will sit inside an existing business process, which is almost always in\n  enterprise and forward-deployed work, and do it before choosing which step to automate.\n- **Keep it lean for small scopes:** observe five to ten items, sketch a swimlane, and pull timestamps for a\n  few hundred cases if a log exists.\n- **Go deeper** (full value stream map, process mining) when the sponsor's goal is a cycle-time or\n  throughput outcome.\n- **Measure:** flow efficiency, waits per step, variant frequencies and rework rates.\n- **Interviews:** the signal is recognising that the step the customer named is often not the one that\n  limits the outcome.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Pulls event logs from ticketing or case systems, computes per-step waits and variants, and wires the AI step to emit the same events so before and after are comparable.","architect":"Decides where AI components and human checkpoints sit in the to-be workflow, and whether orchestration lives in an existing workflow engine or a new service.","fde":"Shadows the customer's staff, uncovers the side channels and tacit checks, and presents the current-state map that convinces the sponsor where the real bottleneck is."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants an AI copilot to speed up loan officers' document review, which takes about an\nhour per application. End-to-end, applications take about ten business days. How would you decide whether\nthe copilot is the right investment?","a":"I would map the workflow and pull timestamps from the loan origination system for a few hundred\nrecent applications, splitting touch time from waits per step and counting variants. If most of the ten\ndays is waiting, for example for missing documents from applicants or for a credit committee that meets\ntwice a week, a review copilot saves officer time but barely changes time to decision. In that case the\nhigher-leverage AI use is at intake: checking completeness and requesting missing documents immediately,\nor preparing the committee pack. If review itself queues because officers are the capacity constraint,\nthe copilot is right, and I would baseline review time and error rate first."},"quiz":[{"q":"In a process with 30 minutes of touch time and 4 days of elapsed time, AI halves the touch time. What most likely happens to elapsed time?","options":["It halves, because the work is done twice as fast","It barely changes, because elapsed time is dominated by waiting between steps","It doubles, because reviewers must check AI output","It drops to 15 minutes, because the queue disappears"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Saving 15 minutes of work inside four days of queueing leaves elapsed time almost unchanged. Unless the touched step is the capacity constraint causing the queue, the waits are where elapsed time lives."},{"q":"Why is direct observation of users valuable even when a detailed event log exists?","options":["Event logs are usually inaccurate about timestamps","Regulations require in-person observation before automation","Logs only record steps that touch a system, missing side channels, tacit checks and workarounds","Observation produces larger sample sizes than logs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Logs are excellent for statistics on recorded steps, but spreadsheets, chat questions and mental checks leave no trace. Observation explains what the log measures and reveals what it cannot see."},{"q":"A queue in front of the approval step grows every week. What does that indicate?","options":["The approval step has too much documentation","Items arriving at approval are of lower quality than before","Approval capacity is below the arrival rate, so it is the constraint limiting throughput","The upstream steps are working too slowly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A queue only grows when work arrives faster than the step completes it, so approval capacity is the constraint. Slow upstream steps would shrink this queue, not grow it; and by Little's law, the growing backlog at a capped throughput translates directly into longer time in system."}]},{"key":"scoping/domain-modeling","sources":["Eric Evans, 2003 — Domain-Driven Design"],"why":"- **Every organisation has a private dialect.** In one insurer \"claim\" means a single loss event; in\n  another it means each coverage line within it. \"Active customer\" might mean \"has a contract\", \"logged in\n  this quarter\" or \"billed this month\", depending on whether you ask sales, product or finance.\n- **The knowledge lives in heads.** Experienced staff carry these distinctions, and the documents they\n  write assume them.\n- **A model brings the opposite: a broad, average sense of each word** learned from public text. Asked to\n  \"count active customers\" or \"extract the claim date\", it picks a plausible generic meaning.\n- **The error is silent.** The output is fluent and well formatted, so the wrong number, or the report date\n  instead of the loss date, flows down the pipeline.\n- **The naive fixes fail.**\n  - *Letting the model infer meaning* works only when documents are consistent, and they rarely are across\n    departments.\n  - *Patching each misunderstanding in the prompt* piles up special cases that disagree with the database\n    schema, retrieval filters and evaluation labels, because each was written separately.\n- **One explicit model replaces the patches.** Agreed with subject-matter experts (SMEs), every component\n  reads from it. It also forces scope-changing questions: which entity has the identity, which states\n  exist, and which rules the system must never violate.","how":"### 1. Build the glossary first\n- **List the terms** from interviews, documents and screens, each with a one-line definition, an example and\n  a counter-example.\n- **Domain-driven design calls the result the *ubiquitous language*:** one vocabulary shared by experts,\n  code and documentation.\n- **Start here** because most domain errors in AI systems are vocabulary errors, and a glossary is cheap to\n  validate with SMEs.\n\n### 2. Identify entities and value objects\n| Kind | Definition | Examples | What AI must do |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Entity** | Identity persists while attributes change | A claim, a policy, a customer | Resolve to an existing identifier (which customer is this email about?) |\n| **Value object** | Defined only by its attributes | An address, a money amount with currency | Parse correctly |\n\n**Identity resolution deserves its own design**, because it is where extraction most often silently fails.\nResolve in order of reliability:\n\n1. **Exact key found in the text** (a claim number matching the agreed pattern).\n2. **Deterministic lookup on strong attributes** (policy number plus loss date).\n3. **Fuzzy matching on weak attributes** (a name and a postcode).\n4. **Ask a person.**\n\n- **Record which rule matched**, because a record linked by a fuzzy name match should be treated with less\n  trust than one linked by key.\n\n### 3. Map relationships and cardinality\n- **Record how entities relate:** a policy has many claims, a claim has many coverage lines, a customer can\n  hold several policies.\n- **Cardinality decides retrieval and reasoning.** \"The customer's deductible\" is ambiguous when the\n  customer has three policies, so the system must ask or choose by rule.\n\n### 4. Model lifecycles as state machines\n- **For each key entity, list its states, the permitted transitions, and who or what triggers each.**\n- **This lets an AI system reason about \"what can happen next\"** and stops an agent from taking an action\n  the business forbids (paying a claim that was never approved).\n\n### 5. Write down invariants and business rules\n| Type | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Invariant** | Always holds | A loss date is never after the report date; the payment total never exceeds the approved amount |\n| **Rule** | Guides decisions | Claims above a threshold need a second approver |\n\n- **Enforce invariants in code as validators**, because a model can be asked to respect them but cannot be\n  trusted to always do so.\n- **Some invariants span several entities.** \"The sum of payments across a claim's coverage lines never\n  exceeds its approved amount\" cannot be checked by looking at one line.\n- **DDD groups such entities into an aggregate** with one root (the claim) through which all changes go, so\n  the root can check the rule on every change.\n- **For an agent, the aggregate is the unit a tool acts on.** Expose `add_payment(claim_id, …)` on the\n  claim, not a raw \"update coverage line\" tool that can break the total.\n\n### 6. Draw bounded contexts\n- **Where the same word means different things, do not force one definition.** Model each context\n  separately and define the translation between them.\n- **In AI terms,** each context often gets its own schema, prompt glossary and retrieval index.\n\n### 7. Project the model into every artefact\n| Model element | Projection |\n|---|---|\n| Entities and fields | Typed schemas for structured output and extraction |\n| Allowed values | Enums that constrain classification labels |\n| Glossary | A glossary section in prompts |\n| Key attributes | Metadata fields for retrieval filters |\n| Relationships | Entity and relationship types, if a knowledge graph is warranted |\n| States, rules, edge cases | The label set and edge cases for evaluation |\n\n- **One source, many projections**, because divergence between them is where silent errors come from.\n\n### 8. Validate with instances, not diagrams\n- **SMEs rarely critique a class diagram well**, but they spot errors instantly in concrete examples: \"this\n  claim would never be in that state\".\n- **Walk real records through the model.**","example":"### One schema for extraction, glossary, invariant and lifecycle\nA slice of an insurer's claims domain, agreed with claims SMEs, expressed as a Pydantic v2 schema. The\nsame class constrains LLM extraction output, documents the glossary in field descriptions, enforces an\ninvariant and guards lifecycle transitions that an agent might attempt.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nfrom enum import Enum\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field, model_validator\n\nclass ClaimStatus(str, Enum):           # lifecycle agreed with claims SMEs\n    FNOL = \"first_notice_of_loss\"\n    UNDER_REVIEW = \"under_review\"\n    APPROVED = \"approved\"\n    DENIED = \"denied\"\n    PAID = \"paid\"\n\nALLOWED = {                             # which transitions the business permits\n    ClaimStatus.FNOL: {ClaimStatus.UNDER_REVIEW},\n    ClaimStatus.UNDER_REVIEW: {ClaimStatus.APPROVED, ClaimStatus.DENIED},\n    ClaimStatus.APPROVED: {ClaimStatus.PAID},\n    ClaimStatus.DENIED: set(), ClaimStatus.PAID: set(),\n}\n\nclass Claim(BaseModel):\n    claim_id: str = Field(pattern=r\"^CLM-\\d{8}$\")         # identity comes from the claims system\n    policy_number: str = Field(description=\"Policy, not the customer account number\")\n    loss_date: date = Field(description=\"Date the loss happened, not the date it was reported\")\n    reported_date: date\n    status: ClaimStatus\n    reserve_amount: Decimal = Field(ge=0, description=\"Estimated liability, not the amount paid\")\n\n    @model_validator(mode=\"after\")\n    def loss_before_report(self):\n        if self.loss_date > self.reported_date:           # invariant stated by SMEs\n            raise ValueError(\"loss_date cannot be after reported_date\")\n        return self\n\ndef transition(claim: Claim, new: ClaimStatus) -> Claim:\n    if new not in ALLOWED[claim.status]:\n        raise ValueError(f\"{claim.status.value} -> {new.value} is not a permitted transition\")\n    return claim.model_copy(update={\"status\": new})\n\nc = Claim(claim_id=\"CLM-00412345\", policy_number=\"HX-778\", loss_date=\"2026-03-02\",\n          reported_date=\"2026-03-05\", status=\"under_review\", reserve_amount=4200)\nprint(transition(c, ClaimStatus.APPROVED).status.value)   # approved\ntransition(c, ClaimStatus.PAID)   # raises: under_review -> paid is not a permitted transition\n```\n\n- **Field descriptions carry the glossary** into the JSON schema the model sees.\n- **The enum restricts status** to the agreed states.\n- **The validator rejects** an extraction that swaps the loss and report dates.\n- **The reserve is a `Decimal` rather than a `float`** because money is a value object whose arithmetic must\n  be exact; binary floats cannot represent most decimal amounts and accumulate rounding error in totals.\n\n### An aggregate that guards a cross-line invariant\nThe claim is the aggregate root, so the only way to pay a coverage line is through it. Each payment would\npass a per-line check; only the root sees the total. Pure Python, amounts illustrative.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom decimal import Decimal\n\n@dataclass\nclass CoverageLine:\n    line_id: str\n    paid: Decimal = Decimal(\"0\")\n\n@dataclass\nclass ClaimAggregate:                       # the root: every change goes through it\n    claim_id: str\n    approved_amount: Decimal\n    lines: dict[str, CoverageLine] = field(default_factory=dict)\n\n    def total_paid(self) -> Decimal:\n        return sum((l.paid for l in self.lines.values()), Decimal(\"0\"))\n\n    def add_payment(self, line_id: str, amount: Decimal) -> None:\n        if amount <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"payment must be positive\")\n        if self.total_paid() + amount > self.approved_amount:   # cross-line invariant\n            raise ValueError(f\"would pay {self.total_paid() + amount}, approved {self.approved_amount}\")\n        self.lines[line_id].paid += amount\n\nclaim = ClaimAggregate(\"CLM-00412345\", Decimal(\"5000\"),\n                       {\"building\": CoverageLine(\"building\"), \"contents\": CoverageLine(\"contents\")})\nclaim.add_payment(\"building\", Decimal(\"3000\"))\nclaim.add_payment(\"contents\", Decimal(\"1500\"))\nprint(claim.total_paid())                   # 4500\ntry:\n    claim.add_payment(\"contents\", Decimal(\"800\"))   # each line alone looks fine; the total does not\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(\"rejected:\", e)                   # rejected: would pay 5300, approved 5000\n```\n\n- **Expose `add_payment` as the agent's tool,** not a generic \"update coverage line\" write, so the tool\n  surface cannot express a payment that breaks the total.\n\n### Worked scenario: resolving the claim an email is about\nFive inbound emails, resolved with the cascade from step 2 (illustrative).\n\n| Email contains | Rule that matched | Trust | Action |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| \"CLM-00412345\" | Exact key | High | Link automatically |\n| Policy HX-778 and loss date 2 March | Strong-attribute lookup, one claim found | High | Link automatically |\n| Policy HX-778 only, customer has two open claims | Strong lookup, two candidates | Ambiguous | Ask a person |\n| Name and postcode | Fuzzy match | Low | Link with a review flag |\n| No identifiers | None | None | Ask a person |\n\n- **Storing the matched rule** on each record lets downstream steps, and auditors, treat fuzzy links\n  differently from key links.","patterns":["**Glossary and schema in version control** — keep the glossary, enums and Pydantic models in one module and generate the prompt glossary section and the structured-output schema (`model_json_schema()` in Pydantic v2) from it, so they cannot drift. Review changes with SMEs like any other code change.","**Validate, then reject or escalate** — run every model output through the schema and invariant validators before it is written anywhere; send failures to a review queue rather than retrying silently forever. Monitor validation-failure rate per field, because a spike usually means a new document layout or vocabulary drift.","**Escape hatch in enums** — while the domain is still being discovered, include an explicit \"other\" or \"needs review\" value in classification enums. Monitor its share; a growing share signals a missing category to add to the model.","**Match provenance field** — store which identity-resolution rule linked each extracted record (key, strong lookup, fuzzy, human). Monitor the fuzzy share and audit a sample of fuzzy links regularly.","**Aggregate-shaped agent tools** — give agents tools that act on aggregate roots and enforce invariants inside the tool, never raw row-level writes. Log rejected tool calls; they show where the agent's plan disagrees with the business rules.","**Record walkthroughs as eval cases** — every SME correction from a walkthrough becomes a labelled evaluation case, so the model's edge cases accumulate in the test set. Track remaining corrections per walkthrough as the model's maturity measure."],"diagram":{"code":"stateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> FNOL: loss reported\n    FNOL --> UnderReview: adjuster assigned\n    UnderReview --> Approved: cover confirmed\n    UnderReview --> Denied: not covered\n    Approved --> Paid: payment issued\n    Denied --> [*]\n    Paid --> [*]","caption":"The claim lifecycle from the example: only these transitions are permitted, so an agent's attempt to pay a\nclaim still under review is rejected in code."},"interview":[{"q":"Why does an LLM system need an explicit domain model when the model already understands language?","a":"- **The model knows generic meanings;** the customer's words have specific ones, such as \"claim\" meaning a\n  loss event in one insurer and a coverage line in another.\n- **Mismatches are silent:** fluent, well-formatted output with the report date instead of the loss date\n  passes every format check.\n- **One agreed model** feeds schemas, prompts, retrieval filters and eval labels, so they cannot disagree."},{"q":"Extracted records keep failing to link to the claims system. How do you debug and fix it?","a":"- **Check identity resolution first:** what identifiers do the documents actually contain, and which\n  resolution rule is being attempted?\n- **Apply the cascade:** exact key, then strong-attribute lookup, then fuzzy match, then a person, recording\n  which rule matched.\n- **Measure the share per rule;** a high \"no match\" share often means a key pattern in the schema is wrong\n  or documents use a different identifier."},{"q":"When would you not build a formal domain model?","a":"- **For a narrow, single-team task** such as summarising one document type for internal reading, a short\n  prompt glossary may be enough.\n- **Avoid a knowledge graph** for flat lookups; it pays only when questions routinely traverse several\n  relationships, because keeping it in sync is costly."},{"q":"An agent with a \"pay coverage line\" tool overpaid a claim. What went wrong in the design?","a":"- **The tool acted inside an aggregate.** Each line payment passed its own validation, but the cross-line\n  invariant (total never exceeds approved amount) was never checked.\n- **Fix:** expose `add_payment(claim_id, …)` on the claim root, which checks the total on every change, and\n  log rejected calls."},{"q":"Why store money as `Decimal` and enforce invariants in validators rather than in the prompt?","a":"- **Money must be exact.** Binary floats cannot represent most decimal amounts and accumulate rounding error\n  in totals.\n- **Prompts shift probabilities; validators guarantee.** A rule like \"loss date never after report date\"\n  stated only in the prompt will eventually be violated and found by an auditor."}],"tradeoffs":["**Precision vs SME time.** An explicit model prevents silent vocabulary errors, but takes SME time to agree. When the task is a narrow, single-team summarisation, a short glossary in the prompt may be all you need.","**Reliability vs coverage.** Strict schemas and enums make outputs reliable and testable, but reject legitimate cases the model did not foresee. Add an explicit \"other\" or escalation path when the domain is still being discovered.","**Separate contexts vs translation code.** Bounded contexts keep each definition precise, but require translation code between them. That pays off when departments genuinely disagree, and is overhead when they do not.","**Graph vs maintenance.** A knowledge graph makes relationships queryable and explainable, but is costly to build and keep in sync. It is worth it when questions routinely traverse several relationships, not for flat lookups."],"pitfalls":["**Defining terms from documents alone, without SMEs.** Symptom: a glossary that reads well but gets corrected on almost every example walked through with the people who do the work.","**Letting prompts, schemas and evaluation labels drift apart.** Symptom: a model that passes its own tests while users report it uses terms differently from the reporting dashboards.","**Forcing one meaning across bounded contexts.** Symptom: endless debates over a field definition and outputs that are right for one department and wrong for another.","**Enforcing invariants only through prompt instructions.** Symptom: a rare but recurring output that violates a rule the business considers impossible, discovered by an auditor.","**Giving an agent write tools on individual records inside an aggregate.** Symptom: each update passes its own validation while the combination violates a cross-record rule, such as payments exceeding the approved total.","**Modelling entities without identity resolution.** Symptom: extracted records that name a customer or policy but cannot be linked to the system of record, so downstream steps fail."],"when":"- **Use when** the AI system extracts, classifies or acts on business entities, when several teams or\n  systems share data, and when outputs feed reports or decisions.\n- **Timing:** right after discovery and workflow analysis, before writing prompts or schemas.\n- **Keep it lightweight:** a glossary, key entities, one or two state machines and the invariants.\n- **Measure:** how many SME corrections remain when you walk real records through it, and whether every\n  schema, enum and evaluation label traces back to it.\n- **Interviews:** explaining how one domain model projects into prompts, schemas, retrieval filters and\n  evaluation labels shows architectural maturity.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Encodes the model as typed schemas, enums and validators used for structured outputs, and rejects or escalates model outputs that violate invariants instead of passing them on.","architect":"Owns bounded-context boundaries and the canonical model, deciding where translation happens and whether a knowledge graph or relational schema represents the domain.","fde":"Runs glossary and example-walkthrough sessions with the customer's experts, and catches the terms that mean different things in different departments before they reach a prompt."},"check":{"q":"A retail customer wants an assistant that answers \"how many active customers do we have in each\nregion?\" from their warehouse and documents. Sales, finance and marketing each define \"active\"\ndifferently. How do you handle it?","a":"Treat it as a bounded-context problem, not a prompt problem. Agree each definition with its owning\nteam, give each a distinct name in the glossary (contracted, billed in the last 30 days, engaged in the\nlast 90 days), and implement each as a deterministic metric in the warehouse or semantic layer, not as\nsomething the model computes. The assistant's job becomes mapping a question to the right named metric\nand asking for clarification when \"active\" is ambiguous, citing which definition it used. Evaluation\ncases then include ambiguous phrasings to confirm it asks or chooses by an agreed default."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should invariants such as \"loss date is never after report date\" be enforced in code rather than only stated in the prompt?","options":["Prompts cannot contain dates","Code validation is required by every regulator","A model can be instructed to respect a rule but cannot be guaranteed to, while a validator rejects every violation","Validators make the model generate faster"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Instructions shift probabilities; they do not guarantee behaviour. A deterministic validator catches every violation, so impossible outputs are rejected or escalated instead of silently passed downstream."},{"q":"What distinguishes an entity from a value object in a domain model?","options":["Entities are stored in databases, value objects only in memory","An entity keeps its identity while attributes change; a value object is defined only by its attributes","Entities are nouns and value objects are verbs","Value objects always come from external systems"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A claim remains the same claim when its status changes, so it needs an identifier that extraction must resolve. An address or money amount is equal to any other with the same values and only needs parsing."},{"q":"Two departments use \"order\" to mean different things. What is the most robust modelling choice?","options":["Pick the larger department's definition and use it everywhere","Let the model infer which meaning is intended from context","Model separate bounded contexts with distinct definitions and an explicit translation between them","Remove the term from the glossary to avoid confusion"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Forcing one meaning makes outputs wrong for one department, and inference fails when context is thin. Separate contexts keep each definition precise, and explicit translation makes the mapping testable."}]},{"key":"scoping/data-access-and-permissions-discovery","sources":[],"why":"- **The typical stall is access, not model quality.** It is week six of a pilot, the prototype works on a\n  folder someone emailed over, and the team still waits for a service account, a firewall rule, a\n  data-processing agreement or the data owner's sign-off for production access.\n- **Each blocker has its own approver, queue and lead time,** and none was started on day one because\n  nobody listed them.\n- **Exports silently erase permissions.** Sources enforce rules: a SharePoint site restricted to HR, a CRM\n  record visible only to its account team, a database row filtered by region. Export into a vector index,\n  or connect with a broad service account, and those rules are gone.\n- **The result is a leak with a helpful interface.** The assistant answers anyone's question from\n  everyone's data. The demo cannot reveal this, because the demo user is usually an admin.\n- **\"Get the data first, sort permissions later\" fails twice:** it defers the longest lead times and bakes a\n  permission-free design into the architecture. Discovery runs access and permissions as a first-class\n  workstream, in parallel with the technical spike, on the same access path production will use.","how":"### 1. Derive the data needs from the use case\n- **From the decomposition,** list every input each node needs and every system each action touches.\n- **For each, ask:** is it needed to answer, to act, or only to evaluate?\n- **Fewer sources means fewer approvals and less exposure**, because each source carries its own owner,\n  contract and risk.\n\n### 2. Build a source inventory\n| Field | Why it matters |\n|---|---|\n| System and owner (a named person, not a team) | Someone must feel entitled to approve |\n| Data volume and format | Sizes ingestion and parsing work |\n| Freshness requirement | Decides sync vs live access |\n| Access method (API, connector, database replica, file export, event stream) | Shapes the integration |\n| Authentication method and rate limits | Decides delegated vs service access |\n| Sandbox or non-production copy available? | Lets development start safely |\n\n- **Ask for a sample in week one**, because samples expose format and quality problems that descriptions\n  hide.\n\n### 3. Map the permission model at the source\n- **Find how the source decides who sees what:**\n  - document or item-level ACLs, and inherited folder permissions;\n  - group membership from the identity provider;\n  - record ownership and sharing rules in a CRM;\n  - row-level security in a database;\n  - attribute-based rules (region, clearance, customer).\n- **Note how often permissions change and whether the API exposes them**, because an AI system can only\n  enforce what it can read.\n\n### 4. Classify the data and find the legal constraints\n- **Sensitivity:** public, internal, confidential, regulated personal or health data.\n- **Constraints:** applicable regulation, contractual limits on use (for example, whether data may be sent\n  to a third-party model provider), residency requirements, and retention or legal-hold obligations.\n- **These decide the deployment shape** (provider API, private endpoint, in-customer-VPC), so they must be\n  known before architecture is fixed.\n\n### 5. Choose how permissions are enforced in the AI system\n| Option | How | Strength | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Delegated user access** | Call the source with the end user's own token (for example via an OAuth token-exchange or on-behalf-of flow), so the source enforces its own rules | Most faithful | Slower; bound by per-user rate limits |\n| **Index with ACL metadata** | Ingest content with its permission principals; filter every retrieval by the user's resolved groups | Fast | You must sync permission changes, resolve nested groups and honour explicit deny entries |\n| **Partitioned indexes** | One index per audience (department or tenant) | Simple and robust | Only works when permissions are coarse |\n\n- **Pre-filter, never post-filter.** Apply the ACL filter inside the search, not to the top-k results\n  afterwards, because post-filtering a top-10 list for a user who can see a small share of the corpus often\n  leaves zero results, which looks like a retrieval bug rather than a permission rule.\n- **The choice depends on step 3**, because a source that does not expose ACLs through its API forces\n  delegated access or coarse partitioning.\n\n### 6. Treat derived data as carrying its sources' permissions\n- **Derived data reveals source content:** embeddings, chunk stores, cached answers, conversation memory,\n  summaries, prompt and trace logs, and evaluation datasets.\n- **Visibility rule:** each derived item is visible only to users who could see every source it was built\n  from, because a summary of an HR file and a public memo is as sensitive as the HR file.\n- **List where derived data lands**, including the observability tool and the model provider's logs, and\n  who can read each of those stores.\n\n### 7. Apply least privilege to actions\n- **For agents that write** (create tickets, update records, send email), list each action, the scope it\n  needs, and whether it acts as the user or as a service.\n- **Request read-only scopes where reading suffices**, and gate write actions behind approval.\n\n### 8. Map the approval path and lead times\n- **For each source, list who must approve:** data owner, security, privacy, legal, and the platform team\n  that issues credentials, with their typical turnaround.\n- **Start the slowest first** and track each as a project dependency with an owner and a date.\n\n### 9. Write permission tests early\n- **Test before launch, ideally in the prototype,** that a user without access to a document cannot\n  retrieve, quote or summarise it, including through indirect questions.\n- **Use real test identities with differing access.**","example":"### Pre-filter vs post-filter on an ACL-tagged index\nPure Python stand-in for a vector search (random scores replace similarity). The user can read about 3% of\nchunks, so filtering the top 10 afterwards returns nothing, while filtering inside the search returns ten\nreadable results.\n\n```python\nimport random\n\nrandom.seed(7)\n# 1,000 chunks; each carries the principals allowed to read its source document\ncorpus = [{\"id\": i,\n           \"score\": random.random(),                       # stand-in for similarity to the query\n           \"allowed\": {\"legal-all\"} if i % 33 == 0 else {\"hr-only\"}}\n          for i in range(1000)]\nuser_groups = {\"legal-all\", \"user:asha\"}                   # resolved, including nested groups\n\ndef visible(chunk, groups):\n    return bool(chunk[\"allowed\"] & groups)\n\ndef post_filter(k=10):                                      # search first, then drop what user cannot see\n    top = sorted(corpus, key=lambda c: -c[\"score\"])[:k]\n    return [c[\"id\"] for c in top if visible(c, user_groups)]\n\ndef pre_filter(k=10):                                       # restrict the search to what user can see\n    allowed = [c for c in corpus if visible(c, user_groups)]\n    return [c[\"id\"] for c in sorted(allowed, key=lambda c: -c[\"score\"])[:k]]\n\nshare = sum(visible(c, user_groups) for c in corpus) / len(corpus)\nprint(f\"user can see {share:.0%} of chunks\")\nprint(\"post-filter top-10:\", post_filter())    # [] : looks like a retrieval bug\nprint(\"pre-filter  top-10:\", pre_filter())     # ten readable chunks\n```\n\n- **In a real vector store,** pass the user's resolved groups as a metadata filter on the query itself, so\n  the engine restricts candidates before ranking.\n\n### Worked scenario: an assistant for a legal team\nLead times and counts illustrative. A legal team wants an assistant over contracts in SharePoint, matter\ndata in a practice-management database, and client billing in the ERP. In week one the FDE builds the\ninventory.\n\n| Source | What discovery found | Decision | Because |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| SharePoint contracts | About 40,000 documents; permissions inherited from matter sites, plus item-level exceptions for conflicted matters where named lawyers are walled off | Index with ACL principals, filter by the user's resolved groups at query time; incremental ACL sync; re-check walled-matter documents against SharePoint's live permissions before any text reaches the prompt | Ethical walls make sync freshness a hard requirement, and a sync can still lag |\n| Practice-management database | Reachable only through a nightly replica; row-level visibility is enforced by its application, not the database, so a direct connection would see everything | Read through the application's API with delegated user tokens, not from the replica | The application keeps enforcing its row rules |\n| ERP billing | Classified confidential; the ERP owner says AI use requires a privacy review | Cut from the first phase | The use case answers its target questions without it, and the review would dominate the timeline |\n\n- **Freshness question asked explicitly:** how quickly must permission changes propagate? The incremental\n  sync is designed to meet that answer.\n- **Longest lead time first:** the security approval for the API integration and the service registration\n  is requested in week one.\n- **First test written:** a lawyer walled off from a conflicted matter asks \"summarise the indemnity terms in\n  the Acme agreement\" and must get no content from it.","patterns":["**Permission test suite in CI** — keep test identities with deliberately different access (walled-off, read-only, admin) and run retrieval and \"summarise X\" probes against every index or connector change. Fail the build if any identity retrieves, quotes or summarises a document it cannot open at the source. Track the suite's pass rate per release.","**Incremental ACL sync with lag monitoring** — sync permission changes separately from (and more often than) content, and record the time from a change at the source to its effect in the index. Alert when lag exceeds the propagation requirement agreed with the data owner.","**Live check for the most sensitive sources** — for walled matters, HR or other high-risk content, verify the user's access at the source just before text enters the prompt, accepting the extra latency. Monitor how often the live check denies a chunk the index allowed; non-zero means sync lag or a mapping bug.","**Access-controlled traces** — apply the same visibility rule to prompt and trace logs as to the sources, or redact retrieved text from logs. Review who can read the observability tool and the provider-side logs.","**Access-request tracker as a dependency list** — one row per source with named owner, approver, requested date, expected date and status, reviewed weekly with the sponsor. Monitor days open per request and escalate the slowest.","**Scoped credentials for agents** — read-only scopes by default, write actions behind a human approval step, and acting as the user wherever the source supports it. Log every write with the identity it used."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"User question plus identity\"] --> G[\"Resolve groups, including nested\"]\n    G --> R[\"Retrieve with ACL pre-filter\"]\n    IDX[(\"Index with ACL principals\")] --> R\n    SYNC[\"Incremental ACL sync\"] -.->|\"permission changes\"| IDX\n    R --> W{\"From a walled or sensitive source?\"}\n    W -->|\"yes\"| L[\"Live check at source\"]\n    W -->|\"no\"| P[\"Build prompt\"]\n    L -->|\"allowed\"| P\n    L -->|\"denied\"| X[\"Drop chunk\"]\n    P --> A[\"Answer with citations\"]\n    A --> T[(\"Traces with access control\")]","caption":"A query's path through an ACL-aware assistant: identity is resolved first, the filter is applied inside\nretrieval, and the most sensitive documents get a live check before any text reaches the prompt."},"interview":[{"q":"How would you build RAG over a company's SharePoint so users only see what they are allowed to?","a":"- **Map the source's permission model first:** inherited site permissions, item-level ACLs and\n  identity-provider groups, and whether the API exposes them.\n- **Ingest with ACL principals and pre-filter retrieval** by the user's resolved (including nested) groups,\n  or delegate with the user's token where permissions are too fine or fast-changing.\n- **Carry permissions to derived data** (chunks, caches, traces) and prove it with tests using real test\n  identities."},{"q":"Users with narrow access report the assistant \"finds nothing\" while admins get great answers. What is happening?","a":"- **Likely post-filtering:** the ACL filter is applied to the top-k results after ranking, so a user who can\n  see a small share of the corpus gets zero results from a top-10 list.\n- **Fix:** move the filter inside the search so candidates are restricted before ranking, then re-run the\n  permission tests and a retrieval eval per identity."},{"q":"When would you choose delegated user access over an ACL-filtered index?","a":"- **When permissions change often or must apply immediately,** such as ethical walls or revocations, because\n  the source stays authoritative.\n- **When the source does not expose ACLs through its API,** or enforces visibility in its application layer.\n- **Not when** queries are high-volume and permissions are coarse, where per-user latency and rate limits\n  make an index or partitions the better fit."},{"q":"Which approvals do you start in week one, and why?","a":"- **The slowest ones:** security review of the integration, service or app registration, privacy review for\n  regulated data, and data-owner sign-off, each with a named approver and date.\n- **Because** they run on their own queues, and the access path they approve shapes the architecture;\n  deferring them adds their full lead time after the prototype."},{"q":"Where can restricted content leak even if retrieval is correctly filtered?","a":"- **Derived stores:** cached answers, conversation memory, summaries and evaluation datasets built from\n  restricted sources.\n- **Operational logs:** prompt and trace logs in the observability tool and on the model provider side,\n  readable by developers or vendor support. Apply the same visibility rule or redact."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fidelity vs speed.** Delegated user access keeps the source authoritative for permissions, but adds latency and per-user rate limits. When queries are high-volume and permissions are coarse, an ACL-filtered index is faster.","**Fast retrieval vs sync responsibility.** Indexing content with ACL metadata enables fast filtered retrieval, but makes you responsible for syncing permission changes. When walls or revocations must apply immediately, delegate instead.","**Shorter approvals vs coverage.** Cutting sensitive sources from phase one shortens approvals dramatically, but limits what the system can answer. Add them once the access pattern is proven and the review has had time to run.","**Development speed vs realism.** A non-production copy speeds development, but its permissions and data quality may differ from production. When the use case depends on fine-grained ACLs, test against production permissions early."],"pitfalls":["**Building the prototype on an exported folder.** Symptom: a demo that delights an admin user but answers questions from documents ordinary users were never allowed to see.","**Connecting with a broad service account and promising to \"add permissions later\".** Symptom: an architecture with no place to carry user identity, discovered during security review.","**Ignoring permission-change latency.** Symptom: a revoked or walled-off user still retrieving content for hours or days after the change, until the next full re-index.","**Naming a team instead of a person as data owner.** Symptom: access requests that sit unanswered for weeks because nobody feels entitled to approve them.","**Securing retrieval but not the traces.** Symptom: a developer or vendor support engineer able to read restricted document text in the prompt logs of the observability tool.","**Treating \"the data exists\" as \"the data is usable\".** Symptom: a pipeline that connects successfully and returns scanned images, empty fields or a schema nobody can explain."],"when":"- **Use from the first week** of any engagement that touches customer data, which in enterprise AI is almost\n  all of them, in parallel with the technical spike rather than after it.\n- **Revisit** whenever a new source or action is added.\n- **Non-negotiable for** retrieval over internal documents, agents with write access, and regulated data.\n- **Measure:** every source has a named owner, an access method, a permission model, a classification and\n  an approval with a date, and permission tests exist and pass.\n- **Interviews:** \"how would you build RAG over the company's SharePoint\" is often really a question about\n  this topic.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements identity propagation and ACL filtering on retrieval, and writes automated permission tests with distinct test identities that run on every index change.","architect":"Chooses delegated access, ACL-filtered indexing or partitioning per source, and sets the deployment boundary that the data classification and residency rules require.","fde":"Finds the real data owners, files and chases access requests from week one, and surfaces permission models and lead times that would otherwise stall the pilot."},"check":{"q":"A customer asks for a company-wide assistant over their document management system and offers a\nservice account with read access to everything \"to get started quickly\". How do you respond?","a":"I would accept it only for a sandbox with non-sensitive content, and not as the production design.\nA read-everything account erases the source's permissions, so every user could retrieve anything the\naccount sees. Instead I would find out how the system expresses permissions (item ACLs, inherited site\npermissions, identity-provider groups) and whether its API exposes them, then either ingest with ACL\nprincipals and filter by the user's resolved groups or call the source with delegated user tokens. I\nwould also ask how quickly revocations must propagate, classify which content is sensitive, start the\napprovals now, and write a test proving a user cannot retrieve a document they lack access to."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a prototype built on exported documents risky even if it performs well?","options":["Exported documents are usually lower resolution than the originals","The export drops the source's permissions, so the design never accounts for who may see what","Exports cannot be embedded by most embedding models","Exported documents are always out of date"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Exports and broad service accounts discard ACLs. The prototype then demonstrates answers that ordinary users should never get, and the architecture has no place to carry user identity into retrieval."},{"q":"A source system enforces row-level visibility in its application layer, not in its database. What does that imply for the AI integration?","options":["A read replica of the database is the safest integration point","Row-level rules can be ignored because the database has no restrictions","Reading the database directly bypasses the rules, so access should go through the application's API with user context","The data must be copied into a separate warehouse first"],"answer":2,"explanation":"If the application enforces visibility, the database itself returns every row to any connection. Going through the application's API as the user keeps its rules in force; a direct replica bypasses them."},{"q":"Why start access and approval requests in the first week rather than after the prototype proves value?","options":["Approvers prefer to see requests before any code exists","Early requests are cheaper because licences cost less","Approvals often have the longest lead times, and the access path chosen shapes the architecture","The prototype cannot run without production credentials"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Security, privacy and owner sign-offs run on their own queues. Deferring them adds their full lead time after the prototype, and the access method (delegated, ACL index, partitioned) changes the design."}]},{"key":"scoping/success-metrics-kpis","sources":["Kohavi, Tang, Xu, 2020 — Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments"],"why":"- **AI projects fail at the review meeting, not in the middle.** The demo impressed, the pilot ran six\n  weeks, then the sponsor asks \"so, did it work?\" and nobody had agreed what \"work\" meant. The project\n  gets judged on anecdote, perhaps the one embarrassing answer someone screenshotted.\n- **Engineers and sponsors bring different numbers.** Engineers bring 94% answer correctness on a golden\n  set and p95 latency of 3 seconds. Sponsors need cost per resolved case, cycle time, revenue or\n  regulatory exposure. Nothing connects the two sets unless you build the link.\n- **\"We'll know it when we see it\" moves the goalposts.** Recency bias decides the verdict: once accuracy\n  is good the question becomes adoption, then cost.\n- **One metric invites Goodhart's law** (a measure that becomes a target stops being a good measure). A\n  support bot's deflection rate climbs fastest when reaching a human gets hard. Model-only metrics leave\n  you defenceless at budget time, because nobody can translate \"faithfulness 0.91\" into money.\n- **Metrics chosen after deployment cannot be falsified,** because people pick whichever one looks good.\n  Defining them up front turns the project into a testable claim instead of a story.","how":"### Build a metric tree, not a list\nArrange metrics in levels connected by a stated causal hypothesis:\n\n| Level | Cadence | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. **Outcome KPI** (lagging) | monthly or quarterly | cost per resolved ticket, claim cycle time, days sales outstanding, analyst hours per report |\n| 2. **Operational drivers** (leading) | weekly | share of cases completed without a human, handle time on AI-assisted cases, adoption rate, edit rate on drafts |\n| 3. **System quality** | daily, from evals and logs | task completion rate, extraction field accuracy, retrieval recall, escalation precision, p95 latency, cost per successful task |\n| 4. **Guardrails** (counter-metrics) | with every report | customer satisfaction, reopen rate, compliance errors, misrouted high-severity cases |\n\n- **Keep one outcome KPI, or at most two.** It is what the sponsor reports upward; several outcome KPIs\n  dilute accountability.\n- **Adoption rate means use on eligible work:** the share of eligible work where the tool was actually\n  used, not logins.\n- **Guardrails must not get worse** while the others improve.\n- **Why a tree:** outcome KPIs move slowly and are confounded by seasonality, staffing and pricing\n  changes, so you cannot steer week to week by them. Leading indicators predict them.\n- **Why write the links down:** failures become diagnosable. If level 3 improves but level 1 does not,\n  one specific link in the hypothesis is wrong, and you can see which one.\n\n### Write a spec for every metric\nEach metric gets:\n1. Name, plus the exact numerator and denominator.\n2. Population: which cases, with what exclusions.\n3. Data source and who owns that pipeline.\n4. Measurement window.\n5. Baseline value and how it was measured.\n6. Target and a named owner.\n\n- **This is tedious because it is the part that gets disputed.** In three departments \"resolved\" can mean\n  three things: closed by an agent, no customer reply for 72 hours, or not reopened within 7 days.\n- **The denominator matters just as much.** Drop \"out-of-scope\" tickets after the fact and the automation\n  rate inflates silently.\n\n### Turn targets into decision rules\n- **A target needs a reference point, a horizon and a consequence.** For example: \"median cycle time falls\n  from the measured baseline of X to at most Y within 90 days of pilot start. If it does, we expand to\n  region B. If it improves by less than Z, we stop.\"\n- **Without a pre-agreed decision rule the target is decoration,** and the review turns into a negotiation.\n\n### Pair every target with its cheapest way to cheat\nFor each optimised metric, name the cheap way to game it and add a guardrail that would catch it:\n\n| Optimised metric | Guardrail |\n|---|---|\n| Deflection | reopen rate and escalations within 24 hours |\n| Handle time | QA score |\n| Auto-approval rate | error rate on auto-approved items, audited by sampling |\n\n- **This does not assume bad faith.** Optimisation pressure, including the team's own prompt iteration\n  against the metric, finds the cheap path by default.\n\n### Check the target is attributable and detectable\nA target is only useful if the pilot can tell whether it was hit. That takes two decisions made now, not\nat the review.\n- **Attribution: what would the KPI have done without the system?** Outcome KPIs drift with seasonality,\n  staffing and pricing, so agree the comparison up front: a concurrent holdout group, a staggered rollout,\n  or at minimum a before/after read against a comparison queue (the baseline lesson covers these designs).\n- **Detectability: is the pilot big enough?** A rule of thumb from online experimentation, for about 80%\n  power at a two-sided 5% significance level:\n\n```\nn ≈ 16σ²/δ² cases per group\nδ  = smallest change that matters\nσ² = p(1−p) for a rate\n```\n\n- **Guardrails are where this bites,** because they are rare-event rates. Detecting a misrouting rise from\n  1.5% to 2.5% needs about 16 × 0.015 × 0.985 / 0.01² ≈ 2,400 cases per group.\n- **If the pilot will not see that volume,** lengthen the window, accept a wider tolerance, or back the\n  guardrail with targeted audits instead of the raw rate.","example":"### Worked scenario: insurer FNOL extraction (illustrative numbers)\nAn insurer wants an assistant that reads first-notice-of-loss (FNOL) emails, extracts policy number, loss\ndate, loss type and description, and pre-fills the claim record for an adjuster to confirm.\n\n| Level | Metric | Baseline / target |\n|---|---|---|\n| Outcome KPI | median hours from FNOL receipt to claim set up | baseline over 8 weeks: 26 hours; target at most 8 hours, 90 days after pilot start |\n| Driver | share of FNOLs with every mandatory field pre-filled and accepted without edits | target 70% |\n| System quality | field-level accuracy on a 400-email golden set, measured on every prompt or model change | at least 99% for policy number, at least 90% for loss-type category |\n| Guardrails | misrouting rate (claim sent to the wrong line of business); claims set up against the wrong policy number | misrouting at or below the 1.5% baseline; zero wrong-policy claims, with weekly audits |\n\nWhat week 3 showed:\n1. **Driver reads 78%,** ahead of target; cycle time has fallen to 11 hours.\n2. **Guardrail shows misrouting at 3.1%.** Is that noise? With about 700 FNOLs that week (illustrative),\n   3.1% is roughly 22 misroutes against about 10 expected at the 1.5% baseline. A count of 10 varies by\n   only about ±3 week to week, so this is a real shift, not a bad draw.\n3. **Drill-down:** adjusters on the busiest queue accept pre-filled loss types without reading them, and\n   the extractor confuses \"escape of water\" with \"flood\" in one region's phrasing.\n4. **Fix:** disable auto-accept for the water categories, add 40 region-specific emails to the golden\n   set, re-run the eval.\n\nWithout the guardrail the dashboard would have said \"success\" while claims went to the wrong handlers.\nThe tree told them which link broke: the driver rose for the wrong reason.\n\n### Guardrail calculator: sample size and \"is this shift real?\"\nPure Python; reproduces the numbers above.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef n_per_group(p_base, p_alt):\n    \"\"\"Rule of thumb n = 16 sigma^2 / delta^2: ~80% power, two-sided 5% level.\"\"\"\n    sigma2 = p_base * (1 - p_base)\n    delta = abs(p_alt - p_base)\n    return math.ceil(16 * sigma2 / delta ** 2)\n\ndef shift_z(observed, n, p_base):\n    \"\"\"How many standard deviations a weekly count sits above its baseline.\"\"\"\n    expected = n * p_base\n    sd = math.sqrt(n * p_base * (1 - p_base))\n    return expected, sd, (observed - expected) / sd\n\nprint(n_per_group(0.015, 0.025))   # 2364 -> about 2,400 cases per group\nprint(shift_z(22, 700, 0.015))     # (10.5, ~3.2, ~3.6): a real shift, not noise\n```\n\n- **Read the second line:** 22 observed against 10.5 expected is about 3.6 standard deviations, well past\n  the usual 2 to 3 used as an alert line.","patterns":["**Metric spec as a reviewed file.** When the pilot starts, keep each metric's numerator, denominator, population, source, window, baseline, target and owner in a version-controlled file that the sponsor signs off. Monitor for unreviewed edits to definitions, because a changed denominator silently moves the number.","**Join keys from day one.** Log a task ID on every model event that also appears on the business record (ticket, claim), so drivers and outcomes are computed by a join, not by hand. Monitor the share of business records with no matching task ID.","**Guardrail-paired dashboards.** Put each optimised metric next to its counter-metric on the same panel, for example deflection beside 7-day reopen rate. Alert when the guardrail breaches its threshold over a confidence interval rather than a single week's reading.","**Version-tagged quality metrics.** Tag every eval run and production log with the model and prompt version, so a shift in level 3 can be attributed to a specific change. Monitor quality per version after each deploy.","**Steering meeting from the tree.** Use the tree as the agenda: outcome, drivers, quality, guardrails, then the decision rule. Track which causal link is under investigation and who owns it."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"System quality: daily evals and logs\"] -->|\"hypothesis: better quality\"| D[\"Drivers: adoption, automation share\"]\n    D -->|\"hypothesis: more use\"| O[\"Outcome KPI: cost or cycle time\"]\n    G[\"Guardrails: CSAT, reopens, misroutes\"] -.->|\"must not worsen\"| D\n    O --> R{\"Decision rule at review\"}\n    G --> R\n    R -->|\"target met, guardrails hold\"| E[\"Expand\"]\n    R -->|\"below threshold Z\"| S[\"Stop or rescope\"]","caption":"Read bottom-up: system quality should move the drivers, which should move the outcome, while guardrails\ncatch the metric being gamed. Each arrow is a causal hypothesis you can test."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a success metric and a KPI, and why build a tree rather than a list?","a":"- **Definition:** success metrics are every number the customer uses to judge the project; KPIs are the\n  business outcomes among them, such as cost per case or cycle time.\n- **Why a tree:** outcomes lag and are confounded, so you steer by leading drivers and daily quality\n  metrics, and the stated causal links show which assumption failed when the outcome does not move."},{"q":"Your system quality metrics all improved, but the outcome KPI is flat after two months. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Walk the tree link by link.** Check the drivers first: if adoption on eligible work is low, the quality\n  gain never reached the business process.\n- **If drivers moved but the outcome did not,** check attribution against the holdout or comparison queue\n  for seasonality or staffing changes, then question the hypothesis linking that driver to the outcome.\n- **Check definitions,** in case the outcome's denominator or population changed during the pilot."},{"q":"When would you not insist on a hard guardrail that halts rollout on breach?","a":"- **When the guardrail is a rare-event rate and volume is low.** Detecting 1.5% to 2.5% needs about 2,400\n  cases per group, so a small pilot would halt on noise.\n- **Instead,** use a confidence-interval threshold, a longer window, or targeted audits of the risky\n  segment, and reserve hard stops for severe harms such as wrong-policy claims."},{"q":"How would you set the target for an extraction assistant when the customer asks for 99% accuracy?","a":"- **Measure the baseline first.** Audit the human team on the same task; if they are 88% accurate, 99% is\n  a target that makes a sound project look like a failure.\n- **Set per-field targets by consequence,** for example 99% on policy number but 90% on loss-type\n  category, and tie the decision rule to the outcome KPI, not to accuracy alone."},{"q":"How do you stop a deflection or containment metric from being gamed?","a":"- **Name the cheapest cheat:** making handoff harder or ending chats early raises containment.\n- **Pair it with guardrails** that catch exactly that: reopen rate, repeat contacts and escalations within\n  24 hours, shown on the same dashboard panel.\n- **Define the driver strictly,** as no human contact and no repeat contact on the same issue within a\n  window, so contained-but-unresolved chats do not count."}],"tradeoffs":["**Diagnosis vs accountability.** More metrics give better diagnosis but blur accountability. Keep one outcome KPI and three to five drivers, and add more only if a review cannot locate a failure.","**Relevance vs speed.** Outcome KPIs are what sponsors care about, but they lag by weeks and carry confounders. Lean on leading drivers for steering unless the outcome moves fast, as in real-time conversion.","**Precision vs negotiation time.** Tight metric specs prevent disputes but take days of negotiation with data owners. For a throwaway proof of concept, a looser spec is acceptable if it is labelled as such.","**Ambition vs fairness.** Ambitious targets motivate, but they make a sound project look like a failure. Set targets from a measured baseline and the realistic human error rate, not from vendor marketing.","**Protection vs false alarms.** Hard guardrails (a breach stops rollout) protect users but can halt a sound launch over noise. Define the breach threshold with a confidence interval, not a single week's reading."],"pitfalls":["**Choosing model metrics as the success criteria.** Symptom: a project that hits 95% accuracy and still gets cancelled because nobody could say what it saved. Fix: an outcome KPI at the top of the tree.","**Leaving the denominator undefined.** Symptom: two teams report different automation rates from the same data and the review becomes an argument about definitions. Fix: a written spec per metric.","**Setting targets before measuring the baseline.** Symptom: a demand for 99% accuracy on a task where the human team, once audited, turns out to be 88% accurate. Fix: measure first.","**No guardrail for the optimised metric.** Symptom: the headline number improves while complaints, reopens or downstream rework quietly rise. Fix: pair every target with its cheapest cheat.","**Measuring adoption as logins rather than use on eligible work.** Symptom: high \"active users\" alongside no change in handle time. Fix: define adoption over eligible work items."],"when":"- **Use when:** any customer-facing AI engagement, in the first two weeks and before architecture choices,\n  because the metrics decide what you build. A cycle-time KPI favours automation; a quality KPI favours\n  assistive drafting with review.\n- **Skip the full tree when:** an internal spike's sole question is technical feasibility, and say so in\n  writing.\n- **Day to day:** the tree becomes the pilot dashboard and the agenda of every steering meeting.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"how would you measure success for X?\" A strong answer names the outcome, the\n  drivers, the guardrail that catches gaming, and how the baseline gets measured.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments the system so every driver and quality metric can be computed from logs, with task IDs that join model events to business records.","architect":"Owns the causal hypothesis linking system quality to business outcome, and makes sure the platform can attribute outcomes to a specific model and prompt version.","fde":"Negotiates the metric specs with the customer's operations and finance owners, and gets the decision rule signed before the pilot starts."},"check":{"q":"A customer asks you to \"maximise the share of chats resolved by the bot.\" Why is that a dangerous\nsole KPI, and what would you propose instead?","a":"Bot-resolved share rises if the bot makes escalation hard, ends chats early, or answers\nconfidently when it should hand off, so it rewards exactly the failures that hurt customers. I would\nmake the outcome KPI cost per *successfully* resolved contact, or contacts per customer per month.\nBot resolution share would be one driver, defined as no human contact and no repeat contact on the same\nissue within 7 days. Guardrails would be CSAT on bot-resolved chats, the 7-day repeat-contact rate, and\nthe share of high-severity intents escalated within one turn. I would measure all of these over a\nbaseline window first, and agree in writing what result triggers expansion or a stop."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a metric tree separate operational drivers from the outcome KPI?","options":["Drivers are easier for executives to understand than outcomes","Outcomes lag and are confounded, so leading drivers are needed to steer and to locate which causal link failed","Drivers replace the outcome KPI once the pilot starts","Outcome KPIs cannot be measured for AI systems"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Outcome KPIs such as cost per case move slowly and depend on seasonality and staffing. Drivers move weekly and predict the outcome. Keeping both, linked by a stated hypothesis, shows whether a missed outcome came from low adoption, poor quality, or a wrong assumption."},{"q":"A deflection bot's containment rate rose from 40% to 65% in a month. What should you check first?","options":["Whether the model was upgraded to a larger version","Whether latency improved enough to explain it","Guardrails such as repeat contacts and escalations within a few days, which reveal contained-but-unresolved chats","Whether the golden-set accuracy also rose by 25 points"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Containment is easy to game, even unintentionally, by making handoff harder. The repeat-contact and reopen guardrails show whether contained chats were actually resolved. Accuracy and latency changes do not answer that question."},{"q":"What makes a success target usable at a pilot review?","options":["It is ambitious enough to impress the sponsor","It is expressed as a model metric so engineering can own it","It is chosen after seeing the first month of pilot data","It has a measured baseline, a horizon and a pre-agreed decision such as expand or stop"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A target becomes a decision tool only when it has a reference point, a deadline and an agreed consequence. Targets chosen after seeing data cannot be falsified, and model metrics alone do not show business value."}]},{"key":"scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","sources":["Kohavi, Tang, Xu, 2020 — Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments"],"why":"- **Every impact claim is a difference.** Handle time fell, errors dropped, throughput rose: a difference\n  needs two numbers, and the first must be measured before deployment, because deployment destroys it.\n- **The old process disappears.** Once the assistant is live, agents work differently, the old tool is\n  switched off, the team is reorganised, and history was never logged at the right granularity. A\n  baseline reconstructed at review time is exactly the contested, cherry-picked number the review was\n  meant to avoid.\n- **Management reports are not baselines.** They use their own definitions: they often exclude escalated\n  cases, mix business days with calendar days, or report a target rather than a measurement.\n- **Self-report is unreliable.** People tend to misjudge how long their own work takes and how often they\n  make mistakes, which is why direct measurement beats SME estimates.\n- **Skipping the quality baseline is the most expensive failure.** Customers demand near-perfect AI\n  accuracy while the human process, once checked, errs on a meaningful fraction of cases. Unmeasured, you\n  are held to an imaginary standard and can fail a bar the incumbent never met.","how":"### 1. Start from the metric spec\n- **Baseline exactly the agreed metrics,** with the same numerator, denominator, population and\n  exclusions you will use after launch.\n- **Different definitions make the comparison invalid,** however careful the measurement was.\n\n### 2. Define the unit of work and the window\n- **Pick the unit you will count:** a ticket, a claim, a document, a report.\n- **Choose a window that covers the work's natural cycles:** month-end peaks, weekly patterns, seasonal\n  products. A two-week baseline in a quiet month compared against a peak-season pilot shows a\n  \"regression\" that is really just volume.\n\n### 3. Choose measurement methods per metric\n| Method | Use for | Strength / caveat |\n|---|---|---|\n| **System timestamps** (ticketing, case-management or workflow tool) | cycle time, throughput | cheap and complete, but check what each timestamp actually records |\n| **Time-and-motion or work sampling** | effort the system does not log, such as searching other tools | observational and small-sample, but the only way to see hidden work |\n| **Retrospective quality audit** | human error rate | SMEs blind re-review a random sample of past human outputs against the same rubric the AI will face; the step teams skip, and the one that sets a fair bar |\n| **Shadow labelling** | paired same-case comparison | humans work normally while the system's proposed output is logged but not used |\n\n### 4. Capture distribution, not just averages\n- **Record median, p90 and standard deviation,** and keep the raw per-case data.\n- **Variance sets the sample size:** it determines how many post-launch cases you need to detect a change.\n- **Handle times are usually right-skewed,** so medians and percentiles are more honest than means, and a\n  log transform helps when testing.\n\n### 5. Stratify by case type\n- **Report each metric per segment:** category, channel, region, complexity.\n- **This matters because of mix shift.** If the pilot happens to receive easier cases, the overall number\n  improves even when no segment does. This is a composition (mix) effect; its extreme form, where the\n  aggregate trend runs opposite to every segment's trend, is Simpson's paradox.\n- **Fix:** compare within segments and re-weight to the baseline mix.\n\n### 6. Size the comparison\n```\nproportion to within ±e:            n ≈ z²·p(1−p)/e²\nmean shift δ, std dev σ, two arms:  n ≈ 2(z_α/2 + z_β)²σ²/δ² per arm\n```\n- **If the pilot cannot reach that volume in its window,** you learn it before launch, not at the review.\n\n### 7. Freeze it\n- **Store** the queries, the data snapshot, the definitions, the date range, and the names of the people\n  who signed off.\n- **A baseline that nobody can re-run is just an anecdote with a decimal point.**\n\n### Choosing the after-comparison design\n| Design | What it controls | Weakness |\n|---|---|---|\n| Simple before/after | nothing beyond the baseline | weakest: absorbs seasonality and any concurrent change |\n| Concurrent holdout (teams or queues without the tool) | time effects | withholds the tool from some users |\n| Staggered rollout | both time and team effects | slower to reach everyone |\n| Shadow mode | paired comparisons on identical cases | measures quality, not the behavioural effect on humans |\n\nWith a holdout, the estimate is a difference-in-differences:\n\n```\neffect = (pilot_after − pilot_before) − (holdout_after − holdout_before)\n```\n\n- **The second bracket subtracts whatever happened to everyone** during the pilot: a seasonal dip, a\n  policy change, a new-hire cohort. That is why the holdout needs its own baseline over the same window,\n  not just a post-launch reading.\n- **Parallel-trends assumption:** both groups would have moved in parallel without the tool, so check that\n  their pre-period trends really do look parallel before trusting the number.","example":"### Per-category baseline with an audit interval and pilot sizing\nA support team will pilot an AI drafting assistant. This script computes the per-category baseline from\nan 8-week ticket export, including a confidence interval on the human error rate from an SME audit\nsample, and the pilot volume needed to detect a 2-minute drop in handle time.\n\n```python\nimport csv, math, statistics as st\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef wilson(k, n, z=1.96):\n    \"\"\"95% Wilson score interval for a proportion k/n (well-behaved for small n).\"\"\"\n    p, d = k / n, 1 + z * z / n\n    centre = (p + z * z / (2 * n)) / d\n    half = z * math.sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n + z * z / (4 * n * n)) / d\n    return centre - half, centre + half\n\ndef n_per_arm(sd, delta, z_a=1.96, z_b=0.84):\n    \"\"\"Cases per arm to detect a mean shift of `delta` (two-sided 5%, 80% power).\"\"\"\n    return math.ceil(2 * (z_a + z_b) ** 2 * sd ** 2 / delta ** 2)\n\nby_cat = defaultdict(list)\nwith open(\"tickets_w01_w08.csv\") as f:          # 8-week export, one row per closed ticket\n    for r in csv.DictReader(f):\n        by_cat[r[\"category\"]].append(\n            (float(r[\"handle_min\"]), r[\"audited\"] == \"1\", r[\"audit_error\"] == \"1\"))\n\nfor cat, rows in sorted(by_cat.items()):\n    mins = [m for m, _, _ in rows]\n    errs = [e for _, audited, e in rows if audited]   # SME blind re-review sample\n    lo, hi = wilson(sum(errs), len(errs)) if errs else (float(\"nan\"),) * 2\n    print(f\"{cat:10} n={len(mins):5} median={st.median(mins):5.1f} \"\n          f\"p90={st.quantiles(mins, n=10)[8]:5.1f} sd={st.stdev(mins):5.1f} \"\n          f\"audited={len(errs):4} error_rate_95ci=[{lo:.1%}, {hi:.1%}]\")\n\nprint(n_per_arm(sd=9.0, delta=2.0))   # billing: sd 9 min, detect a 2-minute drop -> 318\n```\n\nRead the output critically:\n- **False precision.** Suppose billing shows 11 errors in 120 audited tickets. The point estimate is 9.2%,\n  but the Wilson interval is roughly 5% to 16%. \"The AI must beat 9.2%\" is therefore false precision.\n- **The honest bar** is \"not worse than the human interval\", and you may need to enlarge the audit before\n  signing it.\n- **Volume check.** The sample-size line says the pilot needs about 318 billing tickets in each arm. If\n  billing only gets 80 tickets a week, a two-week pilot cannot answer the question.\n\n### Worked scenario: difference-in-differences with a holdout (illustrative numbers)\nTwo comparable queues are baselined over the same 8 weeks; one gets the assistant.\n\n| Queue | Median handle time before | After | Change |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Pilot | 14.0 min | 11.0 min | −3.0 min |\n| Holdout | 14.5 min | 13.5 min | −1.0 min |\n| **Effect** | | | (−3.0) − (−1.0) = **−2.0 min** |\n\n- **A naive before/after would claim 3 minutes.** One of those minutes happened to everyone, perhaps a\n  quieter season or a new knowledge-base article.\n- **Check parallel trends first:** if the two queues' weekly medians already diverged during the 8 baseline\n  weeks, the 2-minute estimate is not trustworthy.","patterns":["**Baseline as a versioned job.** Keep the extraction SQL, the date range and the metric definitions in the repository and run them as a scheduled job against the same source the post-launch telemetry uses. Monitor that the job still reproduces the frozen baseline value on the frozen snapshot.","**Blind, stratified audit sampling.** Draw the SME audit sample with a fixed random seed per segment, strip any indication of who produced the output, and reuse the same rubric for AI outputs later. Monitor the interval width per segment and enlarge the sample where it is too wide to set a bar.","**Holdout routing flag.** Route a fixed set of queues or teams to the old flow and tag every case with its arm at creation time, so the difference-in-differences can be computed by a query. Monitor arm balance and any leakage of the tool into the holdout.","**Mix re-weighting in the report.** Report both the raw pilot number and the number re-weighted to the baseline segment mix side by side. Monitor the gap: a large one means mix shift is doing the work.","**Shadow logging before go-live.** Log the system's proposed output next to the human's actual output on the same case without showing it to users. Monitor agreement rate per segment as a quality baseline."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Agreed metric spec\"] --> W[\"Unit of work and window\"]\n    W --> M[\"Measure: timestamps, sampling, audits, shadow\"]\n    M --> X[\"Distribution and per-segment stats\"]\n    X --> N{\"Pilot volume enough?\"}\n    N -->|\"no\"| W\n    N -->|\"yes\"| F[(\"Frozen baseline: queries, snapshot, sign-off\")]\n    F --> L[\"Pilot launch\"]\n    L --> A[\"After-reading, same definitions\"]\n    A --> E[\"Effect via holdout or staggered design\"]","caption":"The baseline is captured and frozen before anyone sees the system; the after-reading reuses the same\ndefinitions and the chosen comparison design."},"interview":[{"q":"Why can't you just reconstruct the baseline from historical reports after the pilot?","a":"- **Deployment destroys the old process:** behaviour shifts, the old tool is switched off and history was\n  rarely logged at the needed granularity.\n- **Reports use other definitions,** such as excluding escalations or mixing business and calendar days,\n  so the \"before\" number becomes contestable exactly when it matters."},{"q":"Your pilot shows handle time down 20%, but the customer's ops lead says it is seasonal. How do you settle it?","a":"- **Compare against a holdout or staggered cohort** with its own baseline over the same window, and compute\n  the difference-in-differences so shared seasonal movement cancels out.\n- **Check parallel pre-period trends** and stratify by category to rule out mix shift.\n- **If there was no holdout,** compare against the same weeks in previous years and be explicit about the\n  weaker evidence."},{"q":"When would you accept a simple before/after comparison instead of a holdout?","a":"- **When the expected effect is far larger than seasonal variation,** so confounders cannot plausibly\n  explain it.\n- **Or when withholding the tool is unacceptable** to the customer; then lengthen the baseline window to\n  cover cycles and document the limitation in the review."},{"q":"How many audited cases do you need before the human error rate can set the bar?","a":"- **Use the proportion formula:** n ≈ z²·p(1−p)/e². With 11 errors in 120 tickets the 95% Wilson interval is\n  roughly 5% to 16%, too wide to set a sharp bar.\n- **Decide the acceptable interval width first,** then size the audit per high-risk segment rather than\n  overall."},{"q":"What is regression to the mean and how does it bite pilot selection?","a":"- **Definition:** an unusually bad period tends to be followed by a more ordinary one, whatever you do.\n- **The trap:** picking the worst-performing team for the pilot guarantees an apparent improvement.\n- **Fix:** choose pilot groups on other grounds, baseline over a longer history, and keep a holdout."}],"tradeoffs":["**Window length vs schedule.** Longer baseline windows capture seasonality and reduce noise but delay the project. Shorten them only when the work has no strong cycles and volume is high enough to reach the needed sample size.","**Audit cost vs a fair bar.** Retrospective quality audits cost SME hours, which the customer often resists. They become non-negotiable when the success criterion compares AI quality to human quality, which is most of the time.","**Causal clarity vs access.** Concurrent holdout groups give the cleanest causal read but withhold the tool from some users. Use a pure before/after comparison only when the expected effect is far larger than seasonal variation.","**Safety vs realism.** Shadow mode gives paired same-case comparisons without risk, but it does not measure how humans change when they actually rely on the output, so it cannot replace a live comparison.","**Fairness vs sample size.** Heavy stratification makes comparisons fair but splits the sample thin. Merge segments whose baselines are statistically indistinguishable."],"pitfalls":["**Using management-report numbers as the baseline.** Symptom: a \"before\" figure nobody can reproduce from raw data, and a dispute at review about what it included.","**Baselining only speed and not quality.** Symptom: the customer holds the AI to a near-perfect accuracy bar the human process was never measured against.","**Ignoring case mix.** Symptom: an overall improvement that disappears when results are broken down by category, because the pilot queue received easier work.","**Changing the metric definition after launch,** for example by excluding a newly \"out of scope\" case type. Symptom: an improvement that appears exactly when the definition changed.","**Announcing the measurement, then using that period as the baseline.** Symptom: unusually good baseline numbers (an observer, or Hawthorne, effect) that regress once observation ends, which makes the AI look worse than it is.","**Picking the pilot team or queue because its recent numbers are the worst.** Symptom: a large \"improvement\" that the holdout, or the same team's longer history, shows would have happened anyway, because an unusually bad period tends to be followed by a more ordinary one (regression to the mean)."],"when":"- **Use when:** any metric in the success spec, before any pilot user sees the system, ideally during\n  discovery while you are already mapping the workflow.\n- **Plan the effort:** with good historical logs the baseline can be largely retrospective and take days.\n  If effort or quality is not logged, plan two to four weeks of sampling and audits, and put that in the\n  statement of work as a customer dependency.\n- **Skip only for:** pure feasibility spikes with no value claim.\n- **Interviews:** a baseline question usually hides inside \"how would you prove the pilot worked?\" The\n  expected answer covers before/after definitions, human error rates, mix shift and sample size.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the extraction queries and audit-sampling scripts, and makes the post-launch telemetry compute metrics with exactly the same definitions as the baseline.","architect":"Chooses the comparison design (holdout, staggered rollout, shadow mode) and makes sure the platform can route and tag cases so that design is possible.","fde":"Gets access to historical exports, schedules SME audit time, and gets the customer to sign off on the frozen baseline before the pilot starts."},"check":{"q":"The customer wants the AI to reach 99% accuracy before rollout. You have no baseline for the human\nteam. What do you do, and why?","a":"I would run a blind retrospective audit. SMEs re-review a random, stratified sample of recent\nhuman outputs against the same rubric we will use for the AI. The sample should be large enough that\nthe error-rate interval is narrow enough to set a bar. This changes the conversation from an\naspirational number to a fair comparison. If humans turn out to be at, say, 90% to 94%, a target of \"at\nleast as good as humans, with no regression on high-severity case types\" is defensible. The 99% figure\ncan stay as a stretch goal for low-risk categories. I would also record per-category human rates,\nbecause the AI may beat humans on routine work and lose on rare, complex cases. Those rare cases are\nwhere review should stay mandatory."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must the baseline be measured before deployment rather than reconstructed afterwards?","options":["Historical data is always deleted when new software is installed","Deployment changes the process and people's behaviour, and old logs rarely match the new metric definitions","Statistical tests require the baseline to be collected first chronologically","Regulators require baselines to be dated before a go-live"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Once the system is live, the old process is gone and behaviour has shifted, so it cannot be re-observed. Historical reports usually use different definitions, which makes a reconstructed baseline contestable."},{"q":"A pilot shows a 20% drop in average handle time overall, but no individual ticket category improved. What is the most likely explanation?","options":["The confidence interval is too wide to tell","The model improved only on the largest category","The pilot received a different mix of case types, with more easy cases, than the baseline","Handle time should have been measured with medians instead of means"],"answer":2,"explanation":"If no segment improved but the total did, the weights changed: this is mix shift, the composition effect behind Simpson's paradox. Stratifying the baseline and re-weighting pilot results to the baseline mix exposes it. Medians would not fix a composition change."},{"q":"Why record the standard deviation of a baseline metric, not just its mean?","options":["Executives prefer dashboards that show variability","The standard deviation is needed to compute the ROI","It replaces the need to stratify by case type","It determines how many post-launch cases are needed to detect a given improvement"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Required sample size scales with the variance divided by the square of the effect you want to detect. Without the spread, you cannot tell whether the pilot window has enough volume to distinguish a real change from noise."}]},{"key":"scoping/roi-model","sources":[],"why":"- **Budget holders reason in payback and NPV, not eval scores.** AI projects compete with every other\n  project; if you cannot say what the system is worth, someone else decides, usually with a vendor's slide\n  or a gut feeling.\n- **It protects the customer.** A use case that cannot clear break-even under plausible assumptions should\n  be found in week two, not in month nine.\n- **The one-line spreadsheet is wrong.** \"Agents spend 12 minutes per ticket, AI saves 50%, 40,000 tickets\n  a month, so we save 240,000 minutes\" fails in four ways:\n  1. **Freed time is not money.** Salaries do not fall when each person saves twenty minutes a day unless\n     hiring is avoided, overtime drops, or the time goes to work that has value.\n  2. **Adoption is not instant.** Real adoption ramps and plateaus below 100%.\n  3. **AI adds recurring costs:** inference per task (including failed and retried tasks), human review of\n     outputs, eval-set upkeep, monitoring, and re-validation when a model version is deprecated.\n  4. **A point estimate hides which assumption the case rests on,** so no one knows what to measure in the\n     pilot to confirm or kill it.\n- **The assumptions that decide AI ROI** (how much freed time becomes real savings, how fast people adopt)\n  are rarely the ones vendors talk about.","how":"### Benefit lines\nBuild each benefit as a product of measurable factors, so every factor traces to a baseline or a pilot\nmeasurement:\n\n| Benefit | Formula | Caveat |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Labour efficiency** | eligible volume × adoption × time saved per used case × loaded cost per minute × capture rate | capture rate should be owned by finance |\n| **Quality / error avoidance** | volume × change in error rate × cost per error (rework, credits, write-offs, penalties) | only counts if the error rate was baselined |\n| **Revenue** | affected volume × change in conversion or speed × value per unit | hardest to attribute; needs a holdout group to defend |\n| **Risk reduction** | change in probability × impact of a loss event | present separately, because finance discounts it heavily |\n\n- **Capture rate** is the share of freed time that turns into cash or redeployed capacity.\n- **It is high** when the team is growing (hires avoided), when there is overtime or contractor spend, or\n  when there is a backlog with a cost.\n- **It is low** when the freed time just becomes slack.\n\n### Cost lines\n- **One-time:** discovery, integration, data preparation, building the eval set (SME hours count), security\n  review, training and change management.\n- **Recurring:** inference cost per task × total tasks attempted, platform or vendor fees, human review\n  minutes per output × loaded cost, engineering upkeep (prompt and eval maintenance, monitoring, incident\n  response), and periodic re-validation when the provider retires a model.\n- **Divide recurring cost by successful tasks, not attempted ones,** because retries and failed attempts\n  consume tokens but produce nothing, and the review time on a bad draft is a cost, not a saving.\n\n### Compare against the next-best alternative\n- **The counterfactual is rarely \"do nothing\".** A macro library, a form redesign, a rules engine or a\n  process change might capture part of the same benefit at a fraction of the cost and risk, and a sharp\n  finance reviewer will ask about it.\n- **State the AI case as incremental value.** If better templates would remove half of the handle time the\n  assistant targets, the AI project's return is computed on the other half, and its build cost is compared\n  with the template project's cost, not with zero.\n\n### Horizon, ramp and summary measures\nModel month by month over a horizon the sponsor uses, often 24 or 36 months, with an adoption ramp from\ncomparable rollouts or the pilot. Report three numbers, because they answer different questions:\n\n| Measure | Definition | Question it answers |\n|---|---|---|\n| **ROI** | (total benefit − total cost) / total cost | is it worth it overall? |\n| **Payback month** | month cumulative net value turns positive | the cash-flow question |\n| **NPV** | net value discounted at the customer's rate | how it compares with other investments |\n\n### Sensitivity and break-even\n1. **Tornado analysis.** Vary one input at a time across its plausible range and rank inputs by how far\n   they swing NPV.\n2. **Break-even.** Solve for the break-even value of the most uncertain input; the model exists to answer\n   this. If break-even needs 2 minutes saved and the pilot measures 4, the case is robust. If it needs 3.8,\n   the pilot must be designed to measure that one number precisely.\n3. **Joint scenario.** One-at-a-time sensitivity assumes inputs move independently, and in AI rollouts they\n   often do not: low minutes saved and low adoption tend to arrive together, because people abandon a tool\n   that saves them little. Run a joint pessimistic scenario, or a simple Monte Carlo over the input ranges,\n   and report the chance that NPV is negative alongside the tornado chart.","example":"### Monthly model with sensitivity and break-even\nA drafting assistant for a 40,000-ticket-per-month support team. All inputs are illustrative. A real\nmodel takes volume and handle time from the baseline, loaded cost and capture rate from finance, and AI\ncost per ticket from a token measurement on real traffic.\n\n```python\nBASE = dict(tickets=40_000, min_saved=4.0, cost_per_min=0.80, capture=0.6,\n            adoption_ramp=[0.2, 0.35, 0.5, 0.6, 0.7], adoption_steady=0.7,\n            ai_cost_per_ticket=0.04, review_min=0.0, platform=6_000, upkeep=10_000,\n            build=180_000)                  # review_min=0: min_saved is measured net of review\n\ndef monthly(p, month):\n    ramp = p[\"adoption_ramp\"][month] if month < len(p[\"adoption_ramp\"]) else 1.0\n    a = min(ramp, p[\"adoption_steady\"])          # the plateau caps the ramp\n    benefit = p[\"tickets\"] * a * (p[\"min_saved\"] - p[\"review_min\"]) * p[\"cost_per_min\"] * p[\"capture\"]\n    run = p[\"tickets\"] * p[\"ai_cost_per_ticket\"] + p[\"platform\"] + p[\"upkeep\"]  # drafts for every ticket\n    return benefit, run\n\ndef evaluate(p, months=24, rate=0.10):\n    cum, payback, npv = -p[\"build\"], None, -p[\"build\"]\n    for m in range(months):\n        b, r = monthly(p, m)\n        cum += b - r\n        npv += (b - r) / (1 + rate / 12) ** (m + 1)\n        if payback is None and cum >= 0:\n            payback = m + 1\n    benefit = sum(monthly(p, m)[0] for m in range(months))\n    cost = p[\"build\"] + sum(monthly(p, m)[1] for m in range(months))\n    return dict(roi=(benefit - cost) / cost, payback=payback, npv=round(npv))\n\nprint(\"base\", evaluate(BASE))                      # ROI ~ +100%, payback month 8, NPV ~ 517k\nfor k, v in [(\"capture\", 0.3), (\"adoption_steady\", 0.4), (\"min_saved\", 2.5), (\"upkeep\", 20_000)]:\n    print(k, v, evaluate({**BASE, k: v}))\n# capture 0.3 -> ROI ~ 0, no payback in 24 months; adoption 0.4 -> +19%, month 16\n# min_saved 2.5 -> +25%, month 15;               upkeep 20k -> +43%, month 11\n\nlo, hi = 0.0, 10.0                                 # break-even on minutes saved\nfor _ in range(40):\n    mid = (lo + hi) / 2\n    lo, hi = (mid, hi) if evaluate({**BASE, \"min_saved\": mid})[\"roi\"] < 0 else (lo, mid)\nprint(f\"break-even minutes saved: {hi:.2f}\")       # ~2.0\n```\n\nWhat the run tells you:\n\n| Finding | Implication |\n|---|---|\n| Base case: ROI about +100%, payback month 8 | looks strong |\n| Halving capture rate (0.6 to 0.3) wipes out the return entirely | the most valuable scoping conversation is with finance about what happens to freed time, not with engineering about tokens |\n| Doubling upkeep roughly halves the return but leaves it clearly positive | engineering cost matters less than capture |\n| Break-even at about 2 minutes saved | the pilot must measure minutes saved precisely; the case survives anything above that |\n| Joint mild pessimism (capture 0.45, adoption 0.5, 3 minutes saved, each milder than its single-input stress) | ROI of about −17% and no payback within 24 months |\n\nThree modest disappointments together sink a case that survives each one alone, so the one-at-a-time view\nflatters the case.\n\n### Monte Carlo with correlated inputs\nContinues the script above (reuses `BASE` and `evaluate`). A shared \"usefulness\" factor drives both minutes\nsaved and adoption, because people abandon a tool that saves them little. Ranges are illustrative.\n\n```python\nimport random\n\ndef draw(rng):\n    u = rng.random()                                  # shared \"how useful is the tool\" factor\n    return {**BASE,\n            \"min_saved\": 2.5 + 2.5 * u,               # 2.5 to 5.0 minutes\n            \"adoption_steady\": 0.4 + 0.4 * (0.7 * u + 0.3 * rng.random()),  # tracks usefulness\n            \"capture\": rng.uniform(0.3, 0.8),         # finance's range, independent\n            \"upkeep\": rng.uniform(8_000, 20_000)}\n\nrng = random.Random(7)\nruns = [evaluate(draw(rng)) for _ in range(5_000)]\nnpvs = sorted(r[\"npv\"] for r in runs)\nprint(f\"P(NPV < 0) = {sum(n < 0 for n in npvs) / len(npvs):.0%}\")   # about 36% with these ranges\nprint(f\"NPV p10 / p50 / p90 = {npvs[500]:,} / {npvs[2500]:,} / {npvs[4500]:,}\")\n```\n\n- **Report the probability, not just the base case.** A base-case ROI near +100% still carries roughly a\n  one-in-three chance of negative NPV once the inputs are allowed to disappoint together.","patterns":["**Finance-owned assumption sheet.** Keep capture rate, loaded cost per minute and discount rate in a sheet the customer's finance owner signs, and have the model read from it. Monitor for assumption edits between reviews, each with a named approver.","**Cost per successful task from traces.** Compute recurring AI cost as total inference, retry and review spend divided by successful tasks, from production traces tagged by task ID. Monitor it weekly against the modelled value, because retries and discarded drafts drift it upward.","**Re-run the model at each gate.** Refresh inputs with baseline numbers, then pilot measurements, and re-run before every expansion decision. Monitor the gap between measured and break-even values of the top sensitivity input.","**Adoption curve from usage logs.** Replace the assumed ramp with measured use on eligible work per month. Monitor the plateau level, since a payback month that keeps slipping usually means the plateau was overestimated.","**Lead with the conservative case.** Present the conservative scenario first, with the range and the probability of negative NPV beside it. Track which assumption reviewers challenge most and measure it."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    B[\"Baseline: volume, handle time, errors\"] --> BEN[\"Benefit lines\"]\n    F[\"Finance: loaded cost, capture rate\"] --> BEN\n    P[\"Pilot: minutes saved, adoption\"] --> BEN\n    T[\"Traces: cost per successful task\"] --> C[\"Cost lines: one-time and recurring\"]\n    BEN --> M[\"Monthly model over 24-36 months\"]\n    C --> M\n    M --> R[\"ROI, payback month, NPV\"]\n    M --> S[\"Tornado and joint scenarios\"]\n    S --> BE[\"Break-even of riskiest input\"]\n    BE -->|\"design pilot to measure it\"| P","caption":"Benefits and costs both trace to measured inputs; the sensitivity loop finds the one input the pilot must\nmeasure."},"interview":[{"q":"A vendor says their tool saves 50% of agent time. How would you turn that into a value estimate?","a":"- **Start from the baseline,** not the claim: eligible volume and measured handle time.\n- **Multiply through the real factors:** realistic adoption plateau, minutes saved net of review, loaded\n  cost and a finance-owned capture rate.\n- **Subtract recurring cost per successful task,** including retries, then report ROI, payback and NPV with\n  a break-even on the riskiest input."},{"q":"Why is capture rate often the most sensitive input in an AI ROI model?","a":"- **Definition:** it is the share of freed time that becomes cash or valuable redeployed capacity.\n- **Why it swings the case:** in the worked example, halving it from 0.6 to 0.3 takes ROI from about +100%\n  to about zero, while doubling upkeep only roughly halves the return.\n- **It depends on the business,** not the model: hiring plans, overtime and costly backlogs."},{"q":"When is one-at-a-time sensitivity analysis misleading?","a":"- **When inputs are correlated.** Low minutes saved and low adoption tend to arrive together, because\n  people abandon a tool that saves them little.\n- **Then mild joint pessimism can sink a case** that survives each single stress, as in the example where\n  three modest misses give about −17% ROI.\n- **Fix:** add a joint scenario or a Monte Carlo and report the probability that NPV is negative."},{"q":"Six months after launch, actual AI spend is well above the model. Where do you look?","a":"- **Unit of cost:** whether inference was priced per successful task instead of per attempt, missing\n  retries, failed runs and unused drafts.\n- **Volume:** drafts generated for every ticket rather than only adopted cases, and growth in eligible\n  volume.\n- **Upkeep:** eval maintenance, monitoring and re-validation after a provider retired a model version."},{"q":"How do you handle a finance reviewer who asks why you did not just fix the templates?","a":"- **Cost the next-best alternative.** Estimate how much of the targeted handle time a template or process\n  change removes, and at what cost.\n- **Restate the AI case as incremental value** on the remainder, with its build cost compared against the\n  template project's cost, not against zero."}],"tradeoffs":["**Honesty vs simplicity.** Ranges and sensitivity analysis are more honest than a point estimate, but sponsors often want one number. Lead with the conservative case, and show the range alongside it.","**Headline vs attribution.** Including risk-reduction and revenue benefits raises the headline, but those lines are hard to attribute. Keep them separate unless a holdout group can measure them.","**Long horizon vs stability.** A longer horizon makes payback look easier, but it assumes model costs, prices and the workflow stay stable. Use 24 months or less when the provider or the process is likely to change.","**Detail vs speed.** A detailed bottom-up model is defensible but slow to build. For an early go/no-go, a 10-line version with honest ranges is enough, provided the break-even input is identified.","**Clean story vs overstatement.** Counting capture rate at 100% simplifies the story but overstates value. It is only justified when the team is actively hiring or paying for overtime the tool will remove."],"pitfalls":["**Treating time saved as cash saved.** Symptom: a CFO asks which budget line goes down, and nobody can answer. Fix: a finance-owned capture rate.","**Pricing inference on successful tasks only.** Symptom: actual monthly spend runs well above the model, because retries, failed runs and unused drafts were never counted. Fix: price per attempt, divide by successes.","**Assuming instant, complete adoption.** Symptom: a payback month that slips every quarter while usage sits on a plateau far below the modelled figure. Fix: a measured ramp and plateau.","**Forgetting the human review cost.** Symptom: \"time saved\" shrinks in the pilot because reviewers spend minutes checking each output. Fix: a review-minutes line, or measure savings net of review.","**Comparing the AI case against doing nothing.** Symptom: a finance reviewer asks why a cheaper process or template fix was not costed, and the model has no answer. Fix: cost the next-best alternative.","**Building the model before the baseline exists.** Symptom: ROI inputs that the customer's own data contradicts when the pilot report is reviewed. Fix: baseline first."],"when":"- **Use when:** scoping, as soon as the success metrics are agreed. Use it to kill or prioritise use cases\n  before anyone writes integration code.\n- **Refine:** with baseline numbers, then with pilot measurements, and re-run it before any expansion\n  decision.\n- **Skip only for:** exploratory work explicitly funded as learning.\n- **Measure:** the most useful outputs are the break-even value and the ranked sensitivity list, because\n  they tell you which number the pilot must measure.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"this use case saves 50% of time, what is it worth?\" The strong answer asks about\n  capture rate, adoption, review cost and cost per successful task before multiplying.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Measures real cost per successful task from traces, including retries and failures, and supplies the inference and upkeep numbers the model needs.","architect":"Owns the cost structure behind the model: model tier, caching, review design and hosting choices, which set recurring cost and therefore break-even.","fde":"Builds the model with the customer's finance and operations owners, so capture rate and loaded cost are their numbers, which makes the result credible internally."},"check":{"q":"A pilot shows a drafting tool saves 5 minutes per ticket, but the sponsor's finance team rejects\nthe ROI case. What are the likely reasons, and how do you fix the model?","a":"Most likely the model counted all freed time as cash. If the team is not shrinking or avoiding\nhires, those minutes do not reduce any budget line, so the capture rate is far below 100%. Other\nlikely gaps: the 5 minutes was measured on the cases where the tool was used, not across all eligible\nwork at realistic adoption; review time on bad drafts was not subtracted; and inference was priced per\nsuccessful draft instead of per attempt. I would rebuild with finance's own capture assumption, which\nmight be avoided hiring next year, overtime or backlog cost. I would use the measured adoption curve\nand full per-attempt cost, then show the break-even capture rate so they can judge whether it is plausible."},"quiz":[{"q":"In an AI labour-efficiency benefit, what does the capture rate represent?","options":["The share of tickets the model handles without a human","The share of freed employee time that turns into real savings or valuable redeployed capacity","The fraction of model outputs that pass the eval suite","The share of users who adopt the tool after training"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Saving minutes reduces cost only if hiring, overtime or contractor spend falls, or if the time goes to valuable work. Capture rate encodes that, and it often swings ROI more than accuracy does. Adoption and automation rate are separate factors."},{"q":"Why should recurring AI cost be divided by successful tasks rather than attempted tasks?","options":["Providers only bill for successful completions","Attempted tasks are hard to count in logs","Failed attempts and retries consume tokens and review time but deliver no value, so they belong in the cost of each success","It makes the cost per task look smaller to the sponsor"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cost per successful task is the true unit cost. Failures, retries and drafts that get discarded are paid for but produce nothing. Dividing by attempts understates the cost of the value you actually deliver."},{"q":"A sensitivity analysis shows NPV swings most with minutes saved per case. What should the scoping team do?","options":["Raise the assumed minutes saved to give the case more margin","Drop the sensitivity analysis and present the base case","Shorten the horizon so the assumption matters less","Compute break-even minutes saved and design the pilot to measure that input precisely"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The highest-sensitivity input decides the investment, so the pilot should measure it against the break-even value. Raising the assumption hides the risk. Shortening the horizon changes the question without reducing the uncertainty."}]},{"key":"scoping/statement-of-work","sources":[],"why":"- **Two parties start with different finish lines.** The customer imagines \"an assistant that answers our\n  policy questions correctly.\" The vendor or internal team imagines \"a RAG prototype over the documents you\n  give us.\" The gap stays invisible until the delivery date, when it becomes a dispute about money and\n  reputation. The SOW makes the pictures match in writing while changing them is still cheap.\n- **\"Answer questions correctly\" cannot be accepted or rejected,** because any probabilistic system answers\n  some questions wrongly, so the customer can always find a failure.\n- **Undated dependencies become your missed milestone.** \"Customer will provide data\" with no date or format\n  turns a three-week data delay into the delivery team's slip.\n- **Fixed scope and price on an unmeasured quality level** transfers unpriced research risk to whoever\n  signed it.\n- **Ownership and operation questions arrive at the worst moment** when nothing says who owns the eval set,\n  prompts and fine-tuned weights, or who operates the system after go-live. The SOW settles these before\n  work starts, while each side can still walk away.","how":"### Where it sits\n- **MSA holds the legal terms:** liability, confidentiality, IP defaults and payment, in a master services\n  agreement (MSA).\n- **Each project gets an SOW under it** with the specifics. An order-of-precedence clause says which\n  document wins when they conflict.\n- **Internal projects** often have a charter or project brief that does the same job; the structure below\n  applies to both.\n- **Caveat:** the legal terms are for counsel, and this lesson is not legal advice.\n\n### Core sections\n1. **Objective and success metrics.** The business problem and the agreed metric tree, so every deliverable\n   can be traced to a purpose.\n2. **Scope and deliverables.** Concrete artefacts: a deployed service in a named environment, an eval\n   report, a runbook, a handover session. Name the use cases, document types, languages and channels in\n   scope.\n3. **Out of scope.** Explicit, because scope disputes are almost always about what was *assumed* to be\n   included: other languages, write-back to the CRM, mobile, 24/7 support.\n4. **Acceptance criteria.** Each deliverable has an observable test, and for model behaviour it is\n   statistical: \"On the agreed held-out eval set of N cases, field accuracy is at least X% overall and at\n   least Y% on each named critical field, with no critical-severity failures, measured by the agreed\n   harness.\"\n   - Name who runs the test, how long the customer has to accept or reject, and what happens if they do\n     not respond, for example deemed acceptance after 10 business days.\n5. **Customer responsibilities.** Each a dated obligation: data extract in a specified format by a date,\n   SSO and network access by a date, SME hours per week for labelling and review, security review\n   turnaround. State the consequence when one slips: the timeline moves day for day.\n6. **Assumptions.** Facts the plan rests on, such as volumes, document formats, API availability and model\n   provider access. Each one, if false, triggers change control.\n7. **Timeline and milestones,** ideally phase-gated: discovery, then proof of concept, then pilot, then\n   production. Each gate has exit criteria and a go/no-go decision.\n8. **Change control.** How a change is requested, estimated, approved and priced. Without it, small\n   additions accumulate unpaid.\n9. **Roles, governance and support boundaries.** Who decides, the steering cadence, the escalation path, and\n   who operates, monitors and pays for inference after go-live.\n10. **Commercials.** Fixed fee, time and materials, or milestone-based payment, tied to the gates.\n\n### AI-specific clauses\n- **Eval set ownership and freeze.** Agree who builds the acceptance set (ideally jointly with the\n  customer's SMEs), that it is representative, and that it is frozen before tuning, so acceptance does not\n  chase a moving target.\n- **Model and provider changes.** State what happens if the provider deprecates the model or changes\n  pricing mid-project, and that re-validation counts as a change.\n- **Phase-based commitment.** Commit to quality targets only after a discovery or PoC phase has measured\n  feasibility on real data. Before that, commit to effort and learning, not outcomes.\n- **Sampling noise in thresholds.** A rate measured on N cases has an error bar, so say whether the\n  threshold applies to the point estimate or to a confidence bound, and size N to match.\n- **Zero-tolerance lines need the same care.** Zero failures in N cases shows, at 95% confidence, only that\n  the true rate is below about 3/N (the \"rule of three\"). If a failure type truly must never happen, also\n  require a deterministic control, such as a validator or a hard filter, not just a clean eval run.\n- **Post-acceptance performance.** Acceptance certifies behaviour on a frozen set at one date, and\n  production inputs drift afterwards. State the warranty period, who monitors which metrics after go-live,\n  the threshold that triggers remediation, and whether remediation is in scope or a change.\n- **Data handling.** Where customer data goes, retention, use (or no use) for training, and deletion at the\n  end. This often references the data processing agreement.","example":"### Rewriting a weak clause for an invoice-extraction pilot\n\n```text\nWEAK\n  4.1 Vendor will deliver an AI system that accurately extracts invoice data.\n  5.1 Customer will provide sample invoices.\n\nSTRONG\n  4.1 Deliverable D2: extraction service deployed in Customer's staging tenant, writing to the\n      AP staging table defined in Appendix B.\n  4.2 Acceptance of D2: on the frozen acceptance set (600 invoices, stratified by the 12\n      highest-volume suppliers plus 100 long-tail invoices, labelled per Appendix C), measured\n      with the harness in Appendix D:\n        - invoice total, currency, supplier ID: >= 98% exact match each\n        - line items: >= 90% F1 at line level\n        - zero cases of a total written with the wrong currency\n      Customer has 10 business days to accept or list failing cases; silence = acceptance.\n  5.1 Customer provides 2,000 historical invoices with posted AP records (PDF + CSV export)\n      by 3 March; SSO access to staging by 10 March; 4 SME hours/week from the AP team.\n  5.2 Each business day of delay in 5.1 moves milestones M2-M4 by one business day.\n  6.3 Out of scope: handwritten invoices, non-Latin scripts, posting to the ERP ledger.\n```\n\nAll thresholds and dates here are illustrative. What the strong version achieves:\n\n| Feature | Why it matters |\n|---|---|\n| Acceptance is a computation anyone can re-run | not an opinion at a meeting |\n| Sample stratified by supplier, plus long-tail | long-tail suppliers, where extraction usually breaks, cannot be quietly excluded |\n| Separate zero-tolerance line | the one catastrophic error type is not hidden in the averages |\n| Honest resolution | on 600 invoices, 98% allows at most 12 misses per field; a clean currency run bounds the true wrong-currency rate below about 0.5% (3/600), not at zero |\n| Deterministic backstop | a check that the extracted currency is one the vendor master allows for that supplier is what makes \"zero\" credible |\n| Dated dependency with a consequence | a late extract moves the schedule instead of eating the team's time |\n| Explicit out of scope | in week 4 the customer asks for posting to the ERP; clause 6.3 makes it a change request with its own estimate rather than an argument |\n\n### Acceptance harness for clause 4.2\nA minimal, runnable version of the computation behind the clause. It also shows how the choice between\npoint estimate and confidence bound changes the verdict.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\nTHRESHOLDS = {\"total\": 0.98, \"currency\": 0.98, \"supplier_id\": 0.98}   # clause 4.2, exact match\n\ndef wilson_lower(k, n, z=1.96):\n    p, d = k / n, 1 + z * z / n\n    centre = (p + z * z / (2 * n)) / d\n    return centre - z * math.sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n + z * z / (4 * n * n)) / d\n\ndef accept(rows, use_lower_bound=False):\n    \"\"\"rows: one dict per acceptance invoice with gold and predicted values.\"\"\"\n    n, report = len(rows), {}\n    for field, bar in THRESHOLDS.items():\n        k = sum(r[\"gold\"][field] == r[\"pred\"][field] for r in rows)\n        score = wilson_lower(k, n) if use_lower_bound else k / n\n        report[field] = (k, n - k, round(score, 4), score >= bar)\n    wrong_ccy = sum(r[\"gold\"][\"currency\"] != r[\"pred\"][\"currency\"] for r in rows)\n    report[\"zero_tolerance_wrong_currency\"] = (wrong_ccy, wrong_ccy == 0)\n    report[\"rule_of_three_bound\"] = round(3 / n, 4)       # true rate below this at ~95% if clean\n    return report\n\ngold = {\"total\": \"100.00\", \"currency\": \"EUR\", \"supplier_id\": \"S1\"}\nrows = [{\"gold\": gold, \"pred\": dict(gold)} for _ in range(600)]\nfor r in rows[:10]:                                       # 10 wrong totals out of 600\n    r[\"pred\"] = {**gold, \"total\": \"99.00\"}\nprint(accept(rows)[\"total\"])                        # (590, 10, 0.9833, True): passes, max 12 misses\nprint(accept(rows, use_lower_bound=True)[\"total\"])  # lower bound ~0.97: fails the same 0.98 bar\nprint(accept(rows)[\"rule_of_three_bound\"])          # 0.005, about 0.5%\n```\n\n- **The clause must say which reading applies.** The same 10 misses pass on the point estimate and fail on\n  the 95% lower bound, which is exactly the dispute the SOW should prevent.","patterns":["**Phase-gated SOWs.** Sign a short discovery and PoC SOW first, then a pilot or production SOW whose thresholds come from the PoC measurements. Track each gate's exit criteria and the go/no-go decision in the steering notes.","**Acceptance set under version control.** Store the frozen acceptance set, its labelling guide and the harness in a repository both parties can read, with a content hash recorded in the SOW appendix. Monitor that the hash at acceptance matches the signed one.","**Dependency tracker with day-for-day clauses.** Keep each customer obligation, its due date and its actual delivery date in a shared tracker reviewed at every steering meeting. Monitor cumulative slip days, because they move milestones under the day-for-day clause.","**Change log tied to clauses.** Log every change request with the SOW clause it touches, its estimate and its approval. Monitor the number of unapproved requests in flight, which signals scope creep.","**Post-go-live monitoring clause.** Name the metrics, owner and remediation threshold for the warranty period, and wire the same harness to a sample of production traffic. Monitor drift against the acceptance numbers."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[\"Discovery and PoC SOW: effort, not outcomes\"] --> M[\"Measure feasibility and human baseline\"]\n    M --> G1{\"Gate: go or no-go\"}\n    G1 -->|\"no-go\"| X[\"Stop with findings\"]\n    G1 -->|\"go\"| P[\"Pilot SOW: thresholds from measurements\"]\n    P --> A[\"Run frozen acceptance set with harness\"]\n    A --> G2{\"Accept within 10 business days?\"}\n    G2 -->|\"failing cases listed\"| F[\"Fix and re-run\"]\n    F --> A\n    G2 -->|\"accepted or silent\"| W[\"Go-live, warranty, support boundary\"]\n    CR[\"Change request or false assumption\"] -.->|\"estimate, approve, price\"| P","caption":"A phase-gated SOW: each gate turns measured results into the next commitment, and any change or false\nassumption goes through change control rather than silently into scope."},"interview":[{"q":"How is an SOW for an AI project different from one for ordinary software?","a":"- **Acceptance is statistical:** a rate on a frozen, representative eval set with per-field thresholds and\n  a named harness, because a probabilistic system always fails on some inputs.\n- **Extra clauses:** eval set ownership and freeze, model deprecation and re-validation, data handling,\n  post-acceptance drift monitoring, and phase-based commitment before quality targets."},{"q":"The customer shows three wrong answers at acceptance and refuses to sign. What went wrong, and how do you recover?","a":"- **Root cause:** acceptance was written in prose, so any failure counts as a breach.\n- **Recover:** agree a frozen set and thresholds now, run the harness, and evaluate the three cases against\n  severity rules rather than as a veto.\n- **Prevent:** write the computation, the review window and deemed-acceptance terms into the next SOW."},{"q":"When would you not accept a fixed-price SOW?","a":"- **Before feasibility is measured on the customer's data,** because the vendor would be pricing research\n  risk blind.\n- **Use time and materials or a short fixed-fee discovery phase instead,** then move to fixed price or\n  milestones once the PoC has measured achievable quality and the human baseline."},{"q":"Your SOW says zero wrong-currency errors on 600 invoices. What does a clean run actually prove?","a":"- **Only a bound:** by the rule of three, the true rate is below about 3/600, or 0.5%, at 95% confidence.\n- **So pair it with a deterministic control,** such as validating the extracted currency against the vendor\n  master for that supplier, which is what makes \"zero\" credible."},{"q":"What belongs in the customer-responsibilities section, and why the day-for-day clause?","a":"- **Dated obligations:** data extract format and date, SSO and network access, SME hours per week, security\n  review turnaround.\n- **Day-for-day movement** makes slips visible and fair, so a late extract moves milestones instead of\n  silently consuming the delivery team's time."}],"tradeoffs":["**Budget certainty vs priced risk.** Fixed-price SOWs give the customer budget certainty but make the vendor price research risk. Use them only after a PoC has measured feasibility on the customer's own data.","**Flexibility vs cost control.** Time-and-materials terms absorb uncertainty well but give the customer little cost control. They suit discovery and PoC phases, and should give way to milestones once the target is known.","**Fewer disputes vs upfront effort.** Tight, statistical acceptance criteria prevent disputes but take effort to agree on and to build the eval set for. That effort is worth it whenever payment or go-live depends on acceptance.","**Protection vs speed to signature.** A long out-of-scope list protects the delivery team but can read as defensive and slow the signature. Focus it on the extensions customers most often assume are included.","**Capped loss vs repeated negotiation.** Phase gates let both sides stop early, which caps loss. The cost is negotiating several times, which only pays off when uncertainty is high."],"pitfalls":["**Acceptance criteria in prose such as \"accurate\" or \"production-ready\".** Symptom: an acceptance meeting where the customer shows three wrong answers and refuses sign-off.","**Customer dependencies without dates or consequences.** Symptom: the team waits weeks for data or access while the delivery date stays fixed.","**Committing to a quality target before measuring feasibility on real data.** Symptom: a project that is technically sound but contractually failed because the data was worse than the sample.","**No support boundary after go-live.** Symptom: the delivery team is paged for production incidents months after the engagement ended.","**An eval set the customer can change during the project.** Symptom: acceptance thresholds are met and then the set is \"updated\" with new hard cases before sign-off."],"when":"- **Use when:** someone outside the team must accept the result, and especially when money or a go-live\n  date depends on it.\n- **Draft it:** at the end of scoping, once the success metrics, baseline plan and dependencies are known.\n  Ideally phase it so the first SOW covers only discovery and a PoC.\n- **Lighter version:** for a two-week internal spike, a one-page brief with objective, out of scope and an\n  exit question is enough.\n- **Day to day:** the SOW is the reference in every scope discussion.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"how would you write acceptance criteria for a model?\" The answer is a frozen,\n  representative eval set, per-slice thresholds, a zero-tolerance list and a named harness.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Turns acceptance clauses into a runnable eval harness and frozen dataset, so acceptance is a reproducible computation rather than a review meeting.","architect":"Sets the deliverable boundaries, environments, support model and the technical assumptions whose failure would trigger change control.","fde":"Drafts and negotiates the SOW with the customer, makes data and access dependencies dated obligations, and enforces change control on site."},"check":{"q":"A customer insists on a fixed-price SOW guaranteeing \"95% accuracy\" for a document-classification\nassistant, before you have seen their documents. How do you respond?","a":"I would not sign a quality guarantee for unseen data, because nobody knows whether 95% is\nfeasible, what \"accuracy\" covers, or what the human baseline is. I would propose a phased structure.\nFirst, a short fixed-fee discovery and PoC phase: we receive a representative sample, build a jointly\nlabelled eval set, measure the human baseline, and report achievable accuracy per category. Second, a\npilot or production SOW with acceptance criteria set from those measurements: per-class thresholds on\nthe frozen set, zero-tolerance error types, and dated customer obligations. This gives the customer an\nearly exit and a defensible target, and it stops either side from pricing a guess."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are acceptance criteria for model behaviour written against a frozen eval set?","options":["Frozen datasets run faster in the evaluation harness","A fixed, agreed set makes acceptance a reproducible computation and stops the target moving during tuning","Regulators require every model to be tested on frozen data","It lets the team tune the model directly on the acceptance cases"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Probabilistic systems will always fail on some inputs, so acceptance must be a rate on a defined sample. Freezing the set prevents both sides from changing the test mid-project. Tuning on it would make the result meaningless."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of the customer-responsibilities section with dates and consequences?","options":["To shift all project risk onto the customer","To satisfy the MSA's liability terms","To list the customer's internal stakeholders","To make data, access and SME-time dependencies explicit so their delays move the schedule rather than silently consuming it"],"answer":3,"explanation":"AI delivery depends on customer data, access and expert time. Dated obligations with a stated consequence make slips visible and fair, rather than turning them into the delivery team's missed milestones."},{"q":"When does a fixed-price commitment to a quality target become reasonable for an AI project?","options":["When the vendor has hit that target with other customers","When the model provider publishes benchmark results above the target","After a discovery or PoC phase has measured feasibility and the baseline on the customer's own data","Never; AI projects should always be time-and-materials"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Quality depends on the customer's data and task definition, so other customers' results or public benchmarks do not transfer. Once feasibility is measured on real data, the risk is known well enough to price."}]},{"key":"scoping/requirements-traceability","sources":["Gotel, Finkelstein, 1994 — An Analysis of the Requirements Traceability Problem"],"why":"- **Requirements scatter by mid-project.** They live in the SOW, a few Slack threads, and the heads of two\n  SMEs. The eval suite has 800 cases, but nobody can say which requirement each case checks.\n- **\"Probably\" is the honest answer without links.** When the customer asks \"does it meet requirement 7,\n  never discussing individual claims?\", nobody can show evidence.\n- **Averages hide collapses.** After a switch to a cheaper model the eval average moves from 91% to 90%,\n  everyone shrugs, and nobody notices every refusal case for requirement 7 now fails. Those were eight\n  cases, about 1% of the suite, so their collapse moved the average by a single point.\n- **One score plus a stale document breaks both ways.** Forward (requirements to tests): requirements with\n  no test, especially non-functional ones like \"never reveal another employee's data\". Backward (tests and\n  features to requirements): cases and features tracing to nothing, meaning gold-plating, stale behaviour,\n  or unapproved scope creep.\n- **Regulated buyers expect it.** In safety-critical software domains, traceability from requirement to\n  verification evidence is an expected part of certification, and AI governance reviews increasingly ask\n  the same of model-based systems.","how":"### The chain\nGive every element a stable ID and record links between adjacent levels:\n\n1. **Objective** (from the metric tree), for example O1: reduce benefits-desk contacts.\n2. **Requirement:** a testable statement with a priority and an acceptance threshold. Include functional\n   requirements (answer from the current policy), behavioural ones (escalate medical-claim questions), and\n   non-functional ones (p95 latency, data isolation, retention).\n3. **Implementation elements:** the components, prompt templates, tools, retrieval filters and guardrails\n   that realise each requirement, recorded with their version.\n4. **Verification:** eval cases tagged with the requirement IDs they check, plus deterministic tests, load\n   tests and security tests for the non-functional requirements.\n5. **Evidence:** the run ID, the model and prompt versions, the date, and the per-requirement result.\n\n### Why per-requirement thresholds\n- **The slice score decides, not the global average,** because requirements differ in criticality and in\n  volume. A 100% requirement such as \"never reveal another employee's data\" can be outvoted by hundreds of\n  routine cases in an average.\n- **Slicing gives each requirement a sample size,** so you can tell whether a slice has enough cases for its\n  result to mean anything.\n\n### Zero-tolerance slices and the rule of three\nIf a slice of n cases has no failures, the 95% upper bound on the true failure rate is about 3/n:\n\n| Clean cases in slice | Failure rate shown to be below |\n|---|---|\n| 30 | roughly 10% |\n| 300 | roughly 1% |\n\n- **A 100% threshold on a small slice** is evidence of \"not frequent\", not of \"never\".\n- **A requirement that truly must never fail** should also trace to a deterministic control, such as a\n  permission filter or a hard validator with its own unit tests, with the eval slice as a second line of\n  evidence.\n\n### The two queries that matter\n- **Coverage (forward):** which requirements have no verification, or too few cases to judge? Fill these\n  gaps before acceptance, not after an incident.\n- **Orphans (backward):** which tests, prompts or features trace to no requirement? Either a requirement is\n  missing from the document, and should be added and approved, or the element is unneeded and should be\n  removed.\n\n### Change impact analysis\nWhen something changes, the links say what to re-verify:\n\n| Change | Re-verify |\n|---|---|\n| New model version | every requirement; re-run everything and compare per-requirement results |\n| Retrieval filter change | requirements linked to that component |\n| Requirement change | its linked cases, which may need relabelling |\n\n- **This is the payoff that justifies the bookkeeping:** the cost of change becomes proportional to what\n  changed, rather than \"re-test everything and hope\".\n\n### Keep it lightweight\n- **No requirements-management tool needed.** Tags on eval cases (a `reqs` field in the JSONL), a\n  requirements file in the repo with IDs and thresholds, and a CI step that prints the per-requirement\n  report cover most AI projects.\n- **Generate the matrix from the artefacts,** because a traceability matrix that is not generated from them\n  goes stale within weeks.","example":"### Per-requirement CI report with coverage and orphans\nAn HR benefits assistant. Eval cases carry requirement tags, and CI prints a per-requirement report that\ncatches untested requirements and orphan cases. A real suite would have tens to hundreds of cases per\nrequirement; this one is cut down to show the mechanics.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nREQS = {   # from the SOW: id -> (statement, acceptance threshold on its slice)\n    \"R1\": (\"Answer benefits questions from the current policy only\", 0.95),\n    \"R2\": (\"Refuse and escalate questions about individual medical claims\", 1.00),\n    \"R3\": (\"Cite the policy section for every factual answer\", 0.90),\n    \"R4\": (\"Never reveal another employee's data\", 1.00),\n}\nCASES = [  # eval cases tagged with the requirements they verify, plus latest result\n    {\"id\": \"c001\", \"reqs\": [\"R1\", \"R3\"], \"passed\": True},\n    {\"id\": \"c002\", \"reqs\": [\"R1\"],       \"passed\": True},\n    {\"id\": \"c003\", \"reqs\": [\"R2\"],       \"passed\": False},\n    {\"id\": \"c004\", \"reqs\": [\"R1\", \"R3\"], \"passed\": False},\n    {\"id\": \"c005\", \"reqs\": [\"R9\"],       \"passed\": True},   # tag points at no requirement\n    {\"id\": \"c006\", \"reqs\": [],           \"passed\": True},   # untagged case\n]\nMIN_CASES = 30   # below this, a slice result is too noisy to accept on\n\nby_req, orphans = defaultdict(list), []\nfor c in CASES:\n    known = [r for r in c[\"reqs\"] if r in REQS]\n    if not known:\n        orphans.append(c[\"id\"])\n    for r in known:\n        by_req[r].append(c[\"passed\"])\n\nfor rid, (text, threshold) in REQS.items():\n    res = by_req.get(rid, [])\n    if not res:\n        print(f\"{rid} UNTESTED  {text}\")\n        continue\n    rate = sum(res) / len(res)\n    note = \"  (too few cases)\" if len(res) < MIN_CASES else \"\"\n    print(f\"{rid} {'PASS' if rate >= threshold else 'FAIL'}  {rate:.0%} of {len(res)} \"\n          f\"(needs {threshold:.0%}){note}  {text}\")\nprint(\"orphans:\", orphans)   # -> ['c005', 'c006']\n```\n\nReading the output:\n- **R4 (data isolation) is UNTESTED.** That would never show up in an aggregate pass rate, and it is exactly\n  the requirement a security reviewer will ask about.\n- **R2 fails outright,** and its 100% threshold means a single failure blocks acceptance.\n- **Both orphans need a decision:** is c005's \"R9\" a typo, or a requirement nobody wrote down?\n- **When R4 gets cases,** a 30-case slice passing at 100% only bounds the leak rate below about 10%. So R4\n  should also trace to the retrieval layer's per-employee access filter and its deterministic tests, not to\n  the eval slice alone.\n\n### Change impact: what to re-verify for a given change\nThe same links, walked from implementation elements to requirements. A CI job can call this with the\nfiles changed in a pull request (element names here are illustrative).\n\n```python\nLINKS = {   # implementation element -> requirement IDs it realises (from the matrix)\n    \"model\":                   [\"*\"],              # the model touches every requirement\n    \"prompt/answer_v3\":        [\"R1\", \"R3\"],\n    \"prompt/escalation_v2\":    [\"R2\"],\n    \"retrieval/policy_filter\": [\"R1\"],\n    \"retrieval/employee_acl\":  [\"R4\"],\n}\nREQ_IDS = [\"R1\", \"R2\", \"R3\", \"R4\"]\n\ndef to_reverify(changed):\n    hit = set()\n    for element in changed:\n        reqs = LINKS.get(element)\n        if reqs is None:\n            raise KeyError(f\"{element} traces to no requirement: orphan or missing link\")\n        hit |= set(REQ_IDS) if \"*\" in reqs else set(reqs)\n    return sorted(hit)\n\nprint(to_reverify([\"prompt/answer_v3\"]))          # ['R1', 'R3']\nprint(to_reverify([\"retrieval/employee_acl\"]))    # ['R4']\nprint(to_reverify([\"model\"]))                     # ['R1', 'R2', 'R3', 'R4']\n```\n\n- **An unknown element raises,** which turns a missing link into a CI failure instead of an untested change.","patterns":["**Requirements file in the repo.** Keep requirement IDs, statements, priorities and thresholds in a version-controlled file reviewed like code, with changes approved by the requirement owner. Monitor for requirements with no linked cases after each change.","**Tagged eval data.** Add a `reqs` list to every eval case in the JSONL and fail CI when a case has no known tag. Monitor the orphan count and the per-requirement case count against a minimum such as 30.","**Per-requirement gate in CI.** Print the per-requirement report on every prompt or model change, and block merge when a critical slice falls below its threshold. Monitor per-requirement diffs between the current and candidate configuration, not the global average.","**Evidence records with versions.** Store each run's ID, model version, prompt versions, date and per-requirement results as an immutable artefact. Monitor that every production configuration maps to a stored passing run.","**Deterministic controls for never-fail requirements.** Trace data-isolation and similar requirements to a permission filter or validator with its own unit tests, and keep the eval slice as a second line of evidence."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    O[\"Objective O1\"] --> R[\"Requirement with threshold\"]\n    R --> I[\"Implementation: prompt, filter, tool, version\"]\n    R --> V[\"Verification: tagged eval cases, tests\"]\n    V --> E[(\"Evidence: run ID, versions, slice result\")]\n    C[\"Change: model, prompt or filter\"] -->|\"follow links\"| I\n    I -->|\"linked requirements\"| R\n    R -->|\"re-run slices\"| V\n    V -.->|\"no requirement tag\"| OR[\"Orphan: add requirement or remove\"]","caption":"Forward links answer \"is every requirement verified?\"; walking them backward from a changed element answers\n\"what must be re-tested?\"."},"interview":[{"q":"What is requirements traceability, and why does an AI system need it more than ordinary software?","a":"- **Definition:** maintained links from objectives to requirements, implementation elements, verification\n  cases and evidence, each with a stable ID.\n- **Why AI needs it more:** a prompt or model change can shift behaviour everywhere at once, and an\n  aggregate eval score hides a small critical slice collapsing."},{"q":"A security reviewer asks you to prove the assistant never shows one employee another's data. What do you show?","a":"- **The trace:** requirement R4 linked to the per-employee access filter in retrieval, its deterministic\n  unit tests, and a tagged eval slice.\n- **The evidence:** the run ID, model and prompt versions and the slice result for the configuration in\n  production.\n- **The honest bound:** a clean slice of n cases shows the rate is below about 3/n, so the filter, not the\n  eval, carries the \"never\"."},{"q":"When would you not set up a full traceability matrix?","a":"- **For a throwaway PoC,** where a short list of requirements mapped to test names is enough.\n- **When there are only a handful of prompts and one team,** fine-grained tracing of every template slows\n  refactoring more than it helps; tag eval cases at the requirement level instead."},{"q":"Your eval suite has 800 cases and three teams editing prompts. How do you keep the matrix from going stale?","a":"- **Generate it from the artefacts:** a `reqs` tag on every case, a requirements file in the repo, and a CI\n  step that builds the report.\n- **Fail CI on orphans and untested critical requirements,** and enforce minimum case counts per slice.\n- **Record versions with every run,** so evidence always maps to a configuration."},{"q":"What does a backward trace find that a forward trace misses, and what do you do with it?","a":"- **Backward finds orphans:** cases, prompts or features that serve no agreed requirement.\n- **Decide each one:** either a missing requirement to write down and approve, or gold-plating and\n  unapproved scope to remove before a refactor silently deletes behaviour customers rely on."}],"tradeoffs":["**Precision vs tagging time.** Fine-grained tagging gives precise impact analysis but costs time on every new case. Tag at the requirement level, not the sub-clause level, unless the domain is regulated.","**Audit workflow vs drift.** A dedicated requirements tool gives audit trails and approval workflows but drifts away from the code. Prefer IDs in repo files and eval data unless the customer's compliance process mandates a tool.","**Strictness vs blocked releases.** Per-requirement thresholds are stricter than a global average and can block releases over small slices. Pair them with minimum case counts so noise does not decide acceptance.","**Traceable prompts vs refactoring speed.** Tracing every prompt template to requirements makes refactoring slower. It earns its cost once the system has more than a handful of prompts or more than one team editing them."],"pitfalls":["**Reporting a single aggregate eval score as proof of acceptance.** Symptom: a release that passes on average while a small, critical requirement fails every case.","**Non-functional requirements left out of the matrix.** Symptom: a security review finds that data isolation or retention was never tested at all.","**A hand-maintained traceability spreadsheet.** Symptom: a matrix that lists cases which no longer exist and misses the ones added last sprint.","**Not recording model and prompt versions with the evidence.** Symptom: nobody can say which configuration actually passed acceptance once something regresses in production.","**Treating orphan cases as harmless.** Symptom: behaviour customers rely on that is undocumented, untested against a threshold, and removed by the next refactor."],"when":"- **Use when:** there is an SOW with numbered requirements and more than a few dozen eval cases. In practice\n  that means the start of the pilot build, because that is when tagging is cheapest.\n- **Essential when:** acceptance is contractual, several teams change prompts or models, or the buyer is\n  regulated and will audit.\n- **Lighter version:** for a throwaway PoC, a short list of requirements mapped to test names is enough.\n- **Day to day:** a per-requirement CI report, and the first thing you check when a model upgrade is proposed.\n- **Interviews:** it answers \"how do you know a model swap didn't break anything?\"","role_lens":{"engineer":"Tags eval cases with requirement IDs, writes the per-requirement CI report, and records model and prompt versions with every run's evidence.","architect":"Defines the ID scheme and the links between requirements, components and evals, so change impact analysis works across teams and releases.","fde":"Keeps the customer's requirements and the matrix aligned on site, and uses the report as acceptance evidence with the customer's QA or compliance team."},"check":{"q":"The team wants to switch to a cheaper model. The overall eval pass rate drops from 92% to 91%.\nWhy is that not enough to approve the change, and what would you look at?","a":"An aggregate hides how the drop is distributed. One point overall could be noise spread evenly,\nor it could be every case of a small, critical requirement failing, such as refusals for regulated\nquestions or data isolation. I would run the per-requirement report from the traceability matrix.\nThat means checking each requirement's slice against its own threshold, flagging slices with too few\ncases to judge, and diffing per-requirement results between the two models. A model change touches\nevery requirement, so all of them need to be re-verified. I would approve only if every critical\nrequirement still meets its threshold, and record the new model version with the evidence."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does a backward trace (from tests and features to requirements) mainly reveal?","options":["Requirements that have no test coverage","Tests, prompts or features that serve no agreed requirement, such as gold-plating or undocumented scope","The model version that produced each result","Which requirements have the highest business value"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Forward tracing finds untested requirements. Backward tracing finds elements that trace to nothing. Those indicate missing requirements to approve, or unneeded work and unapproved scope to remove."},{"q":"Why should each requirement be accepted on its own slice and threshold rather than the global eval score?","options":["Slices run faster in CI than the full suite","Global scores are not statistically valid","It lets the team drop hard cases from the average","A small but critical requirement can fail completely while the average barely moves"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Requirements differ in criticality and case count. A per-requirement threshold means a zero-tolerance behaviour such as data isolation decides acceptance on its own, rather than being outvoted by many routine cases."},{"q":"Which change requires re-verifying every requirement, not just a subset?","options":["Editing the citation format prompt for one answer type","Adding ten new eval cases to the escalation slice","Upgrading the underlying model to a new version","Renaming a requirement ID in the requirements file"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The model affects every behaviour, so impact analysis links it to all requirements. A prompt edit touches only the requirements linked to that template, and adding cases or renaming IDs changes verification or bookkeeping, not behaviour."}]},{"key":"scoping/dependency-and-risk-management","sources":["Official docs: NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF 1.0)"],"why":"- **The plan says \"weeks 1–2: data ingestion\"; reality differs.** Week 1 is spent learning the document\n  system's export needs a ticket to a team with a three-week queue. Week 3 reveals a third of the documents\n  are scanned images. Week 5 brings the security verdict that customer data cannot go to an external API in\n  that region.\n- **None of these is an engineering failure,** yet each moves the delivery date, and the team takes the\n  blame because nobody wrote the dependency down with an owner and a date.\n- **A kickoff-deck risk list gives no protection.** Written once and never reopened, it records risks and\n  drives no action. Rating everything \"medium\" makes it impossible to prioritise.\n- **Watching risks instead of retiring them** leaves the biggest unknowns (data quality, security approval,\n  feasibility on real inputs) unresolved until late, when there is no slack left to absorb them.\n- **AI adds its own risks:** the data may not support the task, the eval set may not represent production,\n  model behaviour can change when a provider updates or retires a version, and quotas or regional\n  availability can block a deployment design that worked in a demo.","how":"### Keep a RAID log\nOne living log, because each category triggers a different action:\n\n| Category | Meaning | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| **R**isks | things that might happen | get a response |\n| **A**ssumptions | treated as true but not verified | get verified |\n| **I**ssues | risks that have already happened | get resolved now |\n| **D**ependencies | inputs owned by someone else | get chased by their owner |\n\n### Map dependencies\n1. **Record each one:** what is needed (in a specific format), who owns it (a named person, not \"IT\"), the\n   realistic lead time, the need-by date derived from the plan, and the status.\n2. **Find the critical path:** the chain of dependent tasks with zero slack, such as data export → eval set\n   → PoC → pilot sign-off. Anything on that chain slips the end date day for day; anything off it has slack\n   you can spend.\n3. **Start the longest-lead items first.** Security reviews, legal agreements and procurement often take\n   longer than the engineering, so they should start in week zero, before anything is built.\n\n### Identify risks by category\nWalk a checklist so you do not rely on memory:\n- **Data:** availability, quality, volume, representativeness, rights to use.\n- **Access and security:** credentials, network paths, approval of external model APIs, data residency.\n- **Technical and model:** feasibility on real inputs, latency or cost at volume, provider quotas,\n  deprecation of a model version.\n- **People and adoption:** SME availability, sponsor turnover, user resistance.\n- **Commercial and vendor:** pricing changes, contract terms, lock-in.\n- **Regulatory and harm:** whether outputs could harm users or breach rules. This overlaps with AI-system\n  risk management (frameworks such as the NIST AI RMF), which is covered in its own lesson.\n\n### Assess in units people can act on\n- **Score likelihood and impact,** with impact in schedule days or money where possible, not \"high\".\n- **Expected delay = probability × impact,** which compares directly with the slack on the affected path.\n\n| Risk | Probability | Impact | Expected | Path slack | Effect on end date |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | 50% | 15 days | 7.5 days | 0 (critical path) | lands directly |\n| B | 90% | 2 days | 1.8 days | 10 days | absorbed |\n\n- **Expected delay ranks risks but is not the whole story.** A 10% chance of a 60-day slip has the same 6\n  expected days as a near-certain 6-day slip, yet only the first can end the engagement.\n- **Plan fatal risks by impact, not expected value.** If the pilot could be unable to run at all, or a\n  budget window could close, buy the risk down early or prepare a contingency whatever its expected days\n  say.\n\n### Choose a response and a trigger\n| Response | How |\n|---|---|\n| **Avoid** | change the plan |\n| **Reduce** | a spike, a fallback or an earlier start |\n| **Transfer** | to the party best placed to own it, for example a data date as a customer obligation in the SOW |\n| **Accept** | with a contingency plan |\n\n- **Give each risk an early-warning trigger,** such as \"export not received by day 10\", so switching to the\n  contingency is a pre-agreed step rather than a debate held in a crisis.\n\n### Retire the biggest unknowns first\n- **Order early work by the uncertainty it removes,** not by the order the architecture suggests. A two-day\n  spike on 50 real documents removes more risk than two weeks of polished pipeline code over synthetic ones.\n- **Review the log weekly** at the steering meeting, with escalation thresholds agreed in the SOW.","example":"### Worked scenario: 10-week contract-review pilot\nA 10-week pilot of a contract-review assistant for a legal department. All figures are illustrative. At\nkickoff the RAID log holds these top items:\n\n| ID | Item | Owner | Prob | Impact | Exp. days | Response and trigger |\n|----|------|-------|------|--------|-----------|----------------------|\n| D1 | DMS export of 3,000 contracts | Customer IT | — | critical path | — | Ticket raised day 0; need-by end of week 2 |\n| R1 | Security review rejects external LLM API | Customer CISO | 0.3 | 20 days | 6 | Start review day 0; adapter layer allows in-region hosted model |\n| R2 | Many contracts are scans with poor OCR | Delivery lead | 0.5 | 10 days | 5 | Spike on 50 real files in week 1 |\n| R3 | SME lawyers miss labelling sessions | Customer GC | 0.6 | 5 days | 3 | Book sessions now; trigger if <75% attended in week 3 |\n| R4 | Provider quota too low for batch backfill | Engineer | 0.2 | 3 days | 0.6 | Request increase week 1; accept |\n\n- **The plan as drawn is not credible.** D1, then R1 and R2, sit on the critical path, because the eval set\n  needs the export and the pilot needs security approval. Plan slack is 5 days, and the expected delays\n  from R1 and R2 alone (11 days) exceed it.\n- **What the team did:**\n  1. Submitted the security questionnaire on day 0, not when the pilot was ready.\n  2. Ran the OCR spike in week 1. It found 30% of files are scans and OCR is acceptable on 80% of them, so\n     R2 dropped to a 3-day impact.\n  3. Wrote D1's need-by date into the SOW, so a late export moves the schedule.\n- **Outcome:** in week 4 the security review asked for an in-region deployment. Because R1 had a\n  contingency, the switch took 4 days instead of the 20 it would have cost unplanned.\n\n### Exposure calculator for the weekly review\nRanks risks by expected days, flags fatal-impact ones, and compares critical-path exposure with slack.\nRe-run it after each spike; the numbers reproduce the scenario above.\n\n```python\nRISKS = [  # id, probability, impact in schedule days, on critical path?\n    (\"R1\", 0.3, 20, True),    # security review rejects external LLM API\n    (\"R2\", 0.5, 10, True),    # scans with poor OCR\n    (\"R3\", 0.6, 5, False),    # SMEs miss labelling sessions\n    (\"R4\", 0.2, 3, False),    # provider quota too low for backfill\n]\nSLACK_DAYS = 5\nFATAL_DAYS = 30               # illustrative: a slip this large would end the engagement\n\ndef report(risks, slack=SLACK_DAYS):\n    for rid, p, days, critical in sorted(risks, key=lambda r: r[1] * r[2], reverse=True):\n        flag = \"  FATAL-IMPACT: plan by impact\" if days >= FATAL_DAYS else \"\"\n        print(f\"{rid} expected {p * days:4.1f} days  critical={critical}{flag}\")\n    exposure = sum(p * d for _, p, d, c in risks if c)\n    print(f\"critical-path exposure {exposure:.1f} days vs slack {slack}: \"\n          f\"plan {'not credible' if exposure > slack else 'holds'}\")\n\ndef with_impact(risks, rid, days):\n    return [(i, p, days if i == rid else d, c) for i, p, d, c in risks]\n\nreport(RISKS)                                    # 11.0 vs 5: not credible\nafter_spike = with_impact(RISKS, \"R2\", 3)        # OCR spike: R2 impact 10 -> 3 days\nreport(after_spike)                              # 7.5 vs 5: still not credible\nreport(with_impact(after_spike, \"R1\", 4))        # adapter contingency: R1 20 -> 4 days, 2.7 vs 5 holds\n```\n\n- **Exposure should fall week by week** as spikes and contingencies retire unknowns; here it goes 11.0, then\n  7.5, then 2.7 days.","patterns":["**RAID log as a shared, owned document.** Keep it where both delivery team and customer edit it, with a named person per row and a need-by date per dependency. Monitor the count of dependencies past their need-by date at every weekly steering meeting.","**Week-zero approvals.** Submit the security questionnaire, data processing agreement and procurement requests on day 0, in parallel with building. Track each approval's age against its expected lead time.","**Provider seam for contingencies.** Put model calls behind an adapter interface so a switch to an in-region or alternative hosted model is a configuration change. Monitor provider quota usage, regional availability and announced model deprecation dates.","**Spike-first backlog.** Schedule one- to two-day spikes on real data for every risk on the critical path before building pipeline code. Track total critical-path exposure week by week; a flat or rising curve means the work is not attacking the risks.","**Triggers wired to the calendar.** Turn each trigger (for example \"export not received by day 10\") into a dated check owned by a person, with the contingency pre-agreed in steering notes. Monitor fired triggers and time from trigger to switch."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    I[\"Identify: checklist by category\"] --> L[(\"RAID log with named owners\")]\n    L --> S[\"Size: probability x days vs slack\"]\n    S --> F{\"Fatal impact or on critical path?\"}\n    F -->|\"yes\"| R[\"Retire first: spike, early start, fallback\"]\n    F -->|\"no\"| A[\"Accept or transfer with trigger\"]\n    R --> W[\"Weekly steering review\"]\n    A --> W\n    W --> T{\"Trigger fired?\"}\n    T -->|\"yes\"| C[\"Switch to contingency\"]\n    T -->|\"no\"| L\n    C --> L","caption":"The weekly risk loop: every item gets sized against slack, a response and a trigger, and a fired trigger\nswitches to a pre-agreed contingency."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a RAID log, and why keep four categories instead of one risk list?","a":"- **Definition:** a living log of risks, assumptions, issues and dependencies, each row with a named owner.\n- **Why four:** each category triggers a different action. Assumptions get verified, risks get a response\n  and trigger, issues get resolved now, and dependencies get chased by their owner."},{"q":"What are the biggest risks specific to an AI pilot that an ordinary software plan misses?","a":"- **Data:** the data may not support the task, and scans or poor OCR can surface weeks in.\n- **Evaluation:** the eval set may not represent production inputs.\n- **Provider:** behaviour changes when a model version is updated or retired, and quotas or regional\n  availability can block a design that worked in a demo.\n- **Approval:** security review of external model APIs and data residency, often the longest lead time."},{"q":"When would you plan by impact rather than by expected delay?","a":"- **When the impact would be fatal,** such as the pilot being unable to run at all or a budget window\n  closing.\n- **Example:** a 10% chance of a 60-day slip and a near-certain 6-day slip both carry 6 expected days, but\n  only the first can end the engagement, so buy it down early or prepare a contingency."},{"q":"Your critical-path exposure is 11 expected days against 5 days of slack in week 1. What do you do?","a":"- **Say now that the plan is not credible,** rather than at the deadline.\n- **Attack the biggest items:** submit the security review on day 0, build an adapter for an in-region\n  fallback, run a spike on 50 real files, and write the export need-by date into the SOW.\n- **Re-run the numbers** after each action and report the exposure trend to the sponsor weekly."},{"q":"How do you make customer dependencies enforceable without damaging the relationship?","a":"- **Make them dated SOW obligations** with a day-for-day schedule consequence, only for items the customer\n  genuinely controls.\n- **Frame it as shared planning:** named owners, need-by dates visible in the shared log, and early-warning\n  triggers agreed together, so a slip is a known step rather than blame."}],"tradeoffs":["**Coverage vs steering time.** A detailed RAID log catches more surprises but costs steering time every week. Keep the top ten items active and archive the rest, unless the engagement is regulated or multi-vendor.","**Early certainty vs visible progress.** Front-loading spikes removes uncertainty early but delays visible demo progress. That trade is worth making whenever a feasibility or data risk sits on the critical path.","**Schedule protection vs relationship.** Transferring risk to the customer through dated obligations protects the schedule but can strain the relationship. Frame it as shared planning, and only for items they genuinely control.","**Cheap contingency vs upfront effort.** Building contingency architecture, such as a provider adapter or in-region fallback, costs effort upfront. It pays off when security or provider approval is uncertain, and is waste when approval is already confirmed.","**Real prioritisation vs false precision.** Quantified scoring (probability × days) gives real prioritisation but implies false precision. Treat the numbers as ranking tools, not forecasts, when the estimates are rough."],"pitfalls":["**Writing the risk list once at kickoff.** Symptom: a deck slide that still lists \"data quality\" as medium in week 8, after it has already caused a slip.","**Naming teams instead of people as owners.** Symptom: a dependency marked \"with IT\" for three weeks with nobody able to say who is working on it.","**Starting security and legal reviews only when the system is ready.** Symptom: a finished pilot that waits a month for approval to touch production data.","**Accepting risks without triggers or contingencies.** Symptom: a crisis meeting debating options that could have been decided calmly in week one.","**Ignoring model and provider risks.** Symptom: a deployment design that fails on quota, regional availability or a deprecated model version in the final week."],"when":"- **Use when:** scoping, before the SOW is signed, because dated customer dependencies and known risks\n  belong in the SOW itself. Then review the log weekly for the life of the engagement.\n- **Lighter version:** even a two-week internal PoC benefits from a ten-line version naming the data, access\n  and people it depends on.\n- **Measure:** count dependencies past their need-by date, compare expected delay with slack on the critical\n  path, and track total exposure (the sum of expected days on the critical path) week by week. Exposure\n  should fall as spikes retire unknowns; a flat or rising curve means the work is not attacking the risks.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"what could stop this project?\" The strong answer names specific, owned\n  dependencies and the spike that would retire the biggest unknown first.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Runs the early spikes that retire technical and data risks, and builds the adapters and fallbacks that turn contingencies into small switches.","architect":"Identifies architectural risks such as data residency, provider lock-in, quotas and deprecation, and designs seams so the contingency is cheap to execute.","fde":"Owns the RAID log with the customer, chases named owners for access and data, and escalates through the steering group when a trigger fires."},"check":{"q":"You are two weeks into a 12-week pilot. The data export is late, SMEs have missed one labelling\nsession, and the security review has not started. How do you prioritise, and what do you change?","a":"First I would map which items sit on the critical path. The security review usually has the\nlongest lead time and gates any production-data pilot, so not having started it is the biggest risk.\nI would get it submitted this week, with an architecture fallback ready in case external APIs are\nrejected. The late export is a dependency on the critical path, so I would name a specific owner,\nagree a new date, and invoke the SOW clause that moves milestones day for day. The missed SME session\nis a risk to watch: rebook it and set an attendance trigger. Then I would re-estimate expected delay\nagainst remaining slack and tell the sponsor now whether the end date still holds."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should a security review or data-processing agreement usually start in week zero?","options":["Reviewers prefer to see an early design rather than a finished system","It is a legal requirement before any code is written","It often has the longest lead time and gates the pilot, so starting late puts its full duration on the critical path","Starting early lets the team skip the review if nobody objects"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Approval processes are frequently slower than the engineering and block access to real data. Starting them first runs them in parallel with building, instead of serially after it."},{"q":"A risk has a 50% chance of a 15-day slip on the critical path; another has a 90% chance of a 2-day slip on a path with 10 days of slack. Which matters more?","options":["The second, because it is more likely to happen","The first, because its expected delay of 7.5 days lands directly on the end date","They are equal, because both are rated likely","Neither, because risks under 20 days are acceptable"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Expected delay is probability × impact, and a slip moves the end date only by the amount it exceeds the slack on its path. The second risk's 2-day slip, even if it happens, is fully absorbed by 10 days of slack, while the first lands directly on the end date with 7.5 expected days."},{"q":"What distinguishes an assumption from a risk in a RAID log?","options":["Assumptions are low-impact risks","Risks are owned by the customer, while assumptions are owned by the vendor","Assumptions are issues that have already happened","An assumption is something treated as true but unverified, so its action is to verify it"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Each RAID category has a different action. Assumptions get verified, because a false one usually becomes a risk or issue. Risks get a response and a trigger, issues get resolved now, and dependencies get chased with their owner."}]},{"key":"scoping/timeboxing","sources":[],"why":"- **AI work breaks classic estimation.** Estimation assumes known work: list the features, sum the effort.\n  Whether an LLM can extract fields from this customer's scanned invoices at 95% accuracy is an empirical\n  question nobody can answer by thinking; you find out by trying.\n- **The trying has no natural end.** Accuracy climbs fast, then flattens: day one of prompt work might move\n  a metric from 60% to 80%, the next week from 80% to 85%, the following month from 85% to 87%. There is\n  always one more prompt variant, retrieval tweak or model to try.\n- **Estimating the work fails.** \"The extraction feature will take three weeks\" is a guess about an unknown.\n  When it is wrong the team keeps going, because stopping feels like admitting failure and the next tweak\n  always looks promising. Result: a proof of concept that is \"almost there\" for three months.\n- **A timebox inverts the commitment.** You promise a *decision* by a date, backed by evidence, not an\n  outcome. Scope is the variable: missing the target when the box closes is information, not a slip.\n- **It protects the customer relationship,** because the customer buys a de-risked answer on a known\n  schedule rather than an open-ended research project.","how":"### 1. Name the question, not the task\n- **Good:** one question with a yes/no or a number: \"Can we reach 90% field-level accuracy on their real\n  invoice sample with an off-the-shelf model?\"\n- **Bad:** an activity: \"Work on extraction.\"\n- **Why the question form matters:** it tells you when you are done and what evidence counts.\n\n### 2. Set the budget from the value of the answer\nMatch the box length to what the answer is worth and how much uncertainty it removes, not to how long the\nwork \"should\" take. A rough value-of-information test keeps this honest:\n\n```\nworth boxing if   cost of box  <  P(answer changes the decision) × cost of deciding wrongly\n```\n\n- **Large stakes justify longer boxes.** A question that gates a large contract justifies two weeks; which\n  of two chunking strategies to use justifies a day.\n- **Worked check:** three engineers for two weeks is about 30 person-days. If the answer has a\n  one-in-three chance of flipping a decision that would otherwise sink six months of a larger team into the\n  wrong design, the box is cheap.\n- **No decision change, no box.** If the team would make the same choice whatever the result, the box buys\n  nothing; skip or shrink it.\n\n| Box type | Typical length |\n|---|---|\n| Spike | hours |\n| Feasibility check | days |\n| PoC | a few weeks |\n| Open-ended | almost never |\n\n- **Sprints are timeboxes too, but for delivery cadence.** The discovery-style box here differs in that\n  \"stop\" is an expected, legitimate outcome.\n\n### 3. Write the exit criteria before starting\nWrite the decision rule before any work: what result means go, stop or pivot. For example:\n\n| Result on the 100-document set | Decision |\n|---|---|\n| ≥ 90% | proceed to pilot |\n| 75–90% | one more week, focused only on the top two error categories |\n| Below 75% | stop and recommend a human-in-the-loop design |\n\n- **Why pre-commit:** after a week of effort everyone is anchored on their sunk cost, and criteria written\n  afterwards drift toward whatever was achieved.\n\n### 4. Order the work by risk, with a scope ladder\n1. **Split scope** into must, should and could.\n2. **Do the riskiest must-have first,** because if it fails nothing else matters and you want to learn that\n   on day two, not day nine.\n3. **Build the evaluation set before tuning,** so every hour of tuning moves a number you trust.\n\n### 5. Checkpoint at fixed points\nA two-week box gets a midpoint check: are we on a trajectory to the target, or flat? Make it arithmetic\nrather than mood:\n\n```\nrequired rate = (target − current) / days remaining\nobserved rate = gain over the last few working days / those days\n```\n\n- **Observed well below required:** grinding will not close the gap. The second week should cut scope,\n  change approach, or prepare a stop recommendation.\n- **Caution 1, curves bend down:** the observed rate overstates the future one.\n- **Caution 2, noise:** a gain only counts if it exceeds the eval set's noise. On 100 documents a one-point\n  move can come from a handful of borderline cases flipping, so \"we went from 86% to 87%\" may be no\n  movement at all.\n\n### 6. Close the box with a decision record\n- **Record** the question, the evidence, the decision and what was learned, including dead ends.\n- **Extensions are new boxes.** Extending is allowed, but only as an explicit new box with its own question\n  and criteria, never as a silent slide.","example":"### Midpoint checkpoint calculator\nTurns the checkpoint into arithmetic, with a rough noise floor (one standard error of a proportion over\nthe number of documents, because fields within a document fail together).\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef checkpoint(target, scores, day, days_total, n_docs, window=2):\n    \"\"\"scores: accuracy in points (0-100) for the last few working days, oldest first.\"\"\"\n    current = scores[-1]\n    required = (target - current) / (days_total - day)\n    observed = (scores[-1] - scores[-1 - window]) / window\n    p = current / 100\n    noise = 100 * math.sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n_docs)    # one standard error, in points\n    if observed >= 2 * required:\n        verdict = \"continue; drop 'could' items to protect the time\"\n    elif observed >= required:\n        verdict = \"continue, scoped to the top error categories\"\n    else:\n        verdict = \"change approach or prepare a stop recommendation\"\n    return round(required, 2), round(observed, 2), round(noise, 1), verdict\n\nprint(checkpoint(90, [71, 77, 83], day=5, days_total=10, n_docs=100))\n# (1.4, 6.0, 3.8, \"continue; drop 'could' items to protect the time\")\nprint(checkpoint(90, [86, 86.5, 87], day=7, days_total=10, n_docs=100))\n# (1.0, 0.5, 3.4, 'change approach or prepare a stop recommendation')\n```\n\nThe second call shows the trap: a 1-point gain sits well inside a ~3.4-point noise floor, so it is not\nevidence of progress.\n\n### Worked scenario: a two-week extraction box\nA forward-deployed team gets two weeks (10 working days) to test whether an LLM can extract 12 fields from\na logistics customer's bills of lading.\n\n- **Question:** can we reach ≥ 90% field-level accuracy on the customer's real documents?\n- **Exit rule (written day 0):** ≥ 90% → pilot; 80–90% → one focused extra week; < 80% → recommend a\n  review-queue design where the model drafts and a clerk confirms.\n\n| Day | Work | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1–2 | Obtain 150 real documents; customer's clerks label 100 of them (1,200 field values). The must-have: without it every later number is opinion. | eval set |\n| 3 | Baseline: strong general model, simple prompt | 71% (852 of 1,200 correct, 348 wrong) |\n| 4–5 | Error analysis: about 40% of the 348 errors (roughly 140, out of only 300 values in those fields) come from three fields: consignee address on multi-line stamps, weight unit, date format. Explicit format instructions and examples. | 83% (about 996 correct) |\n| 5 (midpoint) | Required rate (90 − 83) / 5 = 1.4 points per day; the last two days delivered about 6 points per day. Even allowing for flattening, continue. Drop the \"could\" item (a review UI). | continue |\n| 6–9 | OCR preprocessing for stamped regions; further prompt variants add under a point | 88% |\n| 10 | 88% (about 1,056 correct, 144 wrong) lands in the 80–90% band. Roughly 60% of remaining errors sit in the ~20 documents with handwritten weights and annotations. | one more week, scoped only to handwritten documents, with its own pass/fail threshold |\n\nThe numbers are illustrative, but the shape is typical: rapid early gains, then a plateau that only a\npre-written rule stops the team from chasing indefinitely.","patterns":["**Decision record per box** — when every box closes. How: a one-page file in the repo or project wiki with question, exit rule, evidence table, decision and dead ends, committed on day 0 with the rule filled in. Monitor: share of boxes that end with a written decision, and how many end in \"stop\".","**Frozen, versioned eval set** — before any tuning. How: label the set, store it read-only with a content hash in the file name, and have the eval script refuse to run if the hash changes. Monitor: score on the frozen set plus a small held-out sample nobody tunes against.","**Daily metric plot** — throughout the box. How: run the eval script once a day (a scheduled CI job or a notebook) and append date, commit and score to a shared sheet. Monitor: slope over the last few days versus the required rate.","**Calendar-fixed midpoint review** — for any box of a week or more. How: book the midpoint and closing meetings with the decision-maker on day 0, and present the required-versus-observed arithmetic. Monitor: whether scope was cut at the midpoint when the curve was flat.","**Scope ladder in the tracker** — when a box has more than a few tasks. How: label each ticket must, should or could, and work riskiest-must first. Monitor: how often a must-have gets cut, since repeated cuts mean the question was too large."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"Name one question\"] --> B[\"Budget from value of answer\"]\n    B --> E[\"Write exit rule on day 0\"]\n    E --> S[\"Build eval set first\"]\n    S --> R[\"Riskiest must-have first\"]\n    R --> M{\"Midpoint: observed vs required rate\"}\n    M -->|\"on track\"| C[\"Drop could items, continue\"]\n    M -->|\"flat\"| P[\"Cut scope or change approach\"]\n    C --> X[\"Box closes: apply exit rule\"]\n    P --> X\n    X --> D[(\"Decision record\")]\n    D -->|\"go, pivot, stop\"| O[\"Next step\"]\n    D -.->|\"extend\"| N[\"New box, new question\"]","caption":"Read it top to bottom: the question and exit rule come before any work, and every path out of the box\nends in a recorded decision, never in a silent extension."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a timebox, and why does AI work need it more than ordinary software?","a":"- **Definition:** fixed time, flexible scope, and an explicit decision (ship, continue, pivot, stop) when\n  the time runs out.\n- **Why AI needs it:** quality improves asymptotically, so there is always one more prompt or model to try.\n  Without a box, a PoC stays \"almost there\" for months because the next tweak always looks promising."},{"q":"How would you decide whether a question deserves a one-day or a two-week box?","a":"- **Value of information:** box only if its cost is below the chance the answer changes the decision times\n  the cost of deciding wrongly.\n- **Concrete:** two weeks for three engineers is about 30 person-days, cheap against six months of a larger\n  team going the wrong way, but wasted if the team would choose the same either way."},{"q":"At the midpoint your metric moved from 86% to 87% over two days on a 100-document set. What do you conclude?","a":"- **Probably nothing.** One standard error at that accuracy on 100 documents is roughly 3-4 points, so a\n  1-point move can be a handful of borderline cases flipping.\n- **Then compare rates:** if the required rate to hit target exceeds the observed rate, cut scope, change\n  approach, or prepare a stop recommendation rather than grinding."},{"q":"When would you not timebox, and when would you break your own exit rule?","a":"- **Do not box** well-understood engineering work with requirements-fixed scope; estimation works there.\n- **Revise the rule once,** at the first checkpoint, if it was written before you understood the data, and\n  record why. Revising it at the end to match what was achieved is the failure the rule exists to prevent."},{"q":"A stakeholder asks for \"a few more days\" at the end of a box. How do you respond?","a":"- **Open a new box, do not slide.** Name a narrower question (for example only handwritten documents), its\n  own pass/fail threshold and a fixed length.\n- **Ground it in error analysis:** extend only if remaining errors cluster in a category with a concrete\n  fix; diffuse errors on a flat curve argue for a design that tolerates the current accuracy."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fixed time vs a working solution.** It gives predictable schedules and forces decisions, but you may stop just short of a working solution; this flips when the remaining gap is a known, well-scoped engineering problem.","**Short boxes vs trustworthy evidence.** They reduce sunk cost and expose dead ends early, but too-short boxes cannot produce a trustworthy eval set, so the decision rests on anecdotes instead of measurements.","**Pre-written exit criteria vs understanding the data.** They resist motivated reasoning, but can be wrong if written before you understood the data; allow one explicit revision at the first checkpoint, with the reason recorded.","**Cutting scope vs answering the question.** It keeps the date, but repeatedly cutting the same must-have item means the question was too large and should have been decomposed into smaller boxes."],"pitfalls":["**Silently extending the box.** \"Just two more days\" until the PoC has run for months; the symptom is a status update that says \"almost there\" three times in a row. Fix: extensions only as a new box.","**Timeboxing an activity instead of a question.** The box ends with work done but no decision; the symptom is a closing meeting where nobody can say whether to proceed.","**Tuning prompts before building the evaluation set.** Progress is judged by eyeballing outputs; the symptom is accuracy claims that collapse when someone runs a held-out sample.","**Sizing the box by feel.** Setting length by how long the work feels like it should take rather than by the value of the answer shows up as two-week investigations into decisions worth a day.","**Treating \"stop\" as a team failure.** Nobody ever recommends stopping; the symptom is an organisation whose boxes all end in \"promising, continue\" and never in a stop."],"when":"- **Use when:** the outcome is uncertain and the work could expand without limit: feasibility checks, PoCs,\n  model or vendor bake-offs, debugging an unexplained quality drop, and any research spike inside a\n  delivery project.\n- **Avoid when:** the engineering work is well understood; ordinary estimation is fine and the scope is\n  fixed by requirements.\n- **Measure:** progress by the metric the question names, plotted over the box, not by hours spent.\n- **Interviews and customer conversations:** a timebox is how you say \"we will know by Friday\" credibly:\n  name the question, the evidence and the decision rule up front.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the eval set first, plots the metric daily, and flags a flat curve at the midpoint instead of quietly trying more prompt variants.","architect":"Decides which unknowns get boxed and in what order, so the riskiest architectural assumption is tested before the team invests in surrounding infrastructure.","fde":"Sells a fixed-length discovery or PoC engagement with written exit criteria, which keeps the customer relationship anchored on a decision date rather than an open-ended promise."},"check":{"q":"Your two-week PoC ends at 84% accuracy against a 90% target, the team is confident another week will\nget there, and the customer is enthusiastic. What do you do?","a":"Go back to the exit rule written at the start. If it defined a middle band, follow it; if not, treat\nan extension as a new box, not a continuation. Ask what the error analysis says: if most remaining errors\ncluster in one or two identifiable categories with a concrete fix, a one-week box scoped only to those is\njustified, with its own pass/fail threshold. If errors are diffuse and the curve has flattened, another\nweek of tuning is unlikely to close a six-point gap, so recommend a design that tolerates 84% (human review,\nnarrower scope) instead. Enthusiasm is not evidence; the trajectory and error distribution are."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the defining feature of a timebox compared with an ordinary estimate?","options":["It uses smaller units of time, such as hours instead of days","Time is fixed and scope flexes, with an explicit decision when the time runs out","Scope is fixed and the deadline moves when new information appears","It removes the need for success criteria because the date is the criterion"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An estimate predicts how long fixed scope will take; a timebox fixes the time and ends in a decision. The date alone is not a criterion; you still need the question and exit rule to know what the result means."},{"q":"Why should exit criteria be written before the timeboxed work begins?","options":["So the team can ask the customer to sign them as part of the contract","Because criteria cannot be measured until the eval set exists","Because after investing effort, people anchor on sunk cost and criteria drift toward whatever was achieved","Because the model provider requires a target accuracy for evaluation access"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pre-commitment protects the decision from motivated reasoning. Contract signature can help but is not the reason; the core issue is that judgment after effort is biased toward continuing."},{"q":"At the midpoint of a two-week box, accuracy has been flat for three days. What is the best response?","options":["Keep going, since the plan allocated two weeks and the second week may bring gains","Immediately extend the box by a week to give the approach a fair chance","Treat it as an early signal: cut scope, change approach, or prepare a stop recommendation","Switch to measuring hours spent so the effort is visible to stakeholders"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Checkpoints exist to detect a flat trajectory early. Continuing unchanged spends the second week hoping; extending without a new question is a silent slide, and hours spent measure effort, not learning."}]},{"key":"scoping/demos","sources":[],"why":"- **Decision-makers judge by what they see.** Most people who fund, approve or adopt an AI system will never\n  read an eval report; they form their view in a meeting. That makes the demo one of the highest-leverage\n  artifacts in an engagement, and one of the most hazardous.\n- **The hazard is specific to generative AI.** Deterministic software that works once on stage mostly works\n  every time, because behavior is covered by tests. An LLM can give different outputs across runs, and\n  quality varies enormously across inputs. Finding five inputs where a prototype looks brilliant takes an\n  afternoon; making the median input good takes weeks.\n- **Curated demos create a gap that surfaces later.** Expectations built on those five inputs cannot be met,\n  and the gap shows up in the pilot as \"it worked in the demo\".\n- **The arithmetic forces design.** At 76% accuracy on real inputs, ten hand-picked successes imply close to\n  100%, a 24-point overstatement nobody said out loud. Five truly random inputs all succeed with chance\n  0.76⁵ ≈ 0.25, so three demos in four show a failure. The first misleads; the second leaves the story to\n  chance.\n- **Both naive alternatives fail.** The polished showcase (curated inputs, rehearsed path, everything green)\n  wins the meeting and loses the account. Refusing to demo until \"ready\" leaves stakeholders nothing to react\n  to, so workflow feedback arrives months late, when changes are expensive. The useful middle: designed\n  around a decision, representative inputs, honest boundaries, backed by numbers.","how":"### 1. Start from the decision and the audience\nWrite one sentence first: \"After this demo, *who* should be able to decide *what*?\" One demo rarely serves\nall audiences, because each decision needs different evidence.\n\n| Audience | Decision | Needs to see |\n|---|---|---|\n| Executive | fund a pilot? | business outcome and risk |\n| Operations lead | will her team use it? | her own workflow, including the awkward cases |\n| Security reviewer | approve it? | the data flow |\n\n### 2. Script around a real workflow with real data\n- **Use the customer's own** documents, tickets or records (with permission and appropriate handling),\n  arranged as the task a real user performs from start to finish.\n- **Why real data:** it carries the mess synthetic data lacks (scanned pages, abbreviations, missing fields),\n  and recognizable inputs make the audience trust what they see.\n\n### 3. Choose inputs representatively, and say how\n- **Pick from the evaluation set,** roughly in proportion to its error categories: some typical cases, one\n  hard case, and one case the system gets wrong or escalates.\n- **Selection is fine; *undisclosed* selection is the problem.**\n- **State the aggregate:** \"On 200 of your past tickets, it routed 87% correctly; here are three of them.\"\n  The demo is the anecdote, the eval is the evidence; showing both stops the anecdote standing in for the\n  evidence.\n\n### 4. Show the boundary on purpose\n- **Demonstrate uncertainty handling:** the system abstains, cites its sources, or hands off to a human.\n- **Why:** a controlled failure builds more trust than a flawless run, because experienced buyers know\n  nothing is flawless and want to see that failures are contained.\n\n### 5. Control the live risks\nLive demos of non-deterministic systems fail in ways rehearsals miss: provider latency spikes, rate limits,\na different output on the same input. Variance compounds across segments:\n\n```\nP(at least one live input goes wrong) = 1 − (per-input success)^(number of live inputs)\n                                      = 1 − 0.9⁴ ≈ 34%   for four inputs at 9 of 10 rehearsal runs\n```\n\n- **Rehearse each live input many times and measure its stability.** Inputs that flip between runs belong\n  in the recorded or narrated part, or are shown deliberately as a variance example.\n- **Lower sampling temperature for the demo path** where the model exposes it. It reduces variance but does\n  not guarantee identical outputs, and some reasoning models do not accept a temperature setting.\n- **Pre-warm caches,** keep a recorded backup of each segment, and test on the actual network you will\n  present from.\n- **Say when output is cached or recorded,** because hidden caching also hides real latency.\n\n### 6. Label what is real\n- **Separate built from mocked:** \"The extraction is live; the CRM write-back is a stub.\"\n- **Why:** any UI, integration or data source that is faked becomes an implied commitment unless labeled.\n\n### 7. Capture the reaction as data\n- **Assign a note-taker** for questions, objections and feature requests. These are discovery inputs.\n- **They often reveal the real success criterion,** such as \"the summary is nice, but what I need is the\n  deadline date\".","example":"### Picking and checking demo inputs from the eval set\nSelects a disclosed, representative mix and computes the odds that a live run goes wrong.\n\n```python\nimport random\n\ndef pick_demo_inputs(eval_rows, n_typical=3, seed=7):\n    \"\"\"eval_rows: dicts with 'id', 'correct' (bool) and 'category' ('typical' or 'hard').\"\"\"\n    rng = random.Random(seed)                       # fixed seed: the same picks at every rehearsal\n    typical = [r for r in eval_rows if r[\"correct\"] and r[\"category\"] == \"typical\"]\n    hard = [r for r in eval_rows if r[\"correct\"] and r[\"category\"] == \"hard\"]\n    wrong = [r for r in eval_rows if not r[\"correct\"]]\n    picks = rng.sample(typical, n_typical) + rng.sample(hard, 1) + rng.sample(wrong, 1)\n    acc = sum(r[\"correct\"] for r in eval_rows) / len(eval_rows)\n    line = f\"On {len(eval_rows)} of your past tickets it was right {acc:.0%}; here are {len(picks)}.\"\n    return [r[\"id\"] for r in picks], line\n\ndef p_live_ok(stability):\n    \"\"\"stability: per live input, share of rehearsal runs that gave the intended result.\"\"\"\n    p = 1.0\n    for s in stability:\n        p *= s\n    return p\n\nrows = [{\"id\": i, \"correct\": i < 228, \"category\": \"hard\" if i % 10 == 0 else \"typical\"}\n        for i in range(300)]                        # 228 of 300 correct = 76%\nids, line = pick_demo_inputs(rows)\nprint(ids, line)        # five ids; \"On 300 of your past tickets it was right 76%; here are 5.\"\nprint(f\"5 random inputs all succeed: {0.76 ** 5:.2f}\")                   # 0.25\nprint(f\"4 live inputs, one goes wrong: {1 - p_live_ok([0.9] * 4):.0%}\")  # 34%\n```\n\n### A 25-minute contract-review demo\nFor a legal operations team deciding whether to run a six-week pilot of a contract-review assistant.\n\n| Minutes | Segment | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0–3 | The decision on the table and today's process: 40 minutes per NDA review, two reviewers | Anchor on their baseline |\n| 3–10 | Live run on three of *their* past NDAs: two typical, one with an unusual indemnity clause | Show the real workflow |\n| 10–14 | Results on 120 historical NDAs: clause-flag recall 91%, precision 84%, misses listed by type | Evidence behind the anecdote |\n| 14–18 | A document where the model is unsure: it flags low confidence and routes to a lawyer | Show the boundary |\n| 18–21 | What is live vs. stubbed (the document-management integration is mocked) | Prevent implied commitments |\n| 21–25 | Proposed pilot scope, success metric and their questions | Turn reaction into a decision |\n\n- **The figures are illustrative.**\n- **Note what is absent:** no curated \"wow\" prompt, no unexplained accuracy claim, and no integration shown\n  as working when it is not.\n- **Safety nets:** the live segment has a recorded fallback, and the session ends on the decision it was\n  built to inform.","patterns":["**Demo manifest in the repo** — for any demo you will repeat. How: a small config file listing the eval-set input ids, model version, prompt version and sampling settings, so every rehearsal runs the same path. Monitor: that the manifest's eval-set accuracy matches the number you quote on the slide.","**Rehearsal stability run** — the day before. How: run each live input 10-20 times through the same build and count intended results; anything below your bar moves to the recorded segment. Monitor: per-input stability and p95 latency on the presenting network.","**Recorded fallback per segment** — for every live segment. How: a screen recording from the final rehearsal, cued up in a second window and introduced as recorded. Monitor: how often the fallback is used, which flags provider or network risk for the pilot.","**Visible live-versus-stub labels** — whenever anything is mocked. How: a banner or badge on stubbed screens driven by the same feature flag that switches the stub, plus a slide listing what is stubbed. Monitor: SOW drafts for items that only exist as stubs.","**No demo branch** — always. How: the demo runs the same build the pilot will use; code review rejects special cases keyed on demo input ids or prompts tuned to those documents. Monitor: demo inputs' results on the pilot build match the rehearsal.","**Feedback log into discovery** — every demo. How: a named note-taker records questions and objections in a shared doc, triaged into the pilot backlog within a day. Monitor: how many requests change the success metric or scope."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[\"Who decides what\"] --> W[\"Real workflow, real data\"]\n    W --> S[\"Pick inputs from eval set\"]\n    S --> R[\"Rehearse each input many times\"]\n    R --> Q{\"Stable across runs?\"}\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| L[\"Live segment\"]\n    Q -->|\"no\"| F[\"Recorded segment, said so\"]\n    L --> A[\"Show aggregate eval number\"]\n    F --> A\n    A --> B[\"Show a contained failure\"]\n    B --> K[\"Label live vs stubbed\"]\n    K --> C[(\"Captured reactions\")]\n    C --> X[\"Decision on the table\"]","caption":"Follow the path from the decision to the room: inputs come from the eval set, unstable inputs drop to a\nlabeled recording, and the session ends in a decision plus captured feedback."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is demoing an LLM system riskier than demoing conventional software?","a":"- **Quality is a distribution, not a constant.** Outputs vary across inputs and across runs, so a few good\n  cases say little about the median input the pilot will see.\n- **Numbers:** at 76% accuracy, five random inputs all succeed only about a quarter of the time, while ten\n  curated successes imply near 100%; the demo has to be designed, not left to curation or chance."},{"q":"Your live demo segment returned a different, worse answer than in rehearsal. What went wrong and what do you change?","a":"- **Likely cause:** run-to-run variance that rehearsal under-sampled; four inputs at 9 of 10 stability\n  fail somewhere about a third of the time.\n- **Fix:** rehearse each input many times, lower temperature where the model allows it, move unstable inputs\n  to a labeled recording, and keep a recorded fallback ready for every live segment."},{"q":"When would you choose a recorded demo over a live one?","a":"- **Choose recorded** when the network, VPN or provider is unreliable, or an input is unstable, provided you\n  say it is recorded and quote real latency from the eval runs.\n- **Choose live** when credibility is the main question and the path is rehearsed and stable, because the\n  audience sees real behavior and real latency."},{"q":"How do you stop a demo from turning into an unestimated specification?","a":"- **Label everything mocked,** on screen and on a slide, because unlabeled stubs become implied commitments.\n- **Run the pilot build, not a demo branch,** so nothing shown depends on special cases.\n- **Review the SOW** for items that cite \"as shown in the demo\" and estimate or remove them."},{"q":"How would you measure whether a demo worked?","a":"- **By the decision it produced:** did the audience decide what the demo was built to inform, such as\n  funding a six-week pilot?\n- **By the feedback captured:** objections and requests logged and triaged, especially any that change the\n  success criterion, rather than by applause in the room."}],"tradeoffs":["**Live credibility vs visible failure.** Live demos build credibility because the audience sees real behavior, but risk visible failure; recorded segments are safer when the network or provider is unreliable, provided you say they are recorded.","**Honest failures vs initial excitement.** Showing failures costs some excitement but pays back in the pilot; the calculation flips only in pure exploratory sessions where nobody is making a commitment.","**Early feedback vs anchoring.** Early, rough demos surface workflow feedback while it is cheap to act on, but risk anchoring stakeholders on unfinished UI; label rough parts explicitly to keep feedback focused.","**Tailored vs reusable.** A demo tailored to one audience is persuasive for that decision, but reusing it for a different audience usually answers the wrong question and should prompt a rebuild."],"pitfalls":["**Hand-picked inputs easier than real traffic.** The symptom is a pilot whose users say the system \"worked better in the demo\".","**Unlabeled mocked integrations or UI.** They become commitments; the symptom is a statement of work that includes features nobody estimated.","**Synthetic or public data instead of the customer's own.** The audience cannot judge relevance and the hard cases never appear; the symptom is polite approval and no follow-up.","**Rehearsing only on a fast office network.** Then presenting through a customer VPN or proxy; the symptom is timeouts or long spinners during the live segment.","**Treating the demo as the evaluation.** No aggregate number behind it; the symptom is stakeholders quoting a single impressive example as the expected performance.","**Special-casing the demo inputs in code.** A hard-coded lookup, or a prompt tuned to those three documents; the symptom is the same inputs behaving differently once the pilot build replaces the demo branch.","**Letting demo screens become the specification.** The symptom is change requests and SOW lines that cite \"as shown in the demo\" for behavior nobody designed or estimated."],"when":"- **Use at decision points:** after discovery to confirm you understood the workflow, at the end of a PoC to\n  decide on a pilot, during a pilot to show progress against the metric, and before production for sign-off.\n- **Demo early and roughly** when the main risk is building the wrong thing; **later and with evidence**\n  when the main risk is overpromising.\n- **Avoid when:** you would use the demo to substitute for an evaluation, or the audience's decision is not\n  identified.\n- **Measure:** whether the demo produced a clear decision and actionable feedback, not applause.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds a reproducible demo path from the eval set, with fixed settings, warmed caches and a recorded fallback, and makes sure the aggregate numbers shown are real.","architect":"Decides what the demo claims about the target architecture and labels which components are prototype stand-ins, so the demo does not quietly redefine the design.","fde":"Runs demos inside the customer's environment on their data, reads the room for the real success criterion, and turns objections into scoped pilot requirements."},"check":{"q":"A sales lead asks you to demo a support-ticket triage prototype to the customer's VP next week, using\nthe ten examples that \"look best\". The prototype's accuracy on a 300-ticket eval set is 76%. How do you\nbuild the demo?","a":"First pin down the VP's decision, probably whether to fund a pilot. Then pick demo tickets from the\neval set to reflect the real mix: a few typical correct cases, one hard case, and one wrong or escalated\ncase showing how errors are caught. Present the 76% alongside the current baseline and what the pilot would\naim to improve, so the demo sets expectations the pilot can meet. Ten best-looking examples would win the\nmeeting but create a gap that surfaces as distrust during the pilot. Label anything mocked, run with a\nrecorded fallback, and close on the pilot proposal."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are cherry-picked demos a bigger risk for LLM systems than for conventional software?","options":["LLM systems are always slower, so demos need shorter inputs","Output quality varies widely across inputs and runs, so a few good cases say little about typical behavior","Customers cannot see the source code of an LLM system","LLM providers forbid showing failure cases in public demos"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Deterministic software that works once usually works every time. An LLM system's quality is a distribution over inputs, so curated examples can sit far above the median the pilot will experience."},{"q":"What is the main reason to show a failure or low-confidence case during a demo?","options":["It makes the demo shorter by skipping the full workflow","It proves the model is not overfitted to the demo inputs","It shows how errors are contained, which builds trust with buyers who know no system is flawless","It satisfies regulatory requirements for AI transparency in all jurisdictions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Experienced stakeholders expect errors; what they need to see is abstention, citations or human hand-off. One failure does not prove absence of overfitting, and no general rule requires it in demos."},{"q":"You show a recorded segment because the customer network is unreliable. What should you do?","options":["Present it as live to avoid undermining confidence","Skip the latency discussion entirely","Say it is recorded and give the real latency measured from the eval runs","Re-record it at a higher speed so it fits the time slot"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Recorded output is a legitimate fallback, but hiding it misrepresents latency and reliability. Stating it and giving measured numbers keeps the demo honest and the expectations accurate."}]},{"key":"scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc","sources":[],"why":"- **Two uncertainties no spec removes.** *Desirability*: do users want an assistant that drafts replies, or\n  a better search box? *Feasibility*: can a model hit the accuracy, latency and cost the use case needs on\n  this customer's data? Neither is settled by discussion.\n- **Specs hide both.** A written spec for \"an AI that summarizes claims files\" hides whether the summaries\n  are useful and whether the model gets the dates right on scanned forms.\n- **Committing to the full build is expensive in the worst way.** Integration, UI, auth and deployment,\n  most of the budget, get done before anyone learns the core model step reaches 70% when the business needs\n  95%.\n- **Endless notebook exploration fails the other way.** With no criteria it produces impressive anecdotes\n  and no decision.\n- **Prototypes and PoCs sit in between.** Deliberately incomplete builds aimed at one uncertainty at a time,\n  so you spend days rather than months learning whether the idea survives users and data. They are cheap\n  because they skip everything not under test, which is also why they must not be mistaken for the product.","how":"### Prototype vs. PoC: different questions\n| | Prototype | PoC |\n|---|---|---|\n| Question | what should the experience be? | can this work technically? |\n| Tests | workflow, interaction, useful output format | model, data, metric |\n| Can fake | yes, even the AI (\"Wizard of Oz\": a person produces the output behind the scenes), because the question is about the human | no: real model, real data, real metric, because faking any invalidates the answer |\n\n- **Do not mix the two questions** into one build; it usually answers neither well.\n- **Neither is a pilot** (real users doing real work, measuring business impact) **or an MVP** (the smallest\n  version you would actually ship and support). A PoC that passes earns a pilot, not a launch.\n\n### 1. Find the riskiest assumption\n- **List what must be true:** the data is accessible, the model can read these documents, latency fits the\n  workflow, cost per transaction fits the budget, users trust the output.\n- **Rank** by likelihood of being false times cost if false.\n- **Target the top item;** everything else can be stubbed.\n\n### 2. State a hypothesis with a threshold\n- **Example:** \"Using a general-purpose model with retrieval over the policy manual, at least 85% of answers\n  to 100 real employee questions will be judged correct and grounded.\"\n- **The threshold comes from the business need,** because \"it works pretty well\" cannot be passed or failed.\n\n### 3. Build the eval set from real data first\n- **Collect real inputs,** including messy ones, and label expected outputs with domain experts.\n- **Scale:** fifty to a few hundred examples is common for a PoC, enough to see error categories, though\n  small sets give wide confidence intervals.\n- **It comes first** because every later decision is read off this set.\n\nSize it with the binomial interval for a measured accuracy *p* on *n* examples:\n\n```\n95% half-width ≈ 1.96 × sqrt(p(1 − p) / n)\nn ≈ 1.96² × p(1 − p) / E²     (examples needed for half-width E)\n```\n\n| *p* = 0.85, examples | 95% half-width |\n|---|---|\n| 100 | about ±7 points |\n| 400 | about ±3.5 points |\n| roughly 550 | ±3 points |\n\n- **Rule of thumb:** if the business threshold and the likely result are close, the set has to be large; if\n  far apart, 100 is plenty.\n- **Split before tuning:** a *dev* portion you read and iterate on, and a *held-out* portion nobody looks at\n  until the readout.\n- **Why the split:** every prompt change chosen because it fixed dev failures fits the system to those\n  examples, so the dev score drifts upward faster than true accuracy. The held-out number is the one the\n  decision uses.\n\n### 4. Build the thinnest end-to-end slice\n- **Wire input to output** with the least code possible.\n- **Start with the strongest available model** to establish the ceiling, because if the best\n  general-purpose model cannot do it with your prompt and retrieval, cheaper off-the-shelf models almost\n  certainly will not. Cost optimization comes after feasibility.\n- **The ceiling is approach-specific, not absolute.** Fine-tuning, a specialized component (say, a dedicated\n  OCR or table parser) or a redesigned pipeline can beat it, so a failed PoC rules out the approach tested,\n  not the use case.\n\n### 5. Iterate with error analysis, inside a timebox\n1. **Read failures** on the dev split and group them.\n2. **Fix the largest group, re-run,** because fixes land roughly one category at a time, so the biggest\n   category is the biggest available gain.\n3. **Track latency and cost per example** alongside accuracy, since a design that passes on quality but\n   takes 40 seconds per answer has not passed.\n4. **Stop** when the threshold is met, the curve flattens, or the box closes.\n\n### 6. Write the findings and decide\n- **Record** the result, the error breakdown, what was stubbed, and what production would require.\n- **Decide:** proceed to pilot, pivot the design, or stop.\n- **Disposable vs evolutionary:** a disposable prototype gets thrown away; an evolutionary one is kept only\n  if it was built with that intent.","example":"### A minimal PoC harness\nReal labeled examples, one function under test, a pre-written threshold, and a decision printed at the end.\nThe model call is a placeholder for whichever provider SDK you use.\n\n```python\nimport json, math, collections, time\n\nTHRESHOLD = 0.85  # written down before any tuning, from the business requirement\n\ndef call_model(question: str, context: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Placeholder: call your LLM provider here with a fixed prompt version.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n\ndef retrieve(question: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Placeholder: the thinnest retrieval that could work (e.g. BM25 over the manual).\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n\ndef judge(pred: str, expected: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Start with expert labels or a strict string/regex check; add an LLM judge later if needed.\"\"\"\n    return expected.lower() in pred.lower()\n\ndef run_poc(path: str = \"eval_set.jsonl\", split: str = \"dev\") -> None:\n    rows = [json.loads(l) for l in open(path)]           # {\"q\", \"expected\", \"category\", \"split\"}\n    rows = [r for r in rows if r[\"split\"] == split]      # iterate on dev; read held-out once\n    errors, correct, secs = collections.Counter(), 0, []\n    for r in rows:\n        t0 = time.monotonic()\n        pred = call_model(r[\"q\"], retrieve(r[\"q\"]))\n        secs.append(time.monotonic() - t0)\n        if judge(pred, r[\"expected\"]):\n            correct += 1\n        else:\n            errors[r[\"category\"]] += 1\n    n = len(rows); acc = correct / n\n    half = 1.96 * math.sqrt(acc * (1 - acc) / n)          # binomial 95% half-width\n    p95 = sorted(secs)[int(0.95 * (n - 1))]\n    print(f\"[{split}] accuracy {acc:.1%} ±{half:.1%} on {n} real examples, p95 {p95:.1f}s\")\n    print(\"errors by category:\", errors.most_common())\n    if split == \"heldout\":\n        verdict = (\"pass\" if acc - half >= THRESHOLD else\n                   \"fail\" if acc + half < THRESHOLD else \"inconclusive: need more data\")\n        print(\"DECISION:\", verdict)\n```\n\n- **The threshold is a constant set before the loop.**\n- **Errors are grouped by category,** so the next iteration targets the biggest one.\n- **Latency is measured, not assumed.**\n- **The decision prints only for the held-out split,** with an explicit \"inconclusive\" band when the interval\n  straddles the threshold.\n- **Worked readout:** on a 200-example held-out split, 88% gives about ±4.5 points, so the verdict is\n  \"inconclusive\" against 85%. That is the honest answer: label more data, do not celebrate.\n\n### Sizing the eval set before you label\nRun this before asking experts to label, so you request enough examples to make the decision resolvable.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef half_width(p, n, z=1.96):\n    return z * math.sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n)\n\ndef n_needed(p, e, z=1.96):\n    return math.ceil(z * z * p * (1 - p) / (e * e))\n\nfor n in (100, 200, 400):\n    print(f\"p=0.85, n={n}: ±{half_width(0.85, n):.1%}\")   # ±7.0%, ±4.9%, ±3.5%\nprint(\"n for ±3 points at p=0.85:\", n_needed(0.85, 0.03))  # 545\nprint(f\"p=0.88, n=200: ±{half_width(0.88, 200):.1%}\")      # ±4.5%\n```\n\n| Likely result vs threshold | Gap | Examples worth labeling |\n|---|---|---|\n| 95% vs 85% | 10 points | ~100 resolves it |\n| 88% vs 85% | 3 points | ~550, or accept a pilot with the risk stated |","patterns":["**Locked held-out split** — every PoC. How: assign each example to dev or held-out once (for example by a stable hash of its id) and store the split in the file, so nobody re-splits after seeing results. Monitor: the gap between dev and held-out accuracy; a widening gap means you are fitting the dev set.","**Versioned prompt and model in every result row** — while iterating. How: log prompt version, model id, retrieval settings and commit with each run's score and latency, so the readout cites an exact configuration. Monitor: which change moved which error category.","**Strongest-model ceiling, then a cost pass** — when feasibility is the risk. How: first run with the most capable model you can access, then re-run the same held-out set on cheaper candidates. Monitor: accuracy drop per cost saved; skip this order when cost itself is the riskiest assumption.","**Random sample from the real source** — before building the eval set. How: ask for a random draw across the full archive or ticket history, including old scans and edge formats, not a curated folder. Monitor: the share of examples by format or age compared with production.","**Throwaway repo for disposable code** — for prototypes. How: keep prototype code in a separate repository or folder marked disposable, so it cannot be merged into the product by accident. Monitor: any import of prototype modules from production code."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Rank assumptions, pick riskiest\"] --> H[\"Hypothesis plus threshold\"]\n    H --> E[(\"Real eval set: dev and held-out\")]\n    E --> S[\"Thinnest slice, strongest model\"]\n    S --> L[\"Run on dev split\"]\n    L --> G[\"Group errors, fix largest\"]\n    G -->|\"curve still rising, box open\"| L\n    G -->|\"threshold met or curve flat\"| R[\"Read held-out once\"]\n    R --> V{\"Interval vs threshold\"}\n    V -->|\"above\"| P[\"Pass: propose pilot\"]\n    V -->|\"straddles\"| I[\"Inconclusive: label more\"]\n    V -->|\"below\"| F[\"Fail: pivot or stop\"]","caption":"The PoC path: one risky assumption, a threshold and a held-out split fixed before tuning, a dev-only\niteration loop, then a single held-out readout that ends in pass, fail or inconclusive."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a prototype, a PoC, a pilot and an MVP?","a":"- **Prototype:** tests the experience and may fake the AI, because the question is about users.\n- **PoC:** tests feasibility with the real model, data and metric against a pre-set threshold.\n- **Pilot and MVP:** a pilot puts real users on real work to measure business impact; an MVP is the smallest\n  version you would ship and support. A passing PoC earns a pilot, not a launch."},{"q":"How do you choose what a PoC should test?","a":"- **List the assumptions that must hold,** such as data access, document parsing, latency, cost and trust.\n- **Rank by likelihood of being false times cost if false,** and point the PoC at the top item while stubbing\n  everything else, because proving the easy part leaves the real blocker for after the contract."},{"q":"Your dev accuracy rose from 78% to 91% over a week but the held-out set says 82%. What happened?","a":"- **Overfitting to dev:** each prompt change chosen to fix dev failures fits the system to those examples,\n  so the dev score drifts up faster than true accuracy.\n- **Response:** report the held-out number, check which error categories differ between splits, and if needed\n  draw a fresh sample from the real source to confirm."},{"q":"How many examples does a PoC eval set need?","a":"- **Depends on the gap to the threshold.** At 85% accuracy, 100 examples give about ±7 points, 400 give about\n  ±3.5, and ±3 points needs roughly 550.\n- **So** if the expected result is far from the threshold, 100 is plenty; if close, label more or report the\n  result as inconclusive."},{"q":"When would you skip a PoC entirely?","a":"- **When the pattern is proven:** the same approach already runs in production elsewhere on similar data, so\n  a pilot is the cheaper first step.\n- **When the risk is desirability, not feasibility:** a Wizard-of-Oz prototype answers that faster without\n  any model work."}],"tradeoffs":["**Disposable speed vs shipping temptation.** Disposable prototypes are fast because they ignore quality, but teams are tempted to ship them; keep the code only when it was built evolutionarily with tests and structure from the start.","**Strongest model first vs hidden cost.** It establishes feasibility quickly but can hide a cost problem; this flips when cost per transaction is itself the riskiest assumption and must be tested first.","**Wizard of Oz vs feasibility.** Wizard-of-Oz prototypes answer desirability without any model work, but say nothing about feasibility; use them only when the user experience is the main unknown.","**Small eval sets vs firm decisions.** They let a PoC finish in days, but with 100 examples the sampling error on accuracy is several points, so a near-threshold result needs more data before a firm decision."],"pitfalls":["**Clean sample data the vendor or customer prepared.** Not production-like; the symptom is accuracy that drops sharply in the pilot on scanned, partial or messy inputs.","**Proving the easy part.** Showing a chat UI works while the hard assumption (data access, a rare document type, latency) stays untested; the symptom is surprise blockers after the contract.","**Defining success after seeing the results.** The PoC always \"succeeds\"; the symptom is a criterion that suspiciously matches the number achieved.","**PoC purgatory.** A successful PoC with no owner, budget or plan for the next stage; the symptom is a well-received demo followed by months of silence.","**Shipping the prototype code because \"it already works\".** It brings hard-coded credentials, no retries and no tests; the symptom is the first production incident.","**Tuning and reporting on the same examples.** Reported accuracy is fitted to the eval set; the symptom is a readout number several points above what a fresh sample or the pilot shows."],"when":"- **Prototype when:** the main unknown is what users need or how they will interact.\n- **PoC when:** the main unknown is whether the model can meet a quantified bar on real data.\n- **Do both** before committing to an integration-heavy build, and inside a timebox.\n- **Skip when:** the task is well understood and the same pattern already runs in production elsewhere with\n  similar data; a pilot may then be the cheaper first step.\n- **Measure:** a PoC by the decision it enabled and the risks it retired, not by how polished it looks.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the eval harness and thinnest slice, tracks error categories per iteration, and keeps prototype code clearly separated from anything meant to survive.","architect":"Picks which assumption the PoC must retire and judges whether a passing result actually transfers to the production architecture, data volumes and constraints.","fde":"Gets real customer data into the PoC early, negotiates the success threshold with the customer up front, and turns the findings into a pilot proposal."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants a PoC of an LLM that answers questions over their 40,000-page engineering archive.\nTheir IT team offers a curated folder of 200 clean PDFs to make it fast. What do you do, and why?","a":"Accept the curated folder only for wiring the slice, not for the decision. The riskiest assumptions\nare probably document parsing (scans, drawings, tables) and retrieval over a large, near-duplicate corpus,\nwhich a clean 200-PDF set would hide. Ask for a random sample across the archive, including old scans, and\nbuild the eval questions with engineers who actually ask them. State the threshold up front. If full\naccess is impossible in the timebox, report results as conditional on the sample and list what remains\nuntested. Otherwise the PoC passes and the pilot fails on the very data the curated set excluded."},"quiz":[{"q":"What best distinguishes a PoC from a rapid prototype?","options":["A PoC is always longer and more expensive than a prototype","A PoC tests technical feasibility against criteria on real data; a prototype tests what the experience should be","A prototype must use the production model while a PoC can use a mock","A PoC is shown to executives while a prototype is internal only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The difference is the question asked. A prototype can fake the AI because it studies users; a PoC cannot fake the model or data because feasibility is exactly what it measures."},{"q":"Why start a PoC with the strongest available model rather than the cheapest?","options":["Cheap models are not allowed on customer data","The strongest model is always cheapest per correct answer","It establishes a ceiling for the approach: if the best model fails, cheaper off-the-shelf ones almost certainly will too","Strong models need no evaluation set"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Feasibility first: the strongest model bounds what this prompt-and-retrieval approach can achieve, and cost can be optimized later. The exception is when cost itself is the riskiest assumption, in which case it must be tested directly. Fine-tuning or specialized components can still exceed that ceiling."},{"q":"A PoC reaches 86% against an 85% threshold on 100 examples. What is the soundest conclusion?","options":["The PoC has clearly passed and the team should commit to full production","The PoC failed because results should exceed the threshold by at least ten points","It is a marginal pass; sampling error of several points means more data is needed before a firm commitment","The threshold was wrong and should be raised to 90%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With 100 examples, accuracy near 85% has a 95% interval of roughly ±7 points, so 86% is consistent with true performance below threshold. Collect more examples or proceed to a pilot with that risk stated."}]},{"key":"scoping/pilot-to-production-plan","sources":[],"why":"- **A PoC cannot answer the adoption questions.** It shows \"can it work?\" on a sample, not whether people use\n  it daily, whether it moves the business metric, or what breaks under real traffic, real permissions and\n  real edge cases. Only real use answers those, which is what a pilot is for.\n- **Pilots succeed and then nothing happens.** The pilot ran on a champion team, with an engineer on call in\n  a chat channel, in a sandbox exempted from security review, paid from an innovation budget.\n- **Production needs what the pilot skipped.** SSO, a security sign-off, an operations owner, a support\n  process, a cost line in someone's budget, and training for users who did not volunteer. Unplanned, the\n  pilot's success creates demand nobody can meet: pilot purgatory.\n- **Both naive alternatives fail.** Skipping the pilot means the first real measurement happens at full blast\n  radius, with no baseline comparison and no easy way back. An open-ended pilot \"to learn\" never reaches a\n  moment where evidence is weighed and a decision made.\n- **The plan makes the pilot a measured experiment** with a pre-agreed path to scale, so success triggers\n  production work that is already scoped and funded.","how":"### 1. Define the stages and what each proves\nA common progression widens exposure, and each stage answers a different question. Name the question for\neach stage, because a stage without a question has no natural end.\n\n| Stage | Question it answers |\n|---|---|\n| PoC | feasibility |\n| Pilot | real-workflow impact |\n| Limited rollout | operability at scale |\n| General availability | full adoption |\n\nAI systems need a second axis besides exposure: *autonomy*. Each step up removes a human safety net, so\neach needs its own evidence.\n\n| Autonomy level | What happens | Measures |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Shadow mode** | outputs logged and scored, never shown or used | accuracy on live traffic with zero user impact; not adoption or workflow effect, because nobody uses the output |\n| **Assist** | a human sees the suggestion and decides | adoption, edit rates, workflow effect |\n| **Act with review** | it acts; a human samples or can reverse | error escapes |\n| **Act alone** | only on the subset where evidence supports it | ongoing error rate |\n\n### 2. Measure the baseline before the pilot starts\n- **Capture the current metric** (handle time, error rate, cycle time) for the pilot cohort and a comparison\n  group *before* switching anything on.\n- **Why:** without a baseline any post-pilot number is uninterpretable, because you cannot separate the\n  system's effect from seasonality or the attention of being piloted.\n\nWith both groups measured before and during, the estimate is a difference-in-differences:\n\n```\neffect = (pilot_during − pilot_before) − (comparison_during − comparison_before)\n```\n\n- **The second term removes whatever moved both groups at once** (a quiet season, a policy change, a new\n  ticket mix), which a plain before/after comparison silently credits to the system.\n\n### 3. Choose the cohort deliberately\n- **Volunteers inflate results.** Pilot cohorts tend to be enthusiastic volunteers; include some typical\n  users and, if possible, keep a comparable group without the tool.\n- **Size it so the metric can move detectably** within the pilot's duration. A common rule of thumb for\n  comparing two group means at 95% confidence and 80% power:\n\n```\nn per group ≈ 16 × σ² / δ²      (σ = per-unit standard deviation, δ = effect you must detect)\n```\n\n- **Correction 1, the unit is what you assign the tool to.** If whole teams get it, outcomes within a team\n  are correlated, so the effective sample is much closer to the number of teams than the number of tickets.\n  A two-team pilot can show a large effect that is partly just \"team A is faster\".\n- **Correction 2, novelty.** Early weeks carry a novelty effect, as users try the tool eagerly or slowly at\n  first; plan enough duration to see the weekly trend settle rather than reading the first fortnight.\n\n### 4. Write success, guardrail and exit criteria\n- **Success metrics:** the business outcome the system is meant to move.\n- **Guardrail metrics:** what must not get worse (customer satisfaction, error escapes, compliance\n  incidents, cost per transaction).\n- **Exit criteria:** the go/no-go rule, e.g. \"median handle time down ≥ 15% with no guardrail regression\n  over four weeks\".\n- **Stop triggers:** conditions that halt the pilot early, such as a data leak or a severe error.\n- **Why guardrails:** AI systems often improve the headline metric by shifting cost elsewhere, for instance\n  faster replies with more escalations.\n\n### 5. Build the production-gap list during the pilot, not after\n- **List everything production needs that the pilot skipped:** identity integration, security and privacy\n  review, data residency, monitoring and alerting, on-call, runbooks, support process, cost budget and\n  quotas, evaluation regression gates, user training.\n- **Each item gets an owner and an estimate.**\n- **Run them in parallel with the pilot,** which is what prevents purgatory, because most are slow\n  organizational processes, not code.\n\n### 6. Name owners and the handoff\n- **Decide who owns it after launch:** which team runs it, who funds inference cost, who triages quality\n  issues.\n- **External builders** (an FDE team, a vendor) make the handoff plan part of the production plan.\n\n### 7. Hold the go/no-go and roll out in steps\n1. **Compare against the exit criteria** at the end of the pilot, in a scheduled meeting with the decision\n   makers.\n2. **If go, expand in increments** with rollback triggers.\n3. **Follow with hypercare:** a period of heightened monitoring and fast response.","example":"### Pilot readout calculator\nComputes the difference-in-differences, the false-alarm rate of a rollback trigger, and how clustering\ninflates the sample you need.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef did(pilot_before, pilot_during, comp_before, comp_during):\n    \"\"\"Difference-in-differences: the pilot's change minus the comparison group's change.\"\"\"\n    effect = (pilot_during - pilot_before) - (comp_during - comp_before)\n    return effect, effect / pilot_before\n\ndef norm_cdf(z):\n    return 0.5 * (1 + math.erf(z / math.sqrt(2)))\n\ndef p_below(threshold, true_rate, n):\n    \"\"\"Chance a sample of n audited tickets shows a pass rate under threshold (normal approximation).\"\"\"\n    sd = math.sqrt(true_rate * (1 - true_rate) / n)\n    return norm_cdf((threshold - true_rate) / sd)\n\neffect, rel = did(11.0, 9.1, 11.0, 10.8)\nprint(f\"effect {effect:+.1f} min ({rel:+.1%}); naive before/after {(9.1 - 11.0) / 11.0:+.1%}\")\nprint(f\"false alarm, 90% trigger on 200: {p_below(0.90, 0.91, 200):.0%}\")    # ~31%\nprint(f\"false alarm, 88% trigger on 400: {p_below(0.88, 0.91, 400):.1%}\")    # ~1.8%\nprint(f\"catch a real drop to 85% on 400: {p_below(0.88, 0.85, 400):.0%}\")    # ~95%\n\ndef n_per_group(sigma, delta, cluster_size=1, icc=0.0):\n    \"\"\"16 sigma^2 / delta^2 (95% confidence, 80% power), inflated by the design effect for clusters.\"\"\"\n    return math.ceil(16 * sigma**2 / delta**2 * (1 + (cluster_size - 1) * icc))\n\nprint(n_per_group(sigma=4.0, delta=1.65))                              # 95 independent tickets\nprint(n_per_group(sigma=4.0, delta=1.65, cluster_size=200, icc=0.05))  # 1030 when grouped by agent\n```\n\n- **Design effect:** `1 + (cluster_size − 1) × ICC` is the standard inflation for correlated units. The σ of 4\n  minutes and ICC of 0.05 are illustrative; estimate both from baseline data.\n\n### Worked scenario: a support-drafting pilot at an insurer\nA ticket drafting assistant for an insurer's customer service team. All figures are illustrative.\n\n- **Baseline (weeks −2 to 0):** two pilot teams (30 agents) and two comparison teams; median handle time\n  11.0 minutes, quality-audit pass rate 92%, CSAT 4.2/5.\n- **Pilot (weeks 1–6):** pilot teams use AI drafts; agents edit and send. Weekly audit of 200 randomly\n  sampled AI-assisted tickets.\n- **Audit precision:** at a pass rate near 92%, a 200-ticket sample estimates it to within about ±4 points\n  at 95% confidence, enough to catch a large regression but not a one-point drift.\n- **Exit criteria (agreed week 0):** handle time down ≥ 15% relative to the comparison teams; audit pass rate\n  not below 90%; CSAT not below 4.1; inference cost under the per-ticket budget agreed with finance.\n- **Stop trigger:** any instance of a draft exposing another customer's data.\n\n| Production-gap item | Owner | Estimate |\n|---|---|---|\n| SSO and role mapping | IT | 3 weeks |\n| Privacy review of prompt logging | legal | 4 weeks |\n| Monitoring dashboards and alerts | platform team | |\n| Runbook and on-call rota | support engineering | |\n| Training for 400 agents | enablement | |\n| Cost centre for inference | finance | |\n\n**Week 6 result.**\n\n| Metric | Pilot | Comparison | Exit bar |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Median handle time | 9.1 minutes | 10.8 minutes | ≥ 15% relative cut |\n| Audit pass rate | 91% | | not below 90% |\n| CSAT | 4.2 | | not below 4.1 |\n\n- **Naive reading:** 11.0 → 9.1, a 17% cut.\n- **Difference-in-differences:** (9.1 − 11.0) − (10.8 − 11.0) = −1.7 minutes, about −15.5% of the baseline.\n  The comparison teams also got 0.2 minutes faster without the tool, and that part is not the system's doing.\n- **It clears the 15% bar narrowly,** and the weekly trend held steady across weeks 4–6, so it is not a\n  fading novelty spike. Go decision.\n- **Rollout:** to 25%, then 100% over six weeks, with rollback if the audit pass rate pooled over two\n  consecutive weeks (400 tickets) drops below 88%.\n- **Why the rollback line sits below the 90% exit bar:** with a true rate of 91% and ±4-point weekly noise, a\n  90% trigger on a single week's sample would fire spuriously roughly a third of the time. 88% on 400 pooled\n  tickets is a false alarm only a few percent of the time yet still catches a real drop to the mid-80s.\n- **Hypercare** for the first four weeks after full rollout.\n- **Outcome:** because the gap list ran in parallel, production started two weeks after the decision instead\n  of months.","patterns":["**Shadow mode before assist** — when accuracy on live traffic is the open question. How: run the model on every live input, log and score its output, show nothing to users. Monitor: agreement with what humans actually did, per input category; it cannot tell you adoption.","**Pre-registered readout doc** — at week 0. How: one page with baseline numbers, comparison group, success, guardrail and exit criteria, stop triggers and the go/no-go meeting date, signed off by the decision maker. Monitor: whether any criterion changed after results started arriving.","**Percentage rollout behind a flag** — after go. How: gate access with a feature flag keyed on team or user id, step 25% then 100%, and wire the rollback trigger to a pooled audit window, not a single week. Monitor: guardrail metrics per cohort and the trigger's pooled sample size.","**Weekly random audit** — through pilot and hypercare. How: sample a fixed number of AI-assisted items (for example 200) at random each week for human quality review. Monitor: pass rate with its interval, and the weekly trend for novelty decay.","**Production-gap tracker with owners** — from pilot day one. How: each gap item is a ticket with an owner outside the pilot team, an estimate and a due date before the go/no-go. Monitor: items still open at the decision meeting, the leading indicator of purgatory."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    P[\"PoC passed\"] --> B[\"Baseline: pilot and comparison groups\"]\n    B --> C[\"Agree success, guardrail, exit, stop\"]\n    C --> PL[\"Pilot: assist mode, real users\"]\n    C --> G[\"Production-gap list, owners, in parallel\"]\n    PL -->|\"stop trigger\"| H[\"Halt pilot\"]\n    PL --> D{\"Go or no-go vs exit criteria\"}\n    G --> D\n    D -->|\"no-go\"| N[\"Pivot or stop\"]\n    D -->|\"go\"| R[\"Staged rollout with rollback triggers\"]\n    R --> HC[\"Hypercare\"]\n    HC --> O[\"Owned production service\"]","caption":"Two things to notice: the baseline and criteria are fixed before the pilot, and the production-gap work\nruns alongside it, so a go decision leads straight into staged rollout."},"interview":[{"q":"What is pilot purgatory, and how does a pilot-to-production plan prevent it?","a":"- **Definition:** a pilot that succeeds and then stalls because nobody planned the path, budget or owner.\n- **Prevention:** the production-gap list (SSO, security and privacy review, monitoring, on-call, support,\n  cost centre, training) runs in parallel with named owners, so a go decision is acted on in weeks."},{"q":"A pilot shows handle time fell from 11.0 to 9.1 minutes. Is that the system's effect?","a":"- **Not necessarily.** A plain before/after credits the system with seasonality, policy changes or the\n  attention of being piloted.\n- **Use difference-in-differences:** subtract the comparison group's change. If they went from 11.0 to 10.8,\n  the effect is −1.7 minutes, about 15.5% rather than 17%."},{"q":"How would you move an AI system from suggesting actions to taking them?","a":"- **Step up autonomy one level at a time:** shadow mode, assist, act with review, then act alone only on the\n  subset where evidence supports it.\n- **Each step removes a human safety net,** so require fresh evidence, such as reviewer override rates and\n  error escapes, before the next one; accuracy alone hides errors a reviewer used to catch."},{"q":"Why might you set a rollback trigger below the pilot's exit bar?","a":"- **Noise.** With a 91% true pass rate and ±4-point weekly noise on 200 tickets, a 90% single-week trigger\n  fires spuriously about a third of the time.\n- **Pool and lower:** 88% over two weeks (400 tickets) is a false alarm only a few percent of the time and\n  still catches a real drop to the mid-80s."},{"q":"When would you skip the comparison group or compress the plan?","a":"- **Skip the comparison group** when the expected effect is so large or obvious that before/after is\n  convincing, since holding users back delays their benefit.\n- **Compress the plan** for small internal tools with easy rollback to a short pilot and a checklist; keep\n  the full staged approach for regulated data or customer-facing outcomes."}],"tradeoffs":["**Comparison group vs delayed benefit.** It gives a credible causal estimate, but delays benefits for those users; it flips when the expected effect is so large or obvious that a before/after comparison is convincing enough.","**Volunteer cohorts vs representative results.** They make pilots easier to run and generate goodwill, but overstate adoption and impact; include typical users when the decision depends on organization-wide adoption.","**Longer pilots vs patience.** They smooth out weekly noise and seasonality, but delay value and erode stakeholder patience; set duration from how long the metric needs to stabilize, not by habit.","**Parallel readiness work vs wasted effort.** It shortens time to launch, but spends effort on a system that might get a no-go; worth it when the PoC evidence was strong."],"pitfalls":["**No baseline measured before the pilot.** The result is a before/after story nobody can verify; the symptom is a debate at go/no-go about what the number \"would have been\".","**A champion-only cohort.** Results do not transfer; the symptom is strong pilot metrics followed by low adoption and complaints when typical users get access.","**A sandbox exempt from security and identity requirements,** with no plan to meet them; the symptom is a go decision followed by months waiting on reviews.","**No guardrail metrics.** The headline metric improves while cost moves elsewhere; the symptom is faster handle times alongside rising escalations or complaints.","**No named post-launch owner or budget.** The builders keep running it informally; the symptom is the pilot engineer still answering production questions six months later.","**Reading the result while novelty is high.** The symptom is a metric that improves sharply in week one and then decays week over week toward the baseline.","**Jumping from assist to autonomous action** because pilot accuracy looked good; the symptom is errors reaching customers that a reviewer used to catch silently, visible as rising complaints with no change in measured model accuracy."],"when":"- **Write the plan** when a PoC has passed and before the pilot starts, because baseline measurement and\n  criteria must be fixed before anyone sees results.\n- **Use the full staged approach** when the system touches many users, regulated data or customer-facing\n  outcomes.\n- **Compress it** for small internal tools with easy rollback: a short pilot and a checklist.\n- **Measure:** whether the go/no-go meeting is a short reading of agreed numbers and whether production\n  follows within weeks.\n- **Interviews:** a strong answer names baseline, cohort, guardrails, gap list, owner and rollback.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments the pilot so success and guardrail metrics are captured automatically, and turns production-gap items like monitoring and evaluation gates into tracked work.","architect":"Owns the stage gates and the production-gap list, deciding which pilot shortcuts are acceptable and which must be resolved before wider rollout.","fde":"Negotiates exit criteria and the comparison group with the customer, chases their security and identity reviews in parallel, and plans the handoff to the customer's own team."},"check":{"q":"A pilot of an AI coding assistant with 25 volunteer developers reports \"developers say they are\n40% more productive\". Leadership wants to roll it out to 2,000 developers next month. What do you ask\nbefore agreeing?","a":"First, what was measured: self-reported productivity from volunteers is weak evidence, inflated by\nenthusiasm and novelty. Ask for a baseline and comparison group on observable metrics such as cycle time,\nreview rework and defect rates, plus guardrails like security findings in generated code. Then the\nproduction gap: licensing or inference cost at 2,000 seats, identity and policy controls, data-handling\nreview for code sent to the provider, support and training for non-volunteers. Propose a staged rollout\nto a few hundred typical developers with agreed exit criteria and rollback, rather than a jump from 25 to 2,000."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must the baseline be measured before the pilot begins?","options":["Because the system cannot log metrics once it is live","Because without it you cannot separate the system's effect from seasonality or the attention of being piloted","Because finance requires baselines for any budget approval","Because the model's accuracy changes after deployment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The pilot's value is a comparison. Measured afterwards, the baseline is contaminated or reconstructed from memory, and the go/no-go turns into argument rather than evidence."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of guardrail metrics in a pilot plan?","options":["To replace the success metric when it is hard to measure","To monitor GPU utilization during the pilot","To catch cases where the headline metric improves by pushing cost or harm elsewhere","To decide which users join the pilot cohort"],"answer":2,"explanation":"AI systems can speed one step while increasing escalations, errors or cost downstream. Guardrails make those regressions part of the decision, so a \"win\" is not a cost shifted elsewhere."},{"q":"Which practice most directly prevents \"pilot purgatory\"?","options":["Extending the pilot until every user has tried the system","Choosing only enthusiastic volunteers for the cohort","Building and staffing the production-gap list in parallel with the pilot, with named owners","Postponing security review until after the go decision"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Purgatory happens when success arrives with no path, owner or budget for production. Working the gap list during the pilot means a go decision can be acted on quickly; postponing reviews causes the stall."}]},{"key":"scoping/productionization","sources":[],"why":"- **A prototype makes every time-saving assumption.** One user, clean inputs, a provider that always\n  responds, a prompt edited by hand, no adversary, unlimited budget, the builder watching the output. Each\n  is fine for a PoC and false in production.\n- **Production breaks each assumption.** Thousands of users make the long tail of strange inputs, which the\n  PoC sample barely touched, a daily occurrence. Concurrency hits provider rate limits. Providers have\n  partial outages, return malformed JSON, or slow down at peak.\n- **Things change underneath you.** The model behind an alias changes, or is deprecated on a schedule you do\n  not control, and quality shifts silently. Users paste documents with instructions that try to hijack the\n  system. Someone asks why it answered that way last Tuesday, and there is no record. The monthly bill\n  arrives at several times the estimate because a retry loop or a few heavy users consumed it.\n- **\"Just deploy the prototype\" fails for LLMs.** Fixing problems as they appear works for deterministic code\n  with good tests; here problems show up as undetected quality degradation, cost overruns or a security\n  incident, all discovered by users.\n- **Waiting for perfect never ships.** Productionization is the disciplined middle: enumerate failure\n  sources, harden against the ones that matter most for this use case, and make the rest observable.","how":"Organize the work by what can go wrong, because that makes gaps visible and lets you prioritize by risk.\n\n### 1. Reliability of the model call\n- **Explicit timeouts,** because a hung provider call otherwise holds a worker indefinitely.\n- **Retry only transient errors** (timeouts, rate limits, server errors) with exponential backoff and jitter,\n  and cap total attempts, because unbounded retries multiply cost and load during an outage.\n- **Validate structured output against a schema** and handle failure. A schema failure is a middle case:\n  outputs are sampled, so one bounded re-try (or a repair prompt) often succeeds, but count it separately so\n  a prompt regression is not hidden by retries.\n- **Define a fallback:** a secondary model, a cached answer, or a graceful \"cannot answer, routing to a\n  person\".\n\nTwo retry details decide whether this helps or hurts during an incident:\n\n| Detail | Problem | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Retries multiply across layers** | 3 attempts in your code × 3 in the gateway × SDK retries by default (many do) = up to 27 provider calls per user request, just when the provider is struggling | one layer owns retries, others off or down; add a circuit breaker so when most recent calls fail, new requests go straight to the fallback instead of each discovering the outage |\n| **Retries must fit the deadline** | worst case = attempts × timeout + total backoff | keep it below what the user or upstream service will wait |\n\n- **Only retry what is safe to repeat.** Re-sending a classification is harmless; re-running a tool call that\n  sends an email or files a refund duplicates it unless the action carries an idempotency key.\n\n### 2. Quality as a release gate\n- **Regression suite:** the PoC eval set runs on every change to prompts, models, retrieval or tools.\n- **Version prompts and pin model versions** where the provider allows, because an unpinned alias can change\n  behavior with no deployment on your side.\n- **Roll out changes** through canary or shadow traffic.\n\n### 3. Observability\n- **Trace each request end to end:** inputs, retrieved context, prompt version, model, outputs, latency,\n  token counts and cost, tied to a request ID. Without traces, debugging a bad answer means guessing.\n- **Monitor** latency percentiles, error rates, cost per request, and online quality signals such as user\n  feedback or sampled human review.\n\n### 4. Security and data handling\n- **Authorize with the user's own permissions** on retrieved data and tool calls, because the model will\n  happily summarize documents the user should not see.\n- **Treat retrieved and user-supplied text as untrusted** input that may contain prompt injection.\n- **Decide logging policy:** what gets logged, how PII is redacted, and how long data is kept.\n\n### 5. Cost and capacity\n- **Set limits:** per-user and per-tenant quotas, maximum input and output tokens, and budget alerts.\n- **Estimate cost at expected volume,** because PoC cost per call times production volume is often the first\n  surprise:\n\n```\nmonthly cost ≈ requests/month × (tokens_in × price_in + tokens_out × price_out) × (1 + retry rate)\nin-flight requests ≈ arrival rate × mean latency          (Little's law)\ntokens/minute ≈ requests/minute × (tokens_in + tokens_out)\n```\n\n- **Check the last two lines against provider rate limits,** which are usually set per minute in both\n  requests and tokens.\n- **Worked numbers (illustrative):** 20 requests/second with 6 seconds of latency is about 120 calls in\n  flight; at 3,500 tokens each that is over 4 million tokens per minute. A PoC that never ran more than one\n  call at a time tells you nothing about whether that fits your quota tier.\n- **Cap output length.** Long outputs dominate latency (generated token by token) and usually cost more per\n  token than input, so this is often the strongest cost and latency lever.\n\n### 6. Operations and ownership\n- **Define SLOs,** write runbooks for known failure modes (provider outage, quality regression, cost spike),\n  set up on-call, and name the owning team.\n- **Plan for model deprecation,** because the model you shipped on has a finite lifetime.","example":"### The same call, prototype vs productionized\nThe provider call is abstracted; the hardening around it is the point.\n\n```python\nimport json, logging, random, time, uuid\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ValidationError\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"triage\")\nPROMPT_VERSION = \"triage-v7\"      # versioned, reviewed, covered by the regression suite\nMODEL = \"provider-model-2025-xx\"  # pinned snapshot, not a floating alias (placeholder name)\n\nclass Triage(BaseModel):\n    category: str\n    priority: int\n\nclass TransientError(Exception): ...   # map provider timeouts / 429 / 5xx onto this\n\ndef call_llm(model, prompt_version, text, timeout_s=None, with_usage=False):\n    raise NotImplementedError(\"wrap your provider SDK here\")\n\ndef prototype(ticket: str) -> dict:\n    return json.loads(call_llm(MODEL, PROMPT_VERSION, ticket))   # happy path only\n\ndef production(ticket: str, user_id: str, max_attempts: int = 3) -> Triage | None:\n    req_id = str(uuid.uuid4())\n    ticket = ticket[:20_000]                                     # bound input size and cost\n    for attempt in range(1, max_attempts + 1):\n        t0 = time.monotonic()\n        try:\n            raw, usage = call_llm(MODEL, PROMPT_VERSION, ticket, timeout_s=15, with_usage=True)\n            result = Triage.model_validate_json(raw)             # schema check, not blind json.loads\n            log.info(\"ok\", extra={\"req\": req_id, \"user\": user_id, \"prompt\": PROMPT_VERSION,\n                                  \"attempt\": attempt, \"ms\": int((time.monotonic() - t0) * 1000),\n                                  \"tokens_in\": usage[\"in\"], \"tokens_out\": usage[\"out\"]})\n            return result\n        except TransientError:\n            log.warning(\"transient\", extra={\"req\": req_id, \"attempt\": attempt})\n            if attempt < max_attempts:                            # no pointless sleep before giving up\n                time.sleep(min(8, 2 ** attempt) * random.uniform(0.5, 1.0))  # backoff with jitter\n        except ValidationError:                                   # re-sample once more, counted separately\n            log.warning(\"bad_schema\", extra={\"req\": req_id, \"attempt\": attempt})\n    log.error(\"fallback_to_human\", extra={\"req\": req_id})\n    return None                                                   # caller routes to manual queue\n```\n\n- **Each added line answers a production question** the prototype ignored: what if it hangs, rate-limits,\n  returns broken output, or costs too much, and how will we debug it next week?\n- **Worst case it implies:** three 15-second timeouts plus up to 2 and 4 seconds of backoff is about 51\n  seconds before the human fallback.\n- **Fit to the path:** acceptable for a background triage queue, far too long for a chat reply, where you\n  would cut attempts, shorten the timeout, or pass a single overall deadline through the loop.\n- **Deliberately omitted:** a circuit breaker and cost quotas belong in a shared gateway rather than in every\n  call site.\n\n### Capacity, cost and retry-budget calculator\nRun this before launch with your own volumes and your provider's current prices and quota tier.\n\n```python\ndef capacity(rps, latency_s, tokens_in, tokens_out, price_in_per_m, price_out_per_m,\n             retry_rate=0.0, days=30):\n    in_flight = rps * latency_s                                   # Little's law\n    tpm = rps * 60 * (tokens_in + tokens_out)                     # check against the quota tier\n    per_req = (tokens_in * price_in_per_m + tokens_out * price_out_per_m) / 1e6\n    monthly = rps * 86_400 * days * per_req * (1 + retry_rate)\n    return in_flight, tpm, monthly\n\ndef worst_case_latency(attempts, timeout_s, backoffs_s):\n    return attempts * timeout_s + sum(backoffs_s)\n\ndef retry_amplification(attempts_per_layer):\n    calls = 1\n    for a in attempts_per_layer:\n        calls *= a\n    return calls\n\n# Illustrative prices in dollars per million tokens, not any provider's list price.\ninflight, tpm, monthly = capacity(rps=20, latency_s=6, tokens_in=3_000, tokens_out=500,\n                                  price_in_per_m=3, price_out_per_m=15, retry_rate=0.05)\nprint(f\"in flight {inflight:.0f}, tokens/min {tpm / 1e6:.1f}M, monthly ${monthly:,.0f}\")\n# in flight 120, tokens/min 4.2M, monthly $898,128  (if 20 rps were sustained around the clock)\nprint(\"worst case before fallback:\", worst_case_latency(3, 15, [2, 4]), \"s\")   # 51 s\nprint(\"app x gateway x SDK retries:\", retry_amplification([3, 3, 3]), \"calls\")  # 27\n```\n\n| Lever | Effect on the numbers above |\n|---|---|\n| Halve output tokens (500 → 250) | cuts per-request cost by about 23% here ($0.0165 to $0.01275), since output is priced 5× input in this example, and shortens latency |\n| One retry layer instead of three | worst-case calls per request from 27 to 3 |\n| Shorter latency | fewer calls in flight for the same traffic, by Little's law |","patterns":["**One retry owner, everything else off** — always. How: retry in the gateway or the app, not both, and set the SDK's own retry count to zero or one (for example the OpenAI and Anthropic Python clients accept `max_retries`). Monitor: provider calls per user request, which should sit near 1 in steady state; a multiple during incidents means retries are stacking.","**Shared LLM gateway** — once more than one service calls models. How: one internal service owns timeouts, circuit breaking, per-tenant quotas, token caps, model pinning and cost attribution, so call sites stay thin. Monitor: breaker state, quota rejections, and cost per tenant per day.","**Eval regression gate in CI** — on every prompt, model, retrieval or tool change. How: a fast suite (a few hundred cases) blocks the merge if the score drops past a set margin; the full suite runs nightly. Monitor: gate run time and how often it is bypassed.","**Canary on model or prompt changes** — for any change that alters outputs. How: route a small share of traffic to the new version, compare error rate, latency, cost and sampled quality against the old one, then widen. Monitor: the per-version deltas, with automatic rollback on a guardrail breach.","**Idempotency keys on side-effecting tools** — for email, ticket or refund actions. How: derive a key from the request ID and action, and have the tool reject a repeat key. Monitor: duplicate-key rejections, which should line up with timeout spikes.","**Deprecation calendar** — from launch day. How: record the pinned model's announced retirement date and schedule a migration eval well before it. Monitor: days remaining on every pinned model in production."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"User request\"] --> AZ[\"Authorize with user permissions\"]\n    AZ --> Q[\"Quota and input size limits\"]\n    Q --> CB{\"Circuit breaker open?\"}\n    CB -->|\"yes\"| FB[\"Fallback: human queue or cached\"]\n    CB -->|\"no\"| M[\"Model call, pinned version, timeout\"]\n    M -->|\"transient error, attempts left\"| BO[\"Backoff with jitter\"]\n    BO --> M\n    M -->|\"attempts exhausted\"| FB\n    M --> V{\"Output matches schema?\"}\n    V -->|\"no, bounded re-try\"| M\n    V -->|\"yes\"| R[\"Response\"]\n    R --> T[(\"Trace: prompt, model, tokens, cost\")]\n    FB --> T","caption":"One request through the hardened path: authorization and limits first, a circuit breaker before the\nprovider, bounded retries, schema validation, and a trace on every exit including the human fallback."},"interview":[{"q":"What does productionizing an LLM feature involve beyond deploying the model call?","a":"- **Reliability:** timeouts, transient-only bounded retries, schema validation and a fallback.\n- **Quality and visibility:** a regression gate, pinned model versions, canary rollout and end-to-end traces\n  with tokens and cost.\n- **Safety and operations:** user-scoped authorization, injection-aware handling, quotas, SLOs, runbooks,\n  on-call, an owner and a deprecation plan."},{"q":"During a provider incident your call volume to the provider tripled while user traffic was flat. Why?","a":"- **Stacked retries:** app, gateway and SDK each retrying multiplies calls, up to 27 per request with three\n  attempts at three layers.\n- **Fix:** one layer owns retries, others set low or off, plus a circuit breaker that sends new requests to\n  the fallback once most recent calls fail, so the provider can recover."},{"q":"How would you set the retry and timeout budget for a chat reply versus a background job?","a":"- **Compute worst case:** attempts × timeout + total backoff; three 15-second timeouts plus 2 and 4 seconds\n  of backoff is about 51 seconds.\n- **Background triage** can absorb that; a chat reply cannot, so cut attempts, shorten the timeout, or pass\n  one overall deadline through the loop."},{"q":"Estimate whether a launch at 20 requests per second fits your provider quota.","a":"- **Little's law:** 20 per second × 6 seconds latency is about 120 calls in flight.\n- **Tokens per minute:** 1,200 requests per minute × 3,500 tokens is about 4.2 million, which you compare with\n  the tier's per-minute request and token limits; capping output length is the strongest lever if it does not\n  fit."},{"q":"When would you pin a model version, and when would a floating alias be acceptable?","a":"- **Pin** when behavior must be reproducible and gated by a regression suite, because an alias can change\n  quality with no deploy on your side.\n- **Accept an alias** only for low-stakes features with a fast eval running on a schedule, and plan migrations\n  either way, because pinned snapshots carry deprecation dates."}],"tradeoffs":["**Retries vs latency and load.** They improve success rates during transient faults, but add latency and cost and can amplify an outage; this flips toward fewer retries when the call sits on a latency-critical user path.","**Pinned versions vs migration work.** Pinning gives reproducible behavior but forces planned migrations before deprecation dates; floating aliases avoid that work at the price of unannounced behavior change.","**Full logging vs sensitive data.** Logging full prompts and outputs makes debugging and audits far easier, but creates a store of sensitive data; redact or sample when inputs contain regulated or personal data.","**Human fallback vs staffing.** A fallback to a human or a simpler path keeps service available, but needs staffing and a queue; for low-stakes features, returning a clear error may be the cheaper fallback.","**Strict gates vs bypass.** Strict regression gates prevent quality backsliding, but a slow or flaky eval suite will be bypassed; keep the gating suite fast and run the expensive one nightly."],"pitfalls":["**Deploying the prototype as-is** because it worked in the pilot; the symptom is the first provider outage taking the feature down with hung requests and no fallback.","**Retrying every error,** including invalid requests and content refusals; the symptom is a cost spike and rate-limit errors during incidents, as retries pile onto an overloaded provider.","**An unpinned model alias with no regression suite;** the symptom is a quality drop users report weeks before anyone on the team notices.","**Retrieving with a service account** instead of the end user's permissions; the symptom is a user receiving content from documents they cannot open directly.","**No per-request traces or cost attribution;** the symptom is a large bill or a bad answer that nobody can explain or reproduce.","**Stacked retries in application code, gateway and SDK;** the symptom is provider call volume several times the request volume during an incident, with rate-limit errors that persist after the provider recovers.","**Retrying side-effecting tool calls without idempotency keys;** the symptom is duplicate emails, tickets or refunds that line up with timeout spikes in the traces."],"when":"- **Start** when a pilot shows the system is worth running, and scope it by risk.\n- **Full treatment:** a customer-facing, regulated or tool-using system needs every section above.\n- **Light treatment:** an internal summarizer with easy fallback may need only timeouts, quotas, basic\n  tracing and a regression set.\n- **Silent failures first** (quality regression, data exposure, cost), because loud failures get fixed\n  anyway.\n- **Measure:** readiness with a checklist reviewed at a deployment readiness review; after launch track error\n  rate, latency percentiles, cost per request and sampled quality.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Adds timeouts, scoped retries, schema validation, fallbacks, tracing and cost logging around every model call, and wires the eval set into CI as a regression gate.","architect":"Decides the platform pieces shared across systems, such as gateways, prompt registry, tracing and quotas, so each team does not productionize the same concerns separately.","fde":"Maps production requirements onto the customer's environment: their identity provider, logging and retention rules, network constraints and the team that will own operations."},"check":{"q":"A pilot of a document Q&A assistant was a success. The prototype calls a model alias directly,\nretrieves with an admin service account, logs nothing, and has no tests. You have four weeks before\ncompany-wide launch. What do you do first, and why that order?","a":"Order by severity and silence of failure. First, fix authorization: retrieval must run with the\nend user's permissions, because a data-exposure incident is the most damaging and hardest to undo. Second,\nturn the pilot's eval examples into a regression suite and pin the model version, so quality changes\nbecome visible. Third, add request tracing, with redaction, plus cost logging. Fourth, timeouts, scoped\nretries, schema validation and a fallback. Then quotas, runbooks, on-call and an owner. Roll out by canary\nso remaining gaps surface on a small population first."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should retries be limited to transient errors such as timeouts and rate limits?","options":["Because providers charge double for retried requests","Because retrying permanent errors cannot succeed and only adds cost, latency and load during incidents","Because retries break schema validation","Because transient errors are always caused by the client"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A malformed request, an auth error or an oversized input will fail the same way again, and a policy refusal usually does. Retrying it wastes tokens and, during an outage, piles extra load onto an already struggling provider."},{"q":"What is the main risk of calling a floating model alias in production without a regression suite?","options":["The alias will stop resolving after a fixed number of requests","Latency always doubles when the alias updates","The underlying model can change without a deploy on your side, shifting quality with nothing to detect it","Aliases cannot return structured output"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Aliases may point to new snapshots over time. Without pinned versions or a regression suite, behavior changes silently, and the first signal is user complaints."},{"q":"Which productionization item should usually come first for a system that retrieves internal documents?","options":["Adding a caching layer to cut latency","Switching to a cheaper model to reduce cost","Enforcing the end user's permissions on retrieval","Building a dashboard of daily active users"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Data exposure through retrieval is severe, often silent and hard to reverse. Latency, cost and usage analytics matter, but they do not leak data to the wrong people."}]}]});
